Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "steerability_emergence_pretraining",
  "Title": "When Does a Design Lever Get Forged? Tracing the Pretraining Emergence of Causal Feature Steerability in Protein Language Models",
  "Short Hypothesis": "In protein language models, the causal STEERABILITY of an interpretable feature (its ability to control the corresponding property in generation) emerges LATER in pretraining than its DECODABILITY (linear readout of the concept), and the emergence time of both is gated by the training-data frequency of the underlying biological concept: rare-but-valid concepts become decodable long before\u2014or without ever\u2014becoming steerable. This decodability-steerability lag is a training-time phenomenon that explains the widely-reported 'decodable-but-inert' anomaly and predicts which design levers a given checkpoint can and cannot provide.",
  "Related Work": "Two literatures converge but have never met. (1) Feature-emergence-during-pretraining work is entirely in TEXT LLMs: 'Evolution of Concepts in Language Model Pre-Training' (2025) and 'Crosscoding Through Time' (2025) use crosscoders to track feature formation; 'The Birth of Knowledge' (2025) maps categorical feature emergence across time/space/scale; and most relevantly 'How Does Controllability Emerge in LMs During Pretraining' (2025) shows linear STEERABILITY of text concepts emerges at intermediate stages and varies by concept, linked to linear separability. None studies scientific generative models, none ties emergence to training-DATA-FREQUENCY of the concept, and none contrasts decodability vs causal steerability trajectories for GENERATED scientific objects. (2) Protein-SAE work (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; 'From Mechanistic Interpretability to Mechanistic Biology' 2025; automated neuron labelling 2025; motif-gated causal patching 2025) is entirely inference-time on a FINAL checkpoint, and reports the 'decodable-but-inert' anomaly as a static puzzle. Data-scaling/bias work (Scaling and Data Saturation in pLMs 2025; pLMs biased by unequal sequence sampling 2024; Compute-Optimal pLMs 2024) shows data frequency shapes likelihoods but never connects it to feature steerability. We are the first to (i) trace decodability AND causal steerability trajectories across pretraining in a scientific generative model, (ii) test data-frequency as the gating variable via curated rare/common concepts and yearly-snapshot corpora, and (iii) reframe the decodable-but-inert anomaly as a training-time developmental lag with a predictive rule for checkpoint-conditional controllability. This uses Formation Tracing\u2014a genuinely different mechanism strategy from prior inference-time steering studies.",
  "Abstract": "Interpretability-guided design assumes that if a concept is decodable from a protein language model it can be used as a design lever\u2014yet multiple studies report 'decodable-but-inert' features that read out a concept perfectly but fail to steer generation. We propose that this anomaly is a developmental one: during pretraining, the causal STEERABILITY of a feature emerges strictly later than its DECODABILITY, and both are gated by how frequently the underlying biological concept appears in the training data. We test this by pretraining small ESM-style masked protein LMs (3.5M\u201335M params, tractable for an academic lab) and saving frequent checkpoints, complemented by publicly available yearly UniRef-snapshot pLMs. At each checkpoint we train a sparse autoencoder and, for a curated bank of concepts spanning a wide data-frequency range (common folds/motifs to rare families and rare property combinations), we measure two trajectories: decodability (probe AUROC of the concept feature) and steerability (causal property change per unit steer in generation, verified by DSSP/ESMFold/HMMER). We hypothesize (i) a consistent decodability-then-steerability ordering with a measurable lag, (ii) that concept training-frequency predicts both onset times, and (iii) that some rare concepts remain decodable-but-inert at convergence\u2014explaining the static anomaly. We further test whether an emergent 'steerability onset' at one scale predicts onset at another, and whether upsampling a rare concept in a controlled retraining accelerates its steerability onset. Results would recast steerability as a trainable, data-dependent developmental property and yield a predictive, frequency-based rule for which design levers a checkpoint can provide.",
  "Experiments": "1) MODELS & CHECKPOINTS: Pretrain small masked protein LMs (ESM-style, 3.5M/8M/35M params) on a fixed UniRef50 subset, saving ~15-20 logarithmically-spaced checkpoints (feasible per Compute-Optimal pLM work; total compute within a single-GPU-week budget for the 3.5M/8M runs). Additionally use publicly available yearly-snapshot pLMs (from the data-saturation study) as an independent data-frequency axis where available. Verify each checkpoint's general quality (pseudo-perplexity, contact-prediction proxy).\n2) CONCEPT BANK WITH FREQUENCY LABELS: Curate ~30-50 concepts with HARD automated evaluators and KNOWN training-data frequency (counted directly in the training corpus): common secondary-structure targets (alpha-helix/beta-sheet content via DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY hydrophobicity, molecular weight, common motifs (zinc finger, catalytic triad via HMMER/regex), and deliberately RARE concepts (rare Pfam families, uncommon motifs, and rare property combinations like high-beta+high-hydrophilicity). Record each concept's occurrence frequency in the pretraining set.\n3) PER-CHECKPOINT DECODABILITY: At each checkpoint, train a TopK SAE on the residual stream (layer via intrinsic-dimension heuristic) and identify the feature(s) best encoding each concept; measure DECODABILITY = probe/feature AUROC on held-out natural sequences. Also track a lightweight raw-activation linear probe as an SAE-independent decodability signal.\n4) PER-CHECKPOINT STEERABILITY (core): For each concept feature, clamp/steer during masked-infilling generation and measure STEERABILITY = property change per unit steer at low strength within the validity budget (validity gated by pseudo-perplexity and ESMFold pLDDT), plus max reliable property gain. Use random-direction and unrelated-feature controls. This yields, per concept, a decodability trajectory and a steerability trajectory across pretraining.\n5) ORDERING & LAG TEST (hypothesis i): Define onset time as the checkpoint at which each trajectory first crosses a fixed threshold. Test whether decodability-onset precedes steerability-onset across concepts (paired sign test / Wilcoxon), and quantify the lag distribution. Confirm the static decodable-but-inert anomaly appears as concepts whose steerability never crosses threshold by convergence.\n6) FREQUENCY-GATING TEST (hypothesis ii): Regress decodability-onset and steerability-onset (and the lag) on log training-frequency of each concept, across concepts and model sizes (mixed-effects model). Hypothesis: higher frequency -> earlier onset; steerability-onset is more frequency-sensitive than decodability-onset.\n7) CAUSAL DATA-INTERVENTION (hypothesis ii, causal): Retrain a small pLM with a chosen RARE concept UPSAMPLED (e.g., 10x) versus a matched control run, holding total tokens fixed. Test whether upsampling accelerates that concept's steerability-onset (and reduces its decodability-steerability lag) relative to control, while non-upsampled concepts are unchanged. This is a controlled data-attribution test of the gating claim.\n8) CROSS-SCALE PREDICTION: Test whether the ORDER of concept steerability-onsets is preserved across the 3.5M/8M/35M models, and whether onset at a small cheap model predicts onset at a larger one (Spearman), giving a practical proxy.\nEVALUATION METRICS: decodability-onset vs steerability-onset ordering (paired test effect size), lag distribution, fraction of decodable-but-inert-at-convergence concepts, frequency-onset regression coefficients / R^2, upsampling-induced onset shift (causal effect size), cross-scale onset Spearman, validity retention (pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity), and SAE reconstruction/interpretability sanity checks.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Small self-trained pLMs may underperform large models, so absolute steerability could be weak; we mitigate by focusing on RELATIVE trajectories/orderings and by cross-checking with public yearly-snapshot pLMs. 2) SAE quality varies across checkpoints and could confound emergence timing; we control with a fixed SAE architecture/hyperparameters per checkpoint, report reconstruction fidelity, and cross-validate decodability with an SAE-independent raw-activation probe. 3) In-silico evaluators (ESMFold, DSSP, HMMER) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal, training-time controllability, not experimental viability. 4) Frequency-onset correlations could reflect confounds (concept complexity, sequence conservation) rather than raw frequency; the controlled upsampling retraining (experiment 7) is designed to isolate frequency causally. 5) Checkpoint spacing may miss sharp emergence transitions; we use log-spaced checkpoints and can densify around detected transitions. 6) If decodability and steerability emerge simultaneously (no lag), that is still an informative, publishable result refuting the developmental-lag account and constraining explanations of the decodable-but-inert anomaly. 7) Retraining runs add compute cost but are limited to the smallest models to stay within an academic budget."
}}
