Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "hazard_entanglement_asymmetry",
"Title": "Easy to Arm, Hard to Disarm: An Entanglement Asymmetry Between Hazardous and Benign Design Features in Scientific Generative Models",
"Short Hypothesis": "In molecular and protein generative models, interpretable features exhibit a dual-use entanglement asymmetry: hazardous structural properties (toxicophores, known toxin/venom motifs, reactive groups) can be AMPLIFIED cheaply by steering ostensibly-benign, property-labeled features, yet cannot be SUPPRESSED without disproportionate collateral loss of valid, benign design capability. We hypothesize this asymmetry mirrors the capability/safety entanglement documented in text LLMs but is sharper and objectively measurable here because hazard is a verifiable structural fact, and that the degree of asymmetry is predicted by how much a hazard shares its feature subspace with benign design features.",
"Related Work": "SAE-based safety steering is well developed in TEXT LLMs: refusal steering (Steering Language Model Refusal with SAEs, 2024) and detoxification (Breaking Bad Tokens, 2025; GSAE, 2025; Safe-SAIL, 2025) all report that suppressing an unsafe concept degrades general capability, revealing deep entanglement between safety-relevant and general features. Separately, scientific-model interpretability (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; pathology-FM SAEs 2024; chemistry-LM SAEs 2025) discovers and steers interpretable features toward single BENIGN target properties, treating hazard only implicitly. The 'Guiding Generative Models for Protein Design' review (2025) notes models undersample rare regions but does not study hazard controllability. No prior work (a) transfers the safety/capability-entanglement phenomenon into SCIENTIFIC generative models where hazard is a structurally-verifiable fact (RDKit SMARTS toxicophores, HMMER toxin motifs) rather than fuzzy text behavior, (b) tests a DIRECTIONAL ASYMMETRY between cheap hazard amplification and costly hazard suppression, or (c) ties that asymmetry to feature-subspace overlap between hazard and benign design. This distinguishes us from my prior 'intervention-leakage' proposal (which asks whether a single-feature suppression leaks through alternative decoding routes) by instead measuring the amplify-vs-suppress cost asymmetry and its collateral impact on legitimate design capability. It converts a known text-LLM tension into an auditable, high-stakes dual-use risk metric for scientific design.",
"Abstract": "Interpretability-guided design promises controllable scientific generation, but control is dual-use: the same interpretable knobs that add a desired property may also arm a model to produce hazardous objects, and safety measures that suppress hazard may cripple legitimate design. Text-LLM studies report that safety-relevant features are entangled with general capability, so suppressing an unsafe concept degrades benign performance. We ask whether this tension transfers, and sharpens, in scientific generative models where hazard is an objective structural fact. We define hazardous properties with hard, non-model evaluators (toxicophore/PAINS/reactive-group SMARTS in molecules; known toxin/venom/cytolytic motifs in proteins) and benign design properties with cheap oracles (logP, QED, secondary-structure content, foldability). Training sparse autoencoders on a SMILES chemical language model and ESM-2, we test a central phenomenon: an entanglement asymmetry in which hazard is cheaply AMPLIFIED by steering ostensibly-benign, property-labeled features (a small steering budget, high validity), but cannot be SUPPRESSED below baseline without a disproportionate drop in valid benign-design capability. We quantify an Asymmetry Index = (cost to suppress) / (cost to amplify) and a Collateral Ratio = benign-design loss per unit hazard removed, comparing single-feature, subspace, and difference-of-means interventions and controlling for the known pitfall that SAE-reconstructed steering vectors are unreliable. We then test whether the asymmetry is predicted by the feature-subspace overlap between each hazard and benign design directions. Results would establish a measurable, mechanistically-grounded dual-use risk profile for scientific generative models and a practical audit for whether hazard can be safely disarmed without breaking legitimate design.",
"Experiments": "1) MODELS & HAZARD/BENIGN LABELS: Use a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM (ChEMBL/ZINC-trained checkpoint) and ESM-2 (150M/650M). Train TopK SAEs on the residual stream at a mid layer (chosen by intrinsic-dimension heuristic); verify reconstruction fidelity. HAZARD properties (hard non-model evaluators): molecules - presence of toxicophore/PAINS SMARTS, reactive/electrophilic groups, structural-alert counts (RDKit); proteins - known toxin/venom/cytolytic sequence motifs (HMMER against curated toxin HMMs / regex) and disallowed motif classes. BENIGN design properties (cheap oracles): molecules - logP band, QED, ring/scaffold targets, synthetic accessibility; proteins - alpha-helix/beta-sheet content (DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY, a fold/foldability target. VALIDITY gates: RDKit-validity + SA for molecules; ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins.\n2) FEATURE BANKS: Identify (i) BENIGN property-labeled features via concept-correlation/probe AUROC on held-out natural objects (the ostensibly-safe design knobs), and (ii) HAZARD-linked features/subspaces for each hazard class. Record concept-correlation for all.\n3) AMPLIFY-VS-SUPPRESS COST (core phenomenon): For each hazard class, measure (a) AMPLIFICATION cost = minimal steering budget (norm of intervention) to raise hazard occurrence in FINISHED objects to a fixed high rate, via steering benign property-labeled features (test whether benign knobs alone arm the model) and via hazard features; (b) SUPPRESSION cost = minimal budget to drive baseline hazard occurrence below a fixed low threshold. Track validity throughout. Report Asymmetry Index = suppression cost / amplification cost per hazard class, across single-feature, k-dim subspace (SVD of correlated features), and difference-of-means interventions. To avoid the known pitfall that SAE-reconstructed steering vectors are OOD/sign-limited, use SAE decoder directions and difference-of-means directions directly (not SAE re-encodings of steering vectors), and report both.\n4) COLLATERAL RATIO (does disarming break benign design?): While suppressing each hazard to the target threshold, measure the drop in valid benign-design capability = success rate of hitting a battery of legitimate benign design targets (logP/QED/scaffold; secondary-structure/fold) and overall validity/diversity. Collateral Ratio = benign-design success loss per unit hazard-occurrence removed. Compare against text-LLM-style capability degradation baselines by including a random-direction suppression control matched in norm.\n5) SUBSPACE-OVERLAP PREDICTOR (mechanism): For each hazard class compute its feature-subspace overlap with benign design directions (principal angles / mean cosine between hazard-feature subspace and benign-feature subspace; effective shared rank). Regress Asymmetry Index and Collateral Ratio on this overlap (Spearman/R^2, bootstrap CIs). Hypothesis: higher overlap -> larger asymmetry and higher collateral. Test a mitigation: subspace suppression that projects out ONLY the hazard-specific complement (orthogonalized against benign directions) and check whether it lowers Collateral Ratio at equal hazard removal.\n6) DUAL-USE STRESS TEST: Quantify how easily benign-only knobs arm the model - fraction of benign property-labeled features whose amplification incidentally raises any hazard above baseline (an off-target hazard leakage rate), establishing an auditable dual-use exposure metric per feature.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, and two SAE variants (TopK, Matryoshka); report where the asymmetry is stable vs artifact.\nEVALUATION METRICS: Asymmetry Index (primary), Collateral Ratio, off-target hazard leakage rate of benign knobs, subspace-overlap predictor Spearman/R^2, validity retention (RDKit-validity/SA, ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity), benign-design success/diversity, and the orthogonalized-suppression mitigation effect.",
"Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Hazard and benign properties are defined by in-silico structural evaluators (RDKit SMARTS, HMMER, ESMFold); these are proxies for real-world hazard/function, so claims concern model-internal, structurally-defined dual-use controllability rather than wet-lab harm. We deliberately study only structural-alert-level hazards with unambiguous detectors and avoid synthesizable-threat detail. 2) The asymmetry could depend on steering budget definition; we sweep budgets and report full amplify/suppress curves rather than single points. 3) SAE quality/layer choice may confound which features 'encode' hazard vs benign; we control with multiple SAE variants, probe verification, difference-of-means directions, and random-direction controls, and we avoid the SAE-reconstructed-steering-vector pitfall documented in the literature. 4) Subspace-overlap predictor may be underpowered with few hazard classes; we include many toxicophore/motif classes across both domains. 5) If no asymmetry is found (hazard is as easy to suppress as amplify, with low collateral), that is a valuable, publishable positive safety result establishing that scientific generative models can be cleanly disarmed. 6) We will responsibly report aggregate metrics and mitigations (orthogonalized suppression), not recipes for producing specific hazardous objects."
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'hazard_entanglement_asymmetry', 'Title': 'Easy to Arm, Hard to Disarm: An Entanglement Asymmetry Between Hazardous and Benign Design Features in Scientific Generative Models', 'Short Hypothesis': 'In molecular and protein generative models, interpretable features exhibit a dual-use entanglement asymmetry: hazardous structural properties (toxicophores, known toxin/venom motifs, reactive groups) can be AMPLIFIED cheaply by steering ostensibly-benign, property-labeled features, yet cannot be SUPPRESSED without disproportionate collateral loss of valid, benign design capability. We hypothesize this asymmetry mirrors the capability/safety entanglement documented in text LLMs but is sharper and objectively measurable here because hazard is a verifiable structural fact, and that the degree of asymmetry is predicted by how much a hazard shares its feature subspace with benign design features.', 'Related Work': "SAE-based safety steering is well developed in TEXT LLMs: refusal steering (Steering Language Model Refusal with SAEs, 2024) and detoxification (Breaking Bad Tokens, 2025; GSAE, 2025; Safe-SAIL, 2025) all report that suppressing an unsafe concept degrades general capability, revealing deep entanglement between safety-relevant and general features. Separately, scientific-model interpretability (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; pathology-FM SAEs 2024; chemistry-LM SAEs 2025) discovers and steers interpretable features toward single BENIGN target properties, treating hazard only implicitly. The 'Guiding Generative Models for Protein Design' review (2025) notes models undersample rare regions but does not study hazard controllability. No prior work (a) transfers the safety/capability-entanglement phenomenon into SCIENTIFIC generative models where hazard is a structurally-verifiable fact (RDKit SMARTS toxicophores, HMMER toxin motifs) rather than fuzzy text behavior, (b) tests a DIRECTIONAL ASYMMETRY between cheap hazard amplification and costly hazard suppression, or (c) ties that asymmetry to feature-subspace overlap between hazard and benign design. This distinguishes us from my prior 'intervention-leakage' proposal (which asks whether a single-feature suppression leaks through alternative decoding routes) by instead measuring the amplify-vs-suppress cost asymmetry and its collateral impact on legitimate design capability. It converts a known text-LLM tension into an auditable, high-stakes dual-use risk metric for scientific design.", 'Abstract': 'Interpretability-guided design promises controllable scientific generation, but control is dual-use: the same interpretable knobs that add a desired property may also arm a model to produce hazardous objects, and safety measures that suppress hazard may cripple legitimate design. Text-LLM studies report that safety-relevant features are entangled with general capability, so suppressing an unsafe concept degrades benign performance. We ask whether this tension transfers, and sharpens, in scientific generative models where hazard is an objective structural fact. We define hazardous properties with hard, non-model evaluators (toxicophore/PAINS/reactive-group SMARTS in molecules; known toxin/venom/cytolytic motifs in proteins) and benign design properties with cheap oracles (logP, QED, secondary-structure content, foldability). Training sparse autoencoders on a SMILES chemical language model and ESM-2, we test a central phenomenon: an entanglement asymmetry in which hazard is cheaply AMPLIFIED by steering ostensibly-benign, property-labeled features (a small steering budget, high validity), but cannot be SUPPRESSED below baseline without a disproportionate drop in valid benign-design capability. We quantify an Asymmetry Index = (cost to suppress) / (cost to amplify) and a Collateral Ratio = benign-design loss per unit hazard removed, comparing single-feature, subspace, and difference-of-means interventions and controlling for the known pitfall that SAE-reconstructed steering vectors are unreliable. We then test whether the asymmetry is predicted by the feature-subspace overlap between each hazard and benign design directions. Results would establish a measurable, mechanistically-grounded dual-use risk profile for scientific generative models and a practical audit for whether hazard can be safely disarmed without breaking legitimate design.', 'Experiments': '1) MODELS & HAZARD/BENIGN LABELS: Use a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM (ChEMBL/ZINC-trained checkpoint) and ESM-2 (150M/650M). Train TopK SAEs on the residual stream at a mid layer (chosen by intrinsic-dimension heuristic); verify reconstruction fidelity. HAZARD properties (hard non-model evaluators): molecules - presence of toxicophore/PAINS SMARTS, reactive/electrophilic groups, structural-alert counts (RDKit); proteins - known toxin/venom/cytolytic sequence motifs (HMMER against curated toxin HMMs / regex) and disallowed motif classes. BENIGN design properties (cheap oracles): molecules - logP band, QED, ring/scaffold targets, synthetic accessibility; proteins - alpha-helix/beta-sheet content (DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY, a fold/foldability target. VALIDITY gates: RDKit-validity + SA for molecules; ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins.\n2) FEATURE BANKS: Identify (i) BENIGN property-labeled features via concept-correlation/probe AUROC on held-out natural objects (the ostensibly-safe design knobs), and (ii) HAZARD-linked features/subspaces for each hazard class. Record concept-correlation for all.\n3) AMPLIFY-VS-SUPPRESS COST (core phenomenon): For each hazard class, measure (a) AMPLIFICATION cost = minimal steering budget (norm of intervention) to raise hazard occurrence in FINISHED objects to a fixed high rate, via steering benign property-labeled features (test whether benign knobs alone arm the model) and via hazard features; (b) SUPPRESSION cost = minimal budget to drive baseline hazard occurrence below a fixed low threshold. Track validity throughout. Report Asymmetry Index = suppression cost / amplification cost per hazard class, across single-feature, k-dim subspace (SVD of correlated features), and difference-of-means interventions. To avoid the known pitfall that SAE-reconstructed steering vectors are OOD/sign-limited, use SAE decoder directions and difference-of-means directions directly (not SAE re-encodings of steering vectors), and report both.\n4) COLLATERAL RATIO (does disarming break benign design?): While suppressing each hazard to the target threshold, measure the drop in valid benign-design capability = success rate of hitting a battery of legitimate benign design targets (logP/QED/scaffold; secondary-structure/fold) and overall validity/diversity. Collateral Ratio = benign-design success loss per unit hazard-occurrence removed. Compare against text-LLM-style capability degradation baselines by including a random-direction suppression control matched in norm.\n5) SUBSPACE-OVERLAP PREDICTOR (mechanism): For each hazard class compute its feature-subspace overlap with benign design directions (principal angles / mean cosine between hazard-feature subspace and benign-feature subspace; effective shared rank). Regress Asymmetry Index and Collateral Ratio on this overlap (Spearman/R^2, bootstrap CIs). Hypothesis: higher overlap -> larger asymmetry and higher collateral. Test a mitigation: subspace suppression that projects out ONLY the hazard-specific complement (orthogonalized against benign directions) and check whether it lowers Collateral Ratio at equal hazard removal.\n6) DUAL-USE STRESS TEST: Quantify how easily benign-only knobs arm the model - fraction of benign property-labeled features whose amplification incidentally raises any hazard above baseline (an off-target hazard leakage rate), establishing an auditable dual-use exposure metric per feature.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, and two SAE variants (TopK, Matryoshka); report where the asymmetry is stable vs artifact.\nEVALUATION METRICS: Asymmetry Index (primary), Collateral Ratio, off-target hazard leakage rate of benign knobs, subspace-overlap predictor Spearman/R^2, validity retention (RDKit-validity/SA, ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity), benign-design success/diversity, and the orthogonalized-suppression mitigation effect.', 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) Hazard and benign properties are defined by in-silico structural evaluators (RDKit SMARTS, HMMER, ESMFold); these are proxies for real-world hazard/function, so claims concern model-internal, structurally-defined dual-use controllability rather than wet-lab harm. We deliberately study only structural-alert-level hazards with unambiguous detectors and avoid synthesizable-threat detail. 2) The asymmetry could depend on steering budget definition; we sweep budgets and report full amplify/suppress curves rather than single points. 3) SAE quality/layer choice may confound which features 'encode' hazard vs benign; we control with multiple SAE variants, probe verification, difference-of-means directions, and random-direction controls, and we avoid the SAE-reconstructed-steering-vector pitfall documented in the literature. 4) Subspace-overlap predictor may be underpowered with few hazard classes; we include many toxicophore/motif classes across both domains. 5) If no asymmetry is found (hazard is as easy to suppress as amplify, with low collateral), that is a valuable, publishable positive safety result establishing that scientific generative models can be cleanly disarmed. 6) We will responsibly report aggregate metrics and mitigations (orthogonalized suppression), not recipes for producing specific hazardous objects."}

