Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "steering_fails_on_memorized_designs", "Title": "You Can't Steer a Memory: Interpretable-Feature Control Silently Fails on Memorized Scientific Designs", "Short Hypothesis": "Scientific generative models occupy a hybrid regime that memorizes some outputs (near-verbatim training retrievals) and generalizes others. We hypothesize that interpretable-feature steering, the dominant paradigm for controllable design, CAUSALLY FAILS on memorized generations while succeeding on generalized ones\u2014because memorized objects sit at contractive attractors whose internal representations are 'spiky' and resist perturbation. Consequently, a cheap, intervention-free representation-spikiness score predicts, before any steering, whether a given generation is controllable, and steering evaluated only on aggregate averages hides a large, systematically uncontrollable memorized subpopulation.", "Related Work": "Two lines converge here for the first time. (1) Memorization geometry: 'Generalization of Diffusion Models Arises with a Balanced Representation Space' (2025) proves memorized samples yield spiky representations and are NOT steerable while generalized samples are steerable in representation space; 'Overparameterized autoencoders implement associative memory' (2018/2020) shows memorized examples are contractive attractors. (2) Scientific-model controllability: InterPLM (2024), ProtSAE (2025), and chemistry-LM SAE steering demonstrate interpretable features steer protein/molecule generation, but ALWAYS report AGGREGATE property gains and never partition outputs by memorization status. Meanwhile 'Transformer Learning in Sequence-Based Drug Design Depends on Compound Memorization' (2026) and protein-LM data-leakage studies (2024/2025) show scientific generative models are heavily memorization-driven, yet treat this as a benchmark-inflation problem, not a controllability problem. No prior work (a) tests whether the memorized-not-steerable geometry transfers to SCIENTIFIC generative models where memorization is objectively measurable via sequence identity to the training set, (b) shows interpretable-feature steering causally fails on the memorized subpopulation, or (c) validates a pre-flight spikiness score that predicts per-generation controllability. This distinguishes the proposal from all my prior steering-focused ideas (which assumed the target is steerable) and from the memorization literature (which ignores controllable design).", "Abstract": "Interpretable-feature steering has become the standard tool for controllable design of proteins and molecules, and it is universally evaluated by average property gains across many generations. We ask whether that average conceals a systematic failure mode. Recent geometry work shows generative models occupy a hybrid regime\u2014memorizing some outputs as contractive attractors with 'spiky' internal representations while generalizing others\u2014and that memorized samples resist representation-space steering. Scientific generative models are known to be heavily memorization-driven, and, uniquely, memorization here is objectively measurable: a generation is memorized if it is near-identical to a training sequence. We hypothesize that interpretable-feature steering causally fails on memorized generations while succeeding on generalized ones, so aggregate steering metrics hide a large uncontrollable subpopulation. Using ESM-style protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders, we label each generation as memorized or generalized via sequence/structure identity to the (known) training corpus, then measure per-generation steerability of feature interventions against cheap property oracles. We test three claims: (1) steering property-gain and validity are far lower on memorized than generalized generations at matched steering budget; (2) an intervention-free representation-spikiness score (activation standard deviation / SAE-feature concentration) predicts per-generation steerability before any intervention, enabling a pre-flight controllability filter; and (3) nudging generation away from memorized attractors (light noise/temperature or a 'de-spiking' repulsion from the nearest training neighbor) restores steerability, at a measurable novelty cost. Results would reveal a hidden, safety- and IP-relevant blind spot in controllable scientific design and provide a cheap test for when steering can be trusted.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, TRAINING-SET ACCESS, ORACLES: Use generators whose training corpus is known so memorization is measurable: a protein LM trained on a defined UniRef subset (a small self-trained ESM-style model, 8M/35M, plus a public checkpoint with a known training set such as ProtGPT2) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM trained on a defined ChEMBL/ZINC subset. Train TopK SAEs on the residual stream at a mid layer (chosen by intrinsic-dimension heuristic); verify reconstruction fidelity. Cheap property oracles: proteins\u2014alpha-helix/beta-sheet content (DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY hydrophobicity, molecular weight, motifs (HMMER/regex); molecules\u2014RDKit logP, QED, ring/aromatic counts, functional groups (SMARTS). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + synthetic accessibility for molecules.\n2) MEMORIZATION LABELING (ground truth): Generate a large unsteered corpus (e.g., 20k per model). Label each generation MEMORIZED if it is near-identical to a training example\u2014proteins: sequence identity above a high threshold to the nearest training sequence (mmseqs2/BLAST), optionally corroborated by structural TM-score; molecules: Tanimoto/Morgan-fingerprint similarity above a high threshold or exact canonical-SMILES match to a training molecule. Label GENERALIZED if identity to the nearest training example is low. Report the memorized fraction per model and its dependence on model size and sampling temperature, establishing the hybrid regime.\n3) FEATURE BANK: Identify interpretable, property-linked SAE features via concept-correlation/probe AUROC on held-out natural objects (for structure content, hydrophobicity, motifs, logP, functional groups). Also include difference-of-means directions as a non-SAE steering baseline.\n4) CORE TEST\u2014STEERABILITY BY MEMORIZATION STATUS: For matched memorized vs generalized generations (matched on baseline property value and sequence length to control confounds), apply feature steering at a swept budget and measure per-generation steerability = target-property change per unit steer within the validity budget, plus max reliable property gain and validity retention. Primary result: memorized generations show significantly lower steerability and worse control-validity frontier than generalized ones at matched budget (mixed-effects model with memorization status as the key factor; report effect size, CIs). Include random-direction controls. Also demonstrate the AGGREGATE-METRIC BLIND SPOT: show that reported average steering gain masks a bimodal distribution split by memorization.\n5) SPIKINESS PREDICTS STEERABILITY (intervention-free filter): For each generation compute cheap representation-spikiness scores at the intervention site: activation standard deviation across dimensions, SAE-feature activation concentration (fraction of mass in top features / entropy of the feature vector), and local contractivity proxy (change in representation under small input perturbation). Regress per-generation steerability on spikiness (Spearman/R^2, bootstrap CIs) and test whether spikiness predicts steerability BETTER than concept-correlation and even better than the (expensive) memorization label\u2014because it captures the continuous attractor geometry. Validate spikiness as a PRE-FLIGHT controllability filter: precision@k for selecting steerable generations, and compute saved by skipping doomed steering attempts.\n6) RESTORING STEERABILITY (causal test of the attractor account): Test interventions that push generation off memorized attractors before steering: (a) mild temperature/nucleus widening, (b) initial-token/masked-position perturbation, (c) an explicit 'de-spiking' repulsion that steers away from the SAE-representation of the nearest training neighbor. Measure whether these convert memorized generations into steerable ones (steerability gain), and quantify the NOVELTY cost (drop in identity to nearest training neighbor) and validity cost. Hypothesis-confirming result: leaving the attractor restores controllability, causally linking memorization geometry to steering failure.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, two SAE variants (TopK, Matryoshka), and two similarity thresholds for the memorization label; report where the effect is stable vs artifact.\nEVALUATION METRICS: steerability gap memorized-vs-generalized (primary effect size), control-validity frontier area per subpopulation, bimodality of aggregate steering gain, spikiness-vs-steerability Spearman/R^2 and precision@k of the pre-flight filter, compute saved, restoration steerability gain vs novelty/validity cost, memorized fraction vs model size/temperature.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Requires knowing the training corpus to label memorization; we mitigate by self-training small models on defined subsets and using public checkpoints with documented training sets, and by using high-similarity thresholds that give conservative memorized labels. 2) In-silico oracles/validity (ESMFold, RDKit) are proxies for wet-lab function; claims concern model-internal controllability and design reliability, not experimental viability. 3) Lower steerability on memorized generations could be confounded by their baseline property values or length; we control via matched-pair comparison and mixed-effects modeling. 4) Spikiness might merely correlate with memorization rather than causing low steerability; experiment 6 (de-spiking restores steerability) is the causal test that disentangles this. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could affect both spikiness and steerability; we control with multiple SAE variants, layers, and difference-of-means baselines. 6) If steering turns out equally effective on memorized generations, that is still a valuable, publishable negative result establishing that the diffusion-model memorized-not-steerable geometry does NOT transfer to scientific sequence models\u2014constraining theories of memorization geometry across modalities. 7) The de-spiking restoration trades novelty for controllability; we quantify this frontier rather than assume a free lunch."}}
