Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "steering_reliability_collapse", "Title": "Controlled but Unrepresentable: Does Interpretable-Feature Steering Push Scientific Generative Models Into Their Own Confidence Blind Spots?", "Short Hypothesis": "Steering interpretable features to control a target property silently drags generation into regions of sequence/molecule space where the model's OWN representation becomes unreliable (embedding-quality collapses toward random-like), so the apparent property gain is untrustworthy exactly where steering is strongest. We hypothesize a cheap, intervention-free internal representation-reliability score falls monotonically with steering strength, predicts when the claimed property is real vs illusory, and can gate steering to keep only trustworthy controlled designs\u2014an axis that output-side property predictors and validity checks completely miss.", "Related Work": "Two threads meet for the first time. (1) SAE/neuron steering of scientific generative models (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; FoldSAE 2025; Automated Neuron Labelling 2025; Steering PLMs/ASPO 2025; ProtoMech 2026; antibody SAE 2025) all report property GAIN from steering and evaluate success via property predictors; none asks whether steering pushes outputs into regions where the model can no longer reliably represent them. (2) Representation-reliability work\u2014'Quantifying Uncertainty in Protein Representations Across Models and Tasks' (2025) shows pLM embeddings for undersampled sequences become vector-indistinguishable from random, and 'Quantifying Feature Space Universality' plus data-saturation studies show embedding quality tracks training coverage\u2014but this is studied only for NATURAL inputs, never as a CONSEQUENCE of steering, and never linked to the trustworthiness of controlled designs. No prior work (a) shows steering induces representation-reliability collapse, (b) demonstrates that property predictors and validity checks fail to detect this while an internal reliability score does, or (c) uses reliability to gate steering. This is distinct from my prior proposals on memorization-attractors (about contractive attractors resisting steering), validity-foresight (about invalid finished objects), and infeasible-spec frustration (about contradictory constraints): here the finished object can be perfectly valid and hit the property predictor, yet sit in a representational blind spot that makes the control claim untrustworthy.", "Abstract": "Interpretable-feature steering is the dominant tool for controllable design of proteins and molecules, and it is judged almost entirely by whether a target-property predictor says the property increased. We surface a hidden failure mode: the very act of steering can push generation into regions where the model's own internal representation becomes unreliable\u2014its embeddings drift toward random-like, low-information vectors\u2014so the property 'gain' is measured on objects the model can no longer faithfully represent. Because the objects still look valid (they fold, they parse) and still satisfy an external predictor, standard evaluations miss this entirely. We hypothesize that a cheap, intervention-free representation-reliability score (embedding self-consistency / distance-to-random / feature-activation coherence) declines monotonically with steering strength, that it predicts per-generation whether the claimed property is real (agrees with a held-out physics oracle) versus illusory, and that gating steering by this score recovers a trustworthy control-validity-reliability frontier. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders, we (1) demonstrate reliability collapse as a function of steering strength and target property; (2) show that output-side property predictors and validity checks fail to flag it while the internal reliability score succeeds; (3) causally test whether reliability collapse is what makes the property claim untrustworthy by comparing predictor-property against an independent oracle stratified by reliability; and (4) deploy a reliability-gated steering controller that keeps only trustworthy designs. Results would add representational reliability as a necessary, currently-ignored axis for controllable scientific design.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK SAEs on the residual stream at a mid layer (intrinsic-dimension heuristic); verify reconstruction fidelity. Assemble a feature bank (~20-40 features) mapped to measurable properties via concept-correlation: proteins\u2014alpha-helix/beta-sheet content, GRAVY hydrophobicity, molecular weight, thermostability proxy, motifs (zinc finger, catalytic triad); molecules\u2014logP, QED, ring/aromatic counts, functional groups. Property predictors: cheap in-silico evaluators used the way the steering literature uses them (DSSP/ESMFold, biopython, fine-tuned heads, RDKit/SMARTS). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + SA for molecules.\n2) DEFINE REPRESENTATION-RELIABILITY SCORE (intervention-free): For each generated object, compute cheap internal reliability signals at the model's own embedding: (a) distance-to-random\u2014Mahalanobis/cosine gap between the object's embedding and the distribution of embeddings of shuffled/random sequences (following the protein-embedding-uncertainty method), (b) embedding self-consistency under small input perturbations (masking/dropout), (c) SAE-feature coherence (how well active features co-fire as they do on natural objects). Combine into a single reliability score; validate on NATURAL held-out objects that it tracks known embedding-quality signals (e.g., sequence-space coverage / neighbor density).\n3) CORE PHENOMENON\u2014RELIABILITY COLLAPSE VS STEERING STRENGTH: For each feature/property, sweep steering strength and, at each level, generate N objects and record: property-predictor gain, validity, AND reliability score. Test the hypothesis that reliability declines monotonically with steering strength\u2014often WELL BEFORE validity or property-predictor gain break down (mixed-effects model; report the steering strength at which reliability crosses a natural-object threshold vs where validity does). Compare SAE-feature steering, difference-of-means steering, and random-direction controls.\n4) DOES THE CONTROL CLAIM STAY TRUE?\u2014RELIABILITY-STRATIFIED ORACLE TEST (causal-ish): For a subset, adjudicate each generation's true property with an INDEPENDENT oracle not used in steering (proteins: FoldX/Rosetta-style stability or a distinct structure metric; molecules: an empirical/second logP model or docking). Test whether predictor-vs-oracle AGREEMENT on the target property degrades sharply for LOW-reliability generations while staying high for HIGH-reliability ones\u2014i.e., the property gain is real when reliable and illusory when not. Primary metric: predictor-oracle agreement stratified by reliability quartile.\n5) DOES ANYTHING ELSE CATCH IT? Compare the internal reliability score against output-side signals available at no extra cost\u2014the property predictor's own confidence, pseudo-perplexity, ESMFold pLDDT/RDKit-validity, and a black-box raw-activation reliability probe\u2014for their ability to predict low predictor-oracle agreement (AUROC, calibration/ECE). Hypothesis: validity/property-confidence are near-chance at flagging untrustworthy control, while the reliability score (and possibly the black-box probe) succeed; the SAE-based score adds interpretability (which features lose coherence).\n6) RELIABILITY-GATED STEERING CONTROLLER (application): Deploy a controller that, at generation time, rejects or down-weights steering that drives reliability below threshold, tracing a control-validity-RELIABILITY frontier. Compare valid+trustworthy yield per unit compute against ungated steering and validity-only gating. Metrics: fraction of generations that are simultaneously property-satisfying (by oracle), valid, and reliable; diversity/novelty retention; compute saved by not validating untrustworthy designs.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, two SAE variants (TopK, Matryoshka), and multiple properties; report which property types collapse reliability fastest (e.g., off-distribution property combinations vs common ones) and where the effect is stable vs artifact.\nEVALUATION METRICS: reliability-vs-steering-strength slope and collapse-before-validity gap (primary phenomenon); predictor-oracle agreement stratified by reliability (trustworthiness test); AUROC/ECE of internal reliability vs output-side baselines for flagging untrustworthy control; control-validity-reliability frontier area and trustworthy-yield-per-compute for the gated controller; per-property reliability-collapse rates; SAE-feature-coherence interpretability.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The independent oracle (FoldX/Rosetta/second logP model/docking) is itself an in-silico proxy; the trustworthiness claim rests on DISAGREEMENT between two independent in-silico signals stratified by reliability, not wet-lab truth\u2014stated explicitly. 2) The reliability score could merely correlate with off-distribution-ness rather than causing untrustworthy property claims; we mitigate by (a) validating it on natural objects with known coverage and (b) the reliability-stratified oracle test that ties low reliability to predictor failure, and by random-direction controls. 3) An internal SAE-based reliability score might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; even so, interpretability (naming which features decohere) and the frontier/compute gains are the contribution and are reported honestly. 4) Reliability collapse might be trivially entangled with validity collapse; the core test specifically isolates the regime where objects remain valid and predictor-satisfying yet unreliable, and reports the gap\u2014if no such gap exists, that is itself a clean, publishable negative result. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound both steering and reliability; controlled via multiple SAE variants, layers, and difference-of-means baselines. 6) In-silico property/validity evaluators are proxies; claims concern model-internal representational trustworthiness of controlled designs, not experimental function."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'steering_reliability_collapse', 'Title': 'Controlled but Unrepresentable: Does Interpretable-Feature Steering Push Scientific Generative Models Into Their Own Confidence Blind Spots?', 'Short Hypothesis': "Steering interpretable features to control a target property silently drags generation into regions of sequence/molecule space where the model's OWN representation becomes unreliable (embedding-quality collapses toward random-like), so the apparent property gain is untrustworthy exactly where steering is strongest. We hypothesize a cheap, intervention-free internal representation-reliability score falls monotonically with steering strength, predicts when the claimed property is real vs illusory, and can gate steering to keep only trustworthy controlled designs—an axis that output-side property predictors and validity checks completely miss.", 'Related Work': "Two threads meet for the first time. (1) SAE/neuron steering of scientific generative models (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; FoldSAE 2025; Automated Neuron Labelling 2025; Steering PLMs/ASPO 2025; ProtoMech 2026; antibody SAE 2025) all report property GAIN from steering and evaluate success via property predictors; none asks whether steering pushes outputs into regions where the model can no longer reliably represent them. (2) Representation-reliability work—'Quantifying Uncertainty in Protein Representations Across Models and Tasks' (2025) shows pLM embeddings for undersampled sequences become vector-indistinguishable from random, and 'Quantifying Feature Space Universality' plus data-saturation studies show embedding quality tracks training coverage—but this is studied only for NATURAL inputs, never as a CONSEQUENCE of steering, and never linked to the trustworthiness of controlled designs. No prior work (a) shows steering induces representation-reliability collapse, (b) demonstrates that property predictors and validity checks fail to detect this while an internal reliability score does, or (c) uses reliability to gate steering. This is distinct from my prior proposals on memorization-attractors (about contractive attractors resisting steering), validity-foresight (about invalid finished objects), and infeasible-spec frustration (about contradictory constraints): here the finished object can be perfectly valid and hit the property predictor, yet sit in a representational blind spot that makes the control claim untrustworthy.", 'Abstract': "Interpretable-feature steering is the dominant tool for controllable design of proteins and molecules, and it is judged almost entirely by whether a target-property predictor says the property increased. We surface a hidden failure mode: the very act of steering can push generation into regions where the model's own internal representation becomes unreliable—its embeddings drift toward random-like, low-information vectors—so the property 'gain' is measured on objects the model can no longer faithfully represent. Because the objects still look valid (they fold, they parse) and still satisfy an external predictor, standard evaluations miss this entirely. We hypothesize that a cheap, intervention-free representation-reliability score (embedding self-consistency / distance-to-random / feature-activation coherence) declines monotonically with steering strength, that it predicts per-generation whether the claimed property is real (agrees with a held-out physics oracle) versus illusory, and that gating steering by this score recovers a trustworthy control-validity-reliability frontier. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders, we (1) demonstrate reliability collapse as a function of steering strength and target property; (2) show that output-side property predictors and validity checks fail to flag it while the internal reliability score succeeds; (3) causally test whether reliability collapse is what makes the property claim untrustworthy by comparing predictor-property against an independent oracle stratified by reliability; and (4) deploy a reliability-gated steering controller that keeps only trustworthy designs. Results would add representational reliability as a necessary, currently-ignored axis for controllable scientific design.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK SAEs on the residual stream at a mid layer (intrinsic-dimension heuristic); verify reconstruction fidelity. Assemble a feature bank (~20-40 features) mapped to measurable properties via concept-correlation: proteins—alpha-helix/beta-sheet content, GRAVY hydrophobicity, molecular weight, thermostability proxy, motifs (zinc finger, catalytic triad); molecules—logP, QED, ring/aromatic counts, functional groups. Property predictors: cheap in-silico evaluators used the way the steering literature uses them (DSSP/ESMFold, biopython, fine-tuned heads, RDKit/SMARTS). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + SA for molecules.\n2) DEFINE REPRESENTATION-RELIABILITY SCORE (intervention-free): For each generated object, compute cheap internal reliability signals at the model's own embedding: (a) distance-to-random—Mahalanobis/cosine gap between the object's embedding and the distribution of embeddings of shuffled/random sequences (following the protein-embedding-uncertainty method), (b) embedding self-consistency under small input perturbations (masking/dropout), (c) SAE-feature coherence (how well active features co-fire as they do on natural objects). Combine into a single reliability score; validate on NATURAL held-out objects that it tracks known embedding-quality signals (e.g., sequence-space coverage / neighbor density).\n3) CORE PHENOMENON—RELIABILITY COLLAPSE VS STEERING STRENGTH: For each feature/property, sweep steering strength and, at each level, generate N objects and record: property-predictor gain, validity, AND reliability score. Test the hypothesis that reliability declines monotonically with steering strength—often WELL BEFORE validity or property-predictor gain break down (mixed-effects model; report the steering strength at which reliability crosses a natural-object threshold vs where validity does). Compare SAE-feature steering, difference-of-means steering, and random-direction controls.\n4) DOES THE CONTROL CLAIM STAY TRUE?—RELIABILITY-STRATIFIED ORACLE TEST (causal-ish): For a subset, adjudicate each generation's true property with an INDEPENDENT oracle not used in steering (proteins: FoldX/Rosetta-style stability or a distinct structure metric; molecules: an empirical/second logP model or docking). Test whether predictor-vs-oracle AGREEMENT on the target property degrades sharply for LOW-reliability generations while staying high for HIGH-reliability ones—i.e., the property gain is real when reliable and illusory when not. Primary metric: predictor-oracle agreement stratified by reliability quartile.\n5) DOES ANYTHING ELSE CATCH IT? Compare the internal reliability score against output-side signals available at no extra cost—the property predictor's own confidence, pseudo-perplexity, ESMFold pLDDT/RDKit-validity, and a black-box raw-activation reliability probe—for their ability to predict low predictor-oracle agreement (AUROC, calibration/ECE). Hypothesis: validity/property-confidence are near-chance at flagging untrustworthy control, while the reliability score (and possibly the black-box probe) succeed; the SAE-based score adds interpretability (which features lose coherence).\n6) RELIABILITY-GATED STEERING CONTROLLER (application): Deploy a controller that, at generation time, rejects or down-weights steering that drives reliability below threshold, tracing a control-validity-RELIABILITY frontier. Compare valid+trustworthy yield per unit compute against ungated steering and validity-only gating. Metrics: fraction of generations that are simultaneously property-satisfying (by oracle), valid, and reliable; diversity/novelty retention; compute saved by not validating untrustworthy designs.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, two SAE variants (TopK, Matryoshka), and multiple properties; report which property types collapse reliability fastest (e.g., off-distribution property combinations vs common ones) and where the effect is stable vs artifact.\nEVALUATION METRICS: reliability-vs-steering-strength slope and collapse-before-validity gap (primary phenomenon); predictor-oracle agreement stratified by reliability (trustworthiness test); AUROC/ECE of internal reliability vs output-side baselines for flagging untrustworthy control; control-validity-reliability frontier area and trustworthy-yield-per-compute for the gated controller; per-property reliability-collapse rates; SAE-feature-coherence interpretability.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) The independent oracle (FoldX/Rosetta/second logP model/docking) is itself an in-silico proxy; the trustworthiness claim rests on DISAGREEMENT between two independent in-silico signals stratified by reliability, not wet-lab truth—stated explicitly. 2) The reliability score could merely correlate with off-distribution-ness rather than causing untrustworthy property claims; we mitigate by (a) validating it on natural objects with known coverage and (b) the reliability-stratified oracle test that ties low reliability to predictor failure, and by random-direction controls. 3) An internal SAE-based reliability score might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; even so, interpretability (naming which features decohere) and the frontier/compute gains are the contribution and are reported honestly. 4) Reliability collapse might be trivially entangled with validity collapse; the core test specifically isolates the regime where objects remain valid and predictor-satisfying yet unreliable, and reports the gap—if no such gap exists, that is itself a clean, publishable negative result. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound both steering and reliability; controlled via multiple SAE variants, layers, and difference-of-means baselines. 6) In-silico property/validity evaluators are proxies; claims concern model-internal representational trustworthiness of controlled designs, not experimental function.'}

