Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "steering_sideeffect_law",
  "Title": "The Conservation of Trouble: Are Off-Target Property Drifts of Interpretable-Feature Steering Lawful and Predictable in Scientific Generative Models?",
  "Short Hypothesis": "When an interpretable feature is steered to control one property of a generated protein or molecule, it induces off-target drift in OTHER, structurally-verifiable properties that is not random noise but LAWFUL: the drift vector across all properties is predicted, before any generation, by a fixed feature-specific 'side-effect signature' derived from the geometry of the SAE dictionary. This lets practitioners forecast and pre-empt collateral damage, choosing minimal-side-effect features and orthogonalized directions to hit a target property while holding others fixed.",
  "Related Work": "Text-LLM steering studies extensively document off-target side effects and entanglement: SAE-Targeted Steering (Chalnev et al. 2024) minimizes side effects using SAE-measured effect vectors; Rogue Scalpel (2025), Steering Externalities (2026), SteeringSafety (2025), Steering Refusal with SAEs (2024), and Mind the Performance Gap (2026) all show steering degrades unrelated capabilities or safety in a method/model-dependent way. Crucially, all of these (a) operate in TEXT LLMs where the off-target 'property' is a fuzzy behavior or benchmark score, and (b) treat side effects as a nuisance to be measured post hoc or empirically minimized. Scientific-model steering work (InterPLM 2024, ProtSAE 2025, chemistry-LM SAEs) reports only ON-target property gains and evaluates validity as a side constraint, never characterizing structured off-target property drift. No prior work (i) transfers the off-target-drift phenomenon to scientific generative models where every off-target property (helix content, charge, logP, ring count, motif presence) is an OBJECTIVELY VERIFIABLE structural fact, (ii) tests whether the drift is LAWFUL and predictable a priori from a fixed feature side-effect signature rather than needing per-target measurement, or (iii) turns that law into a pre-flight side-effect forecaster and a minimal-collateral feature/direction selector. This distinguishes it from my prior compositionality proposal (which co-steers several INTENDED targets) by studying UNINTENDED drift on non-targeted properties and its a-priori predictability.",
  "Abstract": "Steering interpretable features to control a target property of a generated protein or molecule is the standard controllable-design paradigm, and it is judged almost entirely by whether the target property moves. In text language models, steering is known to cause unintended off-target effects, but there the 'off-target property' is a fuzzy behavior and the effect is treated as a post-hoc nuisance. We transfer this phenomenon to scientific generative models, where every off-target property is an objectively verifiable structural fact, and ask a sharper question: is the collateral drift lawful and predictable BEFORE any generation? We hypothesize that each interpretable feature carries a fixed 'side-effect signature'\u2014the direction in property space it perturbs\u2014computable from the SAE dictionary geometry (via decoder-direction overlaps and a per-feature property-Jacobian), so that steering toward property A produces a forecastable drift vector across properties B, C, D that no output-side check anticipates. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders, we (1) demonstrate that single-target steering induces substantial, structured off-target property drift measured by hard oracles (DSSP/ESMFold, biopython, RDKit/SMARTS); (2) test whether an intervention-free side-effect signature predicts each feature's actual drift vector across steering strengths (a 'steering side-effect law'); (3) validate the signature as a pre-flight forecaster that ranks features by expected collateral damage; and (4) deploy a minimal-collateral controller\u2014selecting low-side-effect features or orthogonalizing the steering direction against high-drift property axes\u2014to hit the target while holding others fixed, tracing a target-gain-versus-collateral frontier. Results would recast off-target drift from an unpredictable hazard into a forecastable, controllable design quantity.",
  "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, PROPERTY ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK SAEs on the residual stream at a mid layer (chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic); verify reconstruction fidelity. Define a battery of measurable properties, each with a cheap HARD oracle so drift is an objective fact: proteins\u2014alpha-helix content, beta-sheet content, GRAVY hydrophobicity, net charge, molecular weight, motif presence (HMMER/regex, e.g. zinc finger); molecules\u2014logP, QED, ring/aromatic-ring count, molecular weight, TPSA, functional-group SMARTS. Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + synthetic accessibility for molecules.\n2) FEATURE BANK: Identify interpretable, property-linked SAE features via concept-correlation/probe AUROC on held-out natural objects (features whose intended TARGET is one property in the battery). Include difference-of-means directions as a non-SAE baseline. Assemble ~30-50 target features per domain.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON\u2014STRUCTURED OFF-TARGET DRIFT: For each target feature, sweep steering strength and GENERATE N objects per level. For each level, measure the FULL property vector (all battery oracles), not just the target. Compute the off-target drift vector = change in every non-target property. Quantify: (a) collateral magnitude (norm of off-target drift relative to on-target gain), (b) direction consistency of the drift across strengths and seeds (is it a stable signature or noise?), and (c) that off-target drift is INVISIBLE to output-side checks by correlating validity/pseudo-perplexity/RDKit-validity with true off-target drift (hypothesis: near-zero, so standard evals miss it). Include random-direction controls matched in norm.\n4) THE SIDE-EFFECT LAW (Location + Unit Interpretation): For each feature, compute an INTERVENTION-FREE side-effect signature predicting its drift vector: (i) decoder-direction overlaps\u2014cosine of the feature's decoder direction with other property features' directions; (ii) a per-feature property-Jacobian\u2014gradient of each (differentiable-surrogate) property predictor w.r.t. residual-stream activations, projected onto the feature direction. Predict the drift vector from these and correlate with the MEASURED drift vector (from step 3). Primary metric: cosine similarity / R^2 between predicted and actual drift vectors across features, and per-property Spearman. Hypothesis: the signature predicts drift far better than chance and better than concept-correlation, establishing lawfulness.\n5) PRE-FLIGHT FORECASTER (application): Use the side-effect signature to RANK features by expected total collateral damage for a given target property, before any generation. Validate: precision@k for selecting genuinely low-collateral features, and compute saved vs trial-and-error generation-based screening. Also emit a human-readable forecast ('steering feature F for helix will raise hydrophobicity and lower charge').\n6) MINIMAL-COLLATERAL CONTROLLER (Causal Intervention / Tuning): Two interventions to reduce collateral while preserving target gain\u2014(a) select the lowest-forecast-collateral feature for the target property; (b) orthogonalize the steering direction against the property-Jacobian axes of the properties to be HELD FIXED. Compare against naive best-concept-correlation steering on a target-gain-vs-collateral Pareto frontier: on-target gain, off-target drift norm on held-fixed properties, validity, novelty/diversity. Hypothesis: orthogonalized/selected steering Pareto-dominates naive steering.\n7) POSITIVE CONTROL: Construct two features known to share a decoder subspace (high overlap) and two known-orthogonal ones; confirm the signature predicts high vs low cross-property drift accordingly.\n8) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, two SAE variants (TopK, Matryoshka), and multiple target properties; report where the law is stable vs artifact and which property pairs are most tightly coupled.\nEVALUATION METRICS: off-target drift magnitude and direction-consistency (phenomenon); output-side-check-vs-drift correlation (blind-spot); predicted-vs-actual drift-vector cosine/R^2 and per-property Spearman (the law); forecaster precision@k and compute saved; controller target-gain-vs-collateral frontier area, held-fixed drift reduction, validity/novelty; positive-control recovery; property-coupling map.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) In-silico property oracles (ESMFold/DSSP, RDKit/SMARTS, biopython) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal, structurally-defined off-target drift and its predictability, not experimental function. 2) The property-Jacobian requires a differentiable surrogate for properties measured by non-differentiable evaluators (HMMER motifs, folding); we quantify surrogate fidelity and also report the purely-geometric decoder-overlap predictor which needs no surrogate. 3) Off-target drift could be dominated by generic validity collapse rather than structured coupling; we isolate the regime where objects stay valid and report structured drift separately, using random-direction controls. 4) The law may be property-pair- or model-specific rather than universal; we test multiple properties, model sizes, and SAE variants and report where it fails\u2014a negative/conditional result is still informative. 5) The orthogonalization fix may reduce on-target gain or interpretability; we quantify the tradeoff on the frontier rather than assume a free lunch. 6) SAE quality/layer choice could confound both drift and signature; controlled via multiple SAE variants, layers, difference-of-means baselines, and the positive control. 7) If drift turns out to be unpredictable noise, that is a clean, publishable negative result constraining theories of feature-dictionary geometry and warning against a-priori side-effect claims."
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'steering_sideeffect_law', 'Title': 'The Conservation of Trouble: Are Off-Target Property Drifts of Interpretable-Feature Steering Lawful and Predictable in Scientific Generative Models?', 'Short Hypothesis': "When an interpretable feature is steered to control one property of a generated protein or molecule, it induces off-target drift in OTHER, structurally-verifiable properties that is not random noise but LAWFUL: the drift vector across all properties is predicted, before any generation, by a fixed feature-specific 'side-effect signature' derived from the geometry of the SAE dictionary. This lets practitioners forecast and pre-empt collateral damage, choosing minimal-side-effect features and orthogonalized directions to hit a target property while holding others fixed.", 'Related Work': "Text-LLM steering studies extensively document off-target side effects and entanglement: SAE-Targeted Steering (Chalnev et al. 2024) minimizes side effects using SAE-measured effect vectors; Rogue Scalpel (2025), Steering Externalities (2026), SteeringSafety (2025), Steering Refusal with SAEs (2024), and Mind the Performance Gap (2026) all show steering degrades unrelated capabilities or safety in a method/model-dependent way. Crucially, all of these (a) operate in TEXT LLMs where the off-target 'property' is a fuzzy behavior or benchmark score, and (b) treat side effects as a nuisance to be measured post hoc or empirically minimized. Scientific-model steering work (InterPLM 2024, ProtSAE 2025, chemistry-LM SAEs) reports only ON-target property gains and evaluates validity as a side constraint, never characterizing structured off-target property drift. No prior work (i) transfers the off-target-drift phenomenon to scientific generative models where every off-target property (helix content, charge, logP, ring count, motif presence) is an OBJECTIVELY VERIFIABLE structural fact, (ii) tests whether the drift is LAWFUL and predictable a priori from a fixed feature side-effect signature rather than needing per-target measurement, or (iii) turns that law into a pre-flight side-effect forecaster and a minimal-collateral feature/direction selector. This distinguishes it from my prior compositionality proposal (which co-steers several INTENDED targets) by studying UNINTENDED drift on non-targeted properties and its a-priori predictability.", 'Abstract': "Steering interpretable features to control a target property of a generated protein or molecule is the standard controllable-design paradigm, and it is judged almost entirely by whether the target property moves. In text language models, steering is known to cause unintended off-target effects, but there the 'off-target property' is a fuzzy behavior and the effect is treated as a post-hoc nuisance. We transfer this phenomenon to scientific generative models, where every off-target property is an objectively verifiable structural fact, and ask a sharper question: is the collateral drift lawful and predictable BEFORE any generation? We hypothesize that each interpretable feature carries a fixed 'side-effect signature'—the direction in property space it perturbs—computable from the SAE dictionary geometry (via decoder-direction overlaps and a per-feature property-Jacobian), so that steering toward property A produces a forecastable drift vector across properties B, C, D that no output-side check anticipates. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders, we (1) demonstrate that single-target steering induces substantial, structured off-target property drift measured by hard oracles (DSSP/ESMFold, biopython, RDKit/SMARTS); (2) test whether an intervention-free side-effect signature predicts each feature's actual drift vector across steering strengths (a 'steering side-effect law'); (3) validate the signature as a pre-flight forecaster that ranks features by expected collateral damage; and (4) deploy a minimal-collateral controller—selecting low-side-effect features or orthogonalizing the steering direction against high-drift property axes—to hit the target while holding others fixed, tracing a target-gain-versus-collateral frontier. Results would recast off-target drift from an unpredictable hazard into a forecastable, controllable design quantity.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, PROPERTY ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK SAEs on the residual stream at a mid layer (chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic); verify reconstruction fidelity. Define a battery of measurable properties, each with a cheap HARD oracle so drift is an objective fact: proteins—alpha-helix content, beta-sheet content, GRAVY hydrophobicity, net charge, molecular weight, motif presence (HMMER/regex, e.g. zinc finger); molecules—logP, QED, ring/aromatic-ring count, molecular weight, TPSA, functional-group SMARTS. Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + synthetic accessibility for molecules.\n2) FEATURE BANK: Identify interpretable, property-linked SAE features via concept-correlation/probe AUROC on held-out natural objects (features whose intended TARGET is one property in the battery). Include difference-of-means directions as a non-SAE baseline. Assemble ~30-50 target features per domain.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON—STRUCTURED OFF-TARGET DRIFT: For each target feature, sweep steering strength and GENERATE N objects per level. For each level, measure the FULL property vector (all battery oracles), not just the target. Compute the off-target drift vector = change in every non-target property. Quantify: (a) collateral magnitude (norm of off-target drift relative to on-target gain), (b) direction consistency of the drift across strengths and seeds (is it a stable signature or noise?), and (c) that off-target drift is INVISIBLE to output-side checks by correlating validity/pseudo-perplexity/RDKit-validity with true off-target drift (hypot