rlap) and two known-orthogonal ones; confirm the signature predicts high vs low cross-property drift accordingly.\n8) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, two SAE variants (TopK, Matryoshka), and multiple target properties; report where the law is stable vs artifact and which property pairs are most tightly coupled.\nEVALUATION METRICS: off-target drift magnitude and direction-consistency (phenomenon); output-side-check-vs-drift correlation (blind-spot); predicted-vs-actual drift-vector cosine/R^2 and per-property Spearman (the law); forecaster precision@k and compute saved; controller target-gain-vs-collateral frontier area, held-fixed drift reduction, validity/novelty; positive-control recovery; property-coupling map.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) In-silico property oracles (ESMFold/DSSP, RDKit/SMARTS, biopython) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal, structurally-defined off-target drift and its predictability, not experimental function. 2) The property-Jacobian requires a differentiable surrogate for properties measured by non-differentiable evaluators (HMMER motifs, folding); we quantify surrogate fidelity and also report the purely-geometric decoder-overlap predictor which needs no surrogate. 3) Off-target drift could be dominated by generic validity collapse rather than structured coupling; we isolate the regime where objects stay valid and report structured drift separately, using random-direction controls. 4) The law may be property-pair- or model-specific rather than universal; we test multiple properties, model sizes, and SAE variants and report where it fails—a negative/conditional result is still informative. 5) The orthogonalization fix may reduce on-target gain or interpretability; we quantify the tradeoff on the frontier rather than assume a free lunch. 6) SAE quality/layer choice could confound both drift and signature; controlled via multiple SAE variants, layers, difference-of-means baselines, and