Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "relational_feature_steering",
  "Title": "Intrinsic Is Easy, Relational Is Hard: Do Interpretable Features Give Causal Control Over a Protein's Binding Context, or Only Its Standalone Properties?",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Interpretable features of protein language models afford causal, steerable control over a protein's INTRINSIC (partner-independent) properties (fold, hydrophobicity, motifs), but there is NO comparably compact, causally-steerable feature for RELATIONAL properties\u2014the context-dependent fact that a designed protein binds a SPECIFIED partner. We hypothesize relational control is either absent, entangled with intrinsic features, or requires partner-conditioned representations, so steering toward 'binds partner X' silently degrades to steering toward generic bindability while ignoring specificity\u2014an unrecognized blind spot in interpretability-guided binder design.",
  "Related Work": "All interpretability-for-generation work on protein language models\u2014InterPLM (2024), ProtSAE (2025), Steering Protein Language Models/ASPO (2025), the antibody-SAE paper (2025), Automated Neuron Labelling (2025), and CB-pLM (2024)\u2014discovers and steers features encoding INTRINSIC, single-protein properties (secondary structure, GRAVY, molecular weight, zinc-finger/binding-site motifs) and evaluates whether that standalone property appears. Separately, a large partner-aware PPI literature (BIPSPI 2018; D-SCRIPT 2021; ReCLIP 2026; partner-specific interface prediction 2011) establishes that binding is intrinsically RELATIONAL\u2014the same residue's behavior depends on the partner\u2014but this line is purely PREDICTIVE and never touches the internal features of a GENERATIVE model or their steerability. No prior work asks whether a protein generative model represents relational, partner-conditioned properties as causally-steerable features, contrasts the steerability of intrinsic vs relational properties, or exposes that binder-design steering toward a NAMED partner may collapse to generic bindability. This proposal is the first to introduce the intrinsic-vs-relational axis into interpretability-of-generation and to audit partner-specific controllability\u2014distinct from all prior single-property steering and from all predictive PPI work.",
  "Abstract": "Interpretability-guided protein design has shown that features of protein language models (PLMs) can steer generation toward desired properties\u2014but every demonstrated property is INTRINSIC to a single protein (its fold, hydrophobicity, or a local motif). Real binder design demands RELATIONAL control: producing a protein that binds a SPECIFIED partner, a fact that is meaningless without the partner and known from decades of PPI research to be irreducibly context-dependent. We ask whether interpretable features give causal control over this relational axis or only over standalone properties. We hypothesize an intrinsic-easy/relational-hard asymmetry: compact steerable features exist for intrinsic properties but not for partner-specific binding, so steering a binder toward 'binds partner X' silently collapses to generic bindability, ignoring specificity. Using ESM-2 and an antibody PLM (p-IgGen) with sparse autoencoders, we (1) LOCATE candidate intrinsic-property features and candidate relational (partner-binding) features via probing, including probing partner-CONDITIONED activations where the target sequence is prepended; (2) CAUSALLY test whether steering each yields the property in finished designs\u2014for relational features, whether it produces a protein that binds the INTENDED partner (specificity) rather than any partner (promiscuity), scored by in-silico complex confidence (AlphaFold-Multimer/ESMFold interface metrics) and specificity panels against decoy partners; (3) INTERPRET whether located relational units encode true partner-specificity or generic interface-propensity; and (4) AUDIT partner-specific design reliability, measuring how often steered binders bind the wrong/any partner while validity and generic-bindability checks pass. We predict intrinsic control succeeds, relational control fails or requires partner conditioning, and standard steering evaluations mask this\u2014reframing relational controllability as a necessary, currently-ignored axis for interpretable binder design.",
  "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and an antibody PLM (p-IgGen) with TopK (and Ordered, for steerability) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. INTRINSIC-property oracles (cheap): alpha-helix/beta-sheet content (DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY, molecular weight, motifs (HMMER/regex). RELATIONAL oracle: binding to a SPECIFIED partner scored by in-silico complex prediction\u2014AlphaFold-Multimer / ESMFold-in-complex interface pLDDT/ipTM and interface contact count\u2014plus a SPECIFICITY panel: predicted binding of the SAME designed protein against a set of DECOY partners (specificity = intended-partner score minus best-decoy score). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity.\n2) LOCATION (intrinsic vs relational): Probe SAE features/residual activations for (a) intrinsic properties on single sequences, and (b) partner-binding using a partner-CONDITIONED input format (prepend/concatenate the target partner sequence and probe activations on the binder residues, mirroring ReCLIP-style residue-conditioning). Report probe AUROC and identify top intrinsic and top relational candidate features/directions. Compare partner-conditioned vs partner-agnostic probing to test whether relational information is even present without the partner in context.\n3) CORE CAUSAL TEST (intrinsic-easy/relational-hard, the phenomenon): For intrinsic features, steer and measure standard property gain vs validity budget (calibrate baseline). For relational features, steer generation toward 'binds partner X' and measure, on FINISHED designs: (i) intended-partner binding score, and critically (ii) SPECIFICITY = intended minus best-decoy. Primary metric: the gap between intrinsic steerability (high property gain, high specificity-of-effect) and relational steerability, and specifically the PROMISCUITY COLLAPSE = fraction of steered binders whose binding score rises for the intended AND decoy partners alike (generic bindability, zero specificity). Compare SAE-feature steering, difference-of-means (binders-of-X minus non-binders) directions, partner-conditioned steering, random-direction and matched-intrinsic-feature controls; report sign, dose-response, specificity.\n4) UNIT INTERPRETATION (what relational units mean): Test whether located relational features encode true partner-specificity or generic interface-propensity, using controlled pairs\u2014same binder scored against multiple partners, and different binders of the same partner. A feature that fires identically regardless of the partner is generic bindability, not relational specificity. Auto-label top features (max-activating contexts).\n5) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): For a battery of target partners, request binders via steering and measure the SILENT WRONG-PARTNER RATE = fraction of valid, generically-bindable designs that bind a decoy at least as well as the intended partner. Test whether output-side signals (validity, generic interface score, generation likelihood) detect this\u2014hypothesis: near-chance\u2014while the internal partner-conditioned specificity probe flags it, yielding a pre-flight specificity filter (report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors, and compute saved by flagging non-specific designs before expensive complex prediction).\n6) POSITIVE CONTROL & GENERALIZATION: Antibody CDR design against specified antigen epitopes (p-IgGen) as a second relational setting; a partner-conditioned architecture variant (partner prepended) to test whether relational steerability APPEARS when the partner is in context. Robustness across two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, and two SAE layers.\nEVALUATION METRICS: intrinsic-vs-relational steerability gap (primary); PROMISCUITY COLLAPSE rate and specificity (intended-minus-best-decoy); feature partner-invariance (interpretation); SILENT WRONG-PARTNER RATE and internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE (auditing); partner-conditioned vs partner-agnostic probe AUROC; validity/novelty; dose-response and control specificity.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The relational claim is partly an ABSENCE claim (no clean steerable partner-specificity feature); we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (SAE features, difference-of-means, partner-conditioned steering), sweeping strength, and calibrating that intrinsic control DOES succeed in the same pipeline, so a null relational result is meaningful rather than a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable partner-specific feature) is equally publishable and valuable for binder design. 2) Binding is scored by in-silico complex predictors (AlphaFold-Multimer/ESMFold interface metrics), which are proxies for wet-lab affinity/specificity; claims concern model-internal, in-silico-verified partner-specific controllability, not experimental binding\u2014stated explicitly, and specificity uses relative (intended-vs-decoy) scores to reduce absolute-score bias. 3) Complex prediction is compute-heavier than single-sequence oracles; we cap partner and decoy panel sizes and use ESMFold-in-complex as a cheaper first-pass with AlphaFold-Multimer confirmation on a subset. 4) Relational information may simply be absent without the partner in context; the partner-conditioned probing/steering experiments are designed to detect exactly this and turn it into an informative finding about what representation relational control requires. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, difference-of-means baselines, and random-direction controls. 6) If relational steering unexpectedly succeeds with specificity, that overturns the hypothesis but yields a strong positive contribution."
}}
