Response Content: {"total": 9, "offset": 0, "next": 5, "data": [{"paperId": "3e0561acd6abe7b524acce325f38d3a7efe0d953", "title": "Directly optimizing for synthesizability in generative molecular design using retrosynthesis models", "venue": "Chemical Science", "year": 2024, "citationCount": 24, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1039/d5sc01476j", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12186, which is subject to the license by
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "synthesizability_blindspot_steering", "Title": "The Synthesizability Tax: Do Interpretable Features Reveal That Property-Steering Silently Pushes Generators Toward Unmakeable Designs?", "Short Hypothesis": "When an interpretable feature is steered to improve a target property of a generated molecule, generation silently drifts toward LESS synthesizable chemical space, and this 'synthesizability tax' is systematic rather than random. We further hypothesize that molecular generative models do NOT possess a compact, causally-steerable internal feature encoding synthesizability itself (unlike easily-steerable physicochemical properties), so the manufacturability of a design cannot be controlled the way logP or QED can\u2014making synthesizability an unrecognized interpretability blind spot for controllable design.", "Related Work": "Synthesizability is called 'the elephant in the lab' (Nature-adjacent opinion, 2025): generative models routinely propose molecules that cannot be made, and the field addresses this via post-hoc filtering (RAscore 2021, RetroGNN 2020), retrosynthesis-constrained generation (building-block assembly 2023, TANGO 2024, steerable synthesizability control 2025, SynTwins 2025), and direct retrosynthesis-in-the-loop optimization (2024). All of these are architecture/objective interventions treating synthesizability as an external constraint. Interpretability work on molecular generators\u2014SynFlowNet interpretability (2025), 'Circuits, Features, and Heuristics in Molecular Transformers' (2025), token-level KL importance (2025)\u2014shows physicochemical properties (polarity, lipophilicity, valence capacity) are linearly decodable and steerable, but NONE asks whether synthesizability is internally represented, whether it can be causally steered, or whether steering a DESIRED property incurs a hidden synthesizability cost. No prior work (a) quantifies a systematic property-vs-synthesizability tradeoff induced by interpretable-feature steering, (b) tests for the existence and causal steerability of an internal synthesizability feature, or (c) audits when a designer's property gain is silently paid for in unmakeability. This distinguishes the proposal from all constraint-based synthesizability work (which changes the model) and from all my prior steering proposals (which studied property control, memorization, provenance, chirality, symmetry, binding\u2014never manufacturability).", "Abstract": "Generative molecular design has a notorious blind spot: models propose molecules that cannot be synthesized, the 'elephant in the lab.' The field fights this with retrosynthesis filters and synthesis-constrained generation, but no one has asked what happens to synthesizability when practitioners use the increasingly popular interpretable-feature steering to hit a target property. We surface and explain a behavioral phenomenon we call the synthesizability tax: steering an interpretable feature to improve a desired property (logP, QED, docking, a functional group) systematically drags generation into less synthesizable chemical space, and standard property-focused evaluations never see it. We then ask a mechanistic question: is synthesizability itself internally represented as a compact, causally-steerable feature\u2014the way physicochemical properties demonstrably are\u2014or is it an emergent, non-steerable global property that models cannot control? Using autoregressive SMILES chemical language models with sparse autoencoders and cheap synthesizability oracles (RAscore, SAscore, and a retrosynthesis-planner check on a subset), we (1) quantify the synthesizability tax across many property-steering features and its dose-response; (2) probe for and causally test an internal synthesizability direction (does steering it raise synthesizability while holding the graph's property fixed?); (3) audit per-design reliability, measuring how often a property gain is silently paid for by crossing a synthesizability cliff that validity checks and property predictors miss; and (4) test a mitigation\u2014orthogonalizing property steering against the (weak) synthesizability direction. We predict a real, structured tax, a decodable-but-weakly-steerable synthesizability representation, and a cheap internal audit that flags unmakeable designs before expensive retrosynthesis.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Train (or use public) autoregressive SMILES chemical language models on ChEMBL/ZINC; train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, for steerability) SAEs on the residual stream at a mid layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. SYNTHESIZABILITY oracles (cheap, automated): SAscore and RAscore as primary continuous/binary synthesizability signals, corroborated on a random subset by an actual retrosynthesis planner (AiZynthFinder route-found / not-found) to validate the proxies. PROPERTY oracles (RDKit): logP, QED, ring/aromatic count, molecular weight, specific functional-group SMARTS. Validity gates: RDKit-parseable, valence-valid.\n2) FEATURE BANK: Identify interpretable property-linked SAE features via concept-correlation/probe AUROC on held-out molecules (the target design knobs). Include difference-of-means directions as a non-SAE baseline. Assemble ~30-50 property-steering features.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON\u2014THE SYNTHESIZABILITY TAX: For each property feature, sweep steering strength and generate N molecules per level. At each level measure (i) target property gain, (ii) synthesizability (SAscore/RAscore), and (iii) validity. Quantify the tax = synthesizability drop per unit property gain, its dose-response, and whether it is a stable, correctly-signed effect across features/seeds (mixed-effects model; random-direction and matched-non-property-feature controls). Show the BLIND SPOT: correlate validity and the property predictor's own confidence with true synthesizability drop (hypothesis: near-zero\u2014standard evals miss the tax). Report which property directions carry the largest tax.\n4) LOCATION + CAUSAL TEST\u2014IS SYNTHESIZABILITY A STEERABLE FEATURE? Probe SAE features/residual activations for synthesizability (RAscore/SAscore regression/classification) at generation-relevant token positions; report probe R^2/AUROC and identify top candidate synthesizability directions (Location). Then causally test (Causal Intervention): steer the candidate direction and measure whether finished molecules become MORE synthesizable while the target property and scaffold are held fixed\u2014the operational definition of a genuine synthesizability handle. Compare SAE-feature steering, difference-of-means (high-vs-low-SAscore) directions, sign, dose-response, and specificity vs random-direction controls. Hypothesis: synthesizability is decodable but only weakly/unspecifically steerable relative to physicochemical properties (contrast its steerability head-to-head with logP/QED features calibrated in the same pipeline).\n5) DECISION AUDITING\u2014per-design reliability (payoff): Define a synthesizability cliff (crossing from RAscore-synthesizable to not, or a large SAscore jump). Measure the SILENT UNMAKEABILITY RATE = fraction of property-steered, valid, property-satisfying designs that cross the cliff. Test whether an intervention-free INTERNAL synthesizability score (probe read off mid-generation / feature coherence) flags these earlier and better than output-side signals (validity, property-predictor confidence, generation likelihood): report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors and compute saved by aborting/flagging unmakeable designs before running the retrosynthesis planner.\n6) MITIGATION: Orthogonalize the property-steering direction against the located synthesizability direction (projection removal) and compare against naive steering on a property-gain-vs-synthesizability Pareto frontier: on-target gain, synthesizability retention, validity, novelty/diversity. Test whether even a weak synthesizability handle suffices to reduce the tax.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across SMILES vs SELFIES, two model sizes, two SAE variants (TopK, Ordered/Matryoshka), and multiple target properties; report where the tax and the (non-)steerability finding are stable vs artifact, and whether the tax tracks how far off-distribution the target property pushes generation.\nEVALUATION METRICS: synthesizability tax magnitude and dose-response (primary phenomenon); output-side-check-vs-tax correlation (blind spot); synthesizability probe R^2/AUROC (Location) and synthesizability-steering success/specificity vs property-steering baselines (Causal Intervention); SILENT UNMAKEABILITY RATE and internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE (auditing); mitigation frontier area and tax reduction; RAscore/SAscore-vs-retrosynthesis-planner agreement (proxy validation); cross-encoding/size/SAE robustness.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) SAscore/RAscore are in-silico proxies for true synthesizability; we validate them on a subset against an actual retrosynthesis planner (AiZynthFinder) and report proxy-vs-planner agreement, restricting strong claims to cases where they agree. Claims concern model-internal, proxy-verified manufacturability, not wet-lab synthesis. 2) The synthesizability-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching from high-synthesizability runs), sweeping strength, and head-to-head calibrating that PROPERTY features ARE steerable in the same pipeline\u2014so a null synthesizability-steering result is meaningful rather than a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable synthesizability handle) is equally publishable and directly useful for controllable synthesis-aware design. 3) The tax could be confounded with generic off-distribution drift rather than a property-specific effect; we use random-direction and matched-non-property-feature controls and test whether the tax tracks property-specific displacement. 4) SAE quality/layer choice could confound both Location and the tax; controlled with multiple SAE variants, layers, and difference-of-means baselines. 5) An internal synthesizability probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; even so, the interpretability diagnosis (decodable-but-not-steerable) and the audit/mitigation payoff are the contributions, reported honestly. 6) If no tax is found (property steering leaves synthesizability intact), that is a valuable positive result establishing that interpretable steering is synthesizability-safe."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'synthesizability_blindspot_steering', 'Title': 'The Synthesizability Tax: Do Interpretable Features Reveal That Property-Steering Silently Pushes Generators Toward Unmakeable Designs?', 'Short Hypothesis': "When an interpretable feature is steered to improve a target property of a generated molecule, generation silently drifts toward LESS synthesizable chemical space, and this 'synthesizability tax' is systematic rather than random. We further hypothesize that molecular generative models do NOT possess a compact, causally-steerable internal feature encoding synthesizability itself (unlike easily-steerable physicochemical properties), so the manufacturability of a design cannot be controlled the way logP or QED can—making synthesizability an unrecognized interpretability blind spot for controllable design.", 'Related Work': "Synthesizability is called 'the elephant in the lab' (Nature-adjacent opinion, 2025): generative models routinely propose molecules that cannot be made, and the field addresses this via post-hoc filtering (RAscore 2021, RetroGNN 2020), retrosynthesis-constrained generation (building-block assembly 2023, TANGO 2024, steerable synthesizability control 2025, SynTwins 2025), and direct retrosynthesis-in-the-loop optimization (2024). All of these are architecture/objective interventions treating synthesizability as an external constraint. Interpretability work on molecular generators—SynFlowNet interpretability (2025), 'Circuits, Features, and Heuristics in Molecular Transformers' (2025), token-level KL importance (2025)—shows physicochemical properties (polarity, lipophilicity, valence capacity) are linearly decodable and steerable, but NONE asks whether synthesizability is internally represented, whether it can be causally steered, or whether steering a DESIRED property incurs a hidden synthesizability cost. No prior work (a) quantifies a systematic property-vs-synthesizability tradeoff induced by interpretable-feature steering, (b) tests for the existence and causal steerability of an internal synthesizability feature, or (c) audits when a designer's property gain is silently paid for in unmakeability. This distinguishes the proposal from all constraint-based synthesizability work (which changes the model) and from all my prior steering proposals (which studied property control, memorization, provenance, chirality, symmetry, binding—never manufacturability).", 'Abstract': "Generative molecular design has a notorious blind spot: models propose molecules that cannot be synthesized, the 'elephant in the lab.' The field fights this with retrosynthesis filters and synthesis-constrained generation, but no one has asked what happens to synthesizability when practitioners use the increasingly popular interpretable-feature steering to hit a target property. We surface and explain a behavioral phenomenon we call the synthesizability tax: steering an interpretable feature to improve a desired property (logP, QED, docking, a functional group) systematically drags generation into less synthesizable chemical space, and standard property-focused evaluations never see it. We then ask a mechanistic question: is synthesizability itself internally represented as a compact, causally-steerable feature—the way physicochemical properties demonstrably are—or is it an emergent, non-steerable global property that models cannot control? Using autoregressive SMILES chemical language models with sparse autoencoders and cheap synthesizability oracles (RAscore, SAscore, and a retrosynthesis-planner check on a subset), we (1) quantify the synthesizability tax across many property-steering features and its dose-response; (2) probe for and causally test an internal synthesizability direction (does steering it raise synthesizability while holding the graph's property fixed?); (3) audit per-design reliability, measuring how often a property gain is silently paid for by crossing a synthesizability cliff that validity checks and property predictors miss; and (4) test a mitigation—orthogonalizing property steering against the (weak) synthesizability direction. We predict a real, structured tax, a decodable-but-weakly-steerable synthesizability representation, and a cheap internal audit that flags unmakeable designs before expensive retrosynthesis.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Train (or use public) autoregressive SMILES chemical language models on ChEMBL/ZINC; train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, for steerability) SAEs on the residual stream at a mid layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. SYNTHESIZABILITY oracles (cheap, automated): SAscore and RAscore as primary continuous/binary synthesizability signals, corroborated on a random subset by an actual retrosynthesis planner (AiZynthFinder route-found / not-found) to validate the proxies. PROPERTY oracles (RDKit): logP, QED, ring/aromatic count, molecular weight, specific functional-group SMARTS. Validity gates: RDKit-parseable, valence-valid.\n2) FEATURE BANK: Identify interpretable property-linked SAE features via concept-correlation/probe AUROC on held-out molecules (the target design knobs). Include difference-of-means directions as a non-SAE baseline. Assemble ~30-50 property-steering features.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON—THE SYNTHESIZABILITY TAX: For each property feature, sweep steering strength and generate N molecules per level. At each level measure (i) target property gain, (ii) synthesizability (SAscore/RAscore), and (iii) validity. Quantify the tax = synthesizability drop per unit property gain, its dose-response, and whether it is a stable, correctly-signed effect across features/seeds (mixed-effects model; random-direction and matched-non-property-feature controls). Show the BLIND SPOT: correlate validity and the property predictor's own confidence with true synthesizability drop (hypothesis: near-zero—standard evals miss the tax). Report which property directions carry the largest tax.\n4) LOCATION + CAUSAL TEST—IS SYNTHESIZABILITY A STEERABLE FEATURE? Probe SAE features/residual activations for synthesizability (RAscore/SAscore regression/classification) at generation-relevant token positions; report probe R^2/AUROC and identify top candidate synthesizability directions (Location). Then causally test (Causal Intervention): steer the candidate direction and measure whether finished molecules become MORE synthesizable while the target property and scaffold are held fixed—the operational definition of a genuine synthesizability handle. Compare SAE-feature steering, difference-of-means (high-vs-low-SAscore) directions, sign, dose-response, and specificity vs random-direction controls. Hypothesis: synthesizability is decodable but only weakly/unspecifically steerable relative to physicochemical properties (contrast its steerability head-to-head with logP/QED features calibrated in the same pipeline).\n5) DECISION AUDITING—per-design reliability (payoff): Define a synthesizability cliff (crossing from RAscore-synthesizable to not, or a large SAscore jump). Measure the SILENT UNMAKEABILITY RATE = fraction of property-steered, valid, property-satisfying designs that cross the cliff. Test whether an intervention-free INTERNAL synthesizability score (probe read off mid-generation / feature coherence) flags these earlier and better than output-side signals (validity, property-predictor confidence, generation likelihood): report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors and compute saved by aborting/flagging unmakeable designs before running the retrosynthesis planner.\n6) MITIGATION: Orthogonalize the property-steering direction against the located synthesizability direction (projection removal) and compare against naive steering on a property-gain-vs-synthesizability Pareto frontier: on-target gain, synthesizability retention, validity, novelty/diversity. Test whether even a weak synthesizability handle suffices to reduce the tax.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across SMILES vs SELFIES, two model sizes, two SAE variants (TopK, Ordered/Matryoshka), and multiple target properties; report where the tax and the (non-)steerability finding are stable vs artifact, and whether the tax tracks how far off-distribution the target property pushes generation.\nEVALUATION METRICS: synthesizability tax magnitude and dose-response (primary phenomenon); output-side-check-vs-tax correlation (blind spot); synthesizability probe R^2/AUROC (Location) and synthesizability-steering success/specificity vs property-steering baselines (Causal Intervention); SILENT UNMAKEABILITY RATE and internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE (auditing); mitigation frontier area and tax reduction; RAscore/SAscore-vs-retrosynthesis-planner agreement (proxy validation); cross-encoding/size/SAE robustness.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) SAscore/RAscore are in-silico proxies for true synthesizability; we validate them on a subset against an actual retrosynthesis planner (AiZynthFinder) and report proxy-vs-planner agreement, restricting strong claims to cases where they agree. Claims concern model-internal, proxy-verified manufacturability, not wet-lab synthesis. 2) The synthesizability-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching from high-synthesizability runs), sweeping strength, and head-to-head calibrating that PROPERTY features ARE steerable in the same pipeline—so a null synthesizability-steering result is meaningful rather than a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable synthesizability handle) is equally publishable and directly useful for controllable synthesis-aware design. 3) The tax could be confounded with generic off-distribution drift rather than a property-specific effect; we use random-direction and matched-non-property-feature controls and test whether the tax tracks property-specific displacement. 4) SAE quality/layer choice could confound both Location and the tax; controlled with multiple SAE variants, layers, and difference-of-means baselines. 5) An internal synthesizability probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; even so, the interpretability diagnosis (decodable-but-not-steerable) and the audit/mitigation payoff are the contributions, reported honestly. 6) If no tax is found (property steering leaves synthesizability intact), that is a valuable positive result establishing that interpretable steering is synthesizability-safe.'}

