Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "regulatory_grammar_syntax_steering", "Title": "Motifs Without Grammar: Do Genomic Language Models Causally Steer Cis-Regulatory Syntax, or Just Sprinkle Motifs?", "Short Hypothesis": "Genomic language models (Evo 2 / regLM-style) possess interpretable, causally-steerable features for the PRESENCE of transcription-factor motifs, but NOT for their SYNTAX \u2014 the spacing, orientation, and ordering constraints that actually make a cis-regulatory element functional. Consequently, feature-steering to 'add motif X' silently places motifs in syntactically invalid configurations that break predicted regulatory activity, an unrecognized blind spot: activity/validity checks that look only at motif content miss it, while an intervention-free internal syntax-consistency signal flags it.", "Related Work": "Genomic interpretability is active but confined to motif PRESENCE. Evo 2 SAE work (2025) and small gene-LM SAE work (2025) discover features for coding sequences, TF-binding motifs, and secondary structure, and show early single-property steering. regLM (2024) and DREAM (2024) design CREs with target activity/cell-type-specificity via autoregressive LMs + supervised scorers, and ARGMINN (2024) makes motif SYNTAX (spacing preferences) readable from a purpose-built architecture. Critically: (a) all generative interpretability work steers toward motif content or scalar activity and evaluates whether the motif/activity appears \u2014 none asks whether the model represents motif SYNTAX as a causal, steerable handle; (b) ARGMINN reads syntax from weights but is a bespoke predictive model, not an audit of whether a general genomic LM causally controls syntax during generation; (c) benchmark critiques (DART-Eval 2024; gLM representational-power 2024; Karollus 2022 showing models ignore distal enhancers) hint that gLMs may capture correlational content over causal grammar, but never test this at the level of feature-steering controllability. This proposal is the first to introduce the content-vs-syntax axis into interpretability-of-generation for regulatory DNA and to audit when adding a motif silently violates grammar, distinct from all prior single-property CRE steering.", "Abstract": "The function of a cis-regulatory element (promoter, enhancer) is determined not just by which transcription-factor (TF) motifs it contains, but by their SYNTAX: the spacing, orientation, and order that let bound proteins cooperate. Interpretability-guided DNA design increasingly steers genomic language model (gLM) features to insert desired motifs, and success is judged by whether the motif appears and predicted activity rises. We surface a behavioral phenomenon we call the syntax blind spot: steering an interpretable feature to add a motif reliably increases motif content but places motifs in syntactically incoherent configurations that predicted-activity models score as non-functional \u2014 and content-based checks never notice. We then ask a mechanistic question: do gLMs represent motif syntax as a compact, causally-steerable feature the way they represent motif presence, or is syntax an emergent, non-steerable property? Using an open genomic LM (Evo 2 / a regLM-style autoregressive gLM) with sparse autoencoders and cheap in-silico oracles (FIMO/HMMER motif calling for content; a supervised sequence-to-activity model such as Enformer/DREAM for function; spacing/orientation statistics for syntax), we (1) LOCATE candidate motif-presence and motif-syntax features via probing; (2) CAUSALLY test whether steering each controls the intended axis \u2014 for syntax, whether we can shift inter-motif spacing/orientation while holding motif content fixed; (3) quantify the syntax tax that content-steering incurs on predicted activity; and (4) AUDIT design reliability, testing whether an internal syntax-consistency signal flags broken designs better than motif-content and activity-confidence checks. We predict content is steerable, syntax is decodable-but-weakly-steerable, and a cheap internal audit catches silently non-functional CREs before wet-lab validation.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use an open genomic LM with accessible activations \u2014 Evo 2 (using its published biologically-relevant layer, e.g. layer 26) with BatchTopK SAEs, and/or a regLM-style autoregressive gLM trained on yeast promoters / human enhancers (tractable for an academic lab). Train TopK/BatchTopK SAEs on the residual stream; verify reconstruction. CONTENT oracle: TF-motif calling via FIMO/HMMER against JASPAR PWMs (which motifs present, at what positions). SYNTAX descriptors (computed from motif calls): inter-motif spacing distributions, relative orientation (strand), motif ordering, and helical-phasing periodicity. FUNCTION oracle: a supervised sequence-to-activity predictor (Enformer/DREAM/regLM's scorer) giving predicted regulatory activity / cell-type specificity. Validity gates: sequence composition sanity (GC content, no degenerate repeats), gLM pseudo-perplexity.\n2) FEATURE BANK & LOCATION: Identify interpretable features via concept-correlation on held-out sequences: (a) MOTIF-PRESENCE features (activate when a given TF motif occurs), and (b) candidate MOTIF-SYNTAX features (correlate with inter-motif spacing/orientation/phasing, controlling for which motifs are present). Report probe AUROC/R^2 for presence vs syntax and identify top candidate directions; compare against a raw-activation probe. Test whether syntax is even decodable independent of content.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON \u2014 THE SYNTAX TAX (content-easy/syntax-hard): For each presence feature, sweep steering strength and generate N sequences per level. Measure (i) motif-content gain (FIMO), (ii) predicted activity (function oracle), and (iii) syntax coherence (deviation of realized spacing/orientation from the natural functional distribution for that motif combination). Quantify the syntax tax = activity drop per unit content gain and its dose-response, plus the SILENT-BREAKAGE RATE = fraction of content-satisfying, composition-valid sequences whose predicted activity is non-functional due to bad syntax. Show the blind spot: correlate content-satisfaction and pseudo-perplexity with true activity (hypothesis: content passes while activity fails). Random-direction and matched-non-motif-feature controls.\n4) CAUSAL SYNTAX TEST: Steer candidate syntax features and activation-patch from sequences with correct syntax; measure whether we can shift inter-motif SPACING/ORIENTATION toward a target while HOLDING motif content fixed \u2014 the operational definition of a syntax handle. Compare SAE-feature steering, difference-of-means (functional-vs-scrambled-syntax) directions, and patching; report sign, dose-response, specificity. Head-to-head calibrate against presence-feature steerability in the same pipeline. Hypothesis: syntax is decodable but far less steerable than content.\n5) INTERPRETATION: Test whether located syntax units encode true grammar (invariant to motif identity, tracking spacing/phasing) vs shallow local composition, using controlled sequence pairs that dissociate spacing from content (same motifs, functional vs scrambled spacing).\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal SYNTAX-CONSISTENCY score (syntax-probe confidence / feature coherence read mid-generation). Measure whether it flags silently-broken designs (content-OK, activity-broken) better than motif-content checks and the function oracle's own confidence: AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors, and compute/wet-lab-screening saved by flagging non-functional CREs before full activity prediction. Emit human-readable diagnoses ('motif present but spaced 4 bp off functional phasing').\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across Evo 2 vs regLM-style model, promoter vs enhancer tasks, yeast vs human, two SAE variants; report where the tax and (non-)steerability findings are stable vs artifact and whether the tax tracks how far off-distribution the requested motif combination is.\nEVALUATION METRICS: syntax tax magnitude & dose-response and SILENT-BREAKAGE RATE (primary phenomenon); content-check-vs-activity correlation (blind spot); presence vs syntax probe AUROC/R^2 (Location); syntax-steering success/specificity vs presence-steering (Causal Intervention); syntax-feature invariance to motif identity (Interpretation); internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE and screening saved (auditing); robustness across models/tasks/species.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Both function and syntax are assessed by in-silico oracles (Enformer/DREAM/regLM scorers, FIMO/JASPAR); these are proxies for wet-lab regulatory activity, so claims concern model-internal, in-silico-verified syntactic controllability, not experimental function \u2014 stated explicitly. Sequence-to-activity models are known to capture promoters better than distal enhancers (Karollus 2022), so we focus core claims on promoter/proximal-enhancer regimes where the oracle is most reliable and report enhancer results separately. 2) The syntax-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching), sweeping strength, and head-to-head calibrating that PRESENCE features ARE steerable in the same pipeline \u2014 so a null syntax result is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable syntax handle) is equally publishable and valuable for grammar-aware CRE design. 3) Content and syntax may be irreducibly entangled for some motif combinations (identity implies canonical spacing); the controlled dissociation pairs (step 5) are designed to separate them and we report where separation is impossible. 4) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with multiple SAE variants, the Evo 2 published biologically-relevant layer, raw-activation probes, and random-direction controls. 5) An internal syntax probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (content-decodable, syntax-weakly-steerable) and the audit payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 6) If no syntax tax is found (content steering preserves grammar and activity), that is a valuable positive result establishing that gLM feature-steering is grammar-safe for regulatory DNA design."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'regulatory_grammar_syntax_steering', 'Title': 'Motifs Without Grammar: Do Genomic Language Models Causally Steer Cis-Regulatory Syntax, or Just Sprinkle Motifs?', 'Short Hypothesis': "Genomic language models (Evo 2 / regLM-style) possess interpretable, causally-steerable features for the PRESENCE of transcription-factor motifs, but NOT for their SYNTAX — the spacing, orientation, and ordering constraints that actually make a cis-regulatory element functional. Consequently, feature-steering to 'add motif X' silently places motifs in syntactically invalid configurations that break predicted regulatory activity, an unrecognized blind spot: activity/validity checks that look only at motif content miss it, while an intervention-free internal syntax-consistency signal flags it.", 'Related Work': 'Genomic interpretability is active but confined to motif PRESENCE. Evo 2 SAE work (2025) and small gene-LM SAE work (2025) discover features for coding sequences, TF-binding motifs, and secondary structure, and show early single-property steering. regLM (2024) and DREAM (2024) design CREs with target activity/cell-type-specificity via autoregressive LMs + supervised scorers, and ARGMINN (2024) makes motif SYNTAX (spacing preferences) readable from a purpose-built architecture. Critically: (a) all generative interpretability work steers toward motif content or scalar activity and evaluates whether the motif/activity appears — none asks whether the model represents motif SYNTAX as a causal, steerable handle; (b) ARGMINN reads syntax from weights but is a bespoke predictive model, not an audit of whether a general genomic LM causally controls syntax during generation; (c) benchmark critiques (DART-Eval 2024; gLM representational-power 2024; Karollus 2022 showing models ignore distal enhancers) hint that gLMs may capture correlational content over causal grammar, but never test this at the level of feature-steering controllability. This proposal is the first to introduce the content-vs-syntax axis into interpretability-of-generation for regulatory DNA and to audit when adding a motif silently violates grammar, distinct from all prior single-property CRE steering.', 'Abstract': 'The function of a cis-regulatory element (promoter, enhancer) is determined not just by which transcription-factor (TF) motifs it contains, but by their SYNTAX: the spacing, orientation, and order that let bound proteins cooperate. Interpretability-guided DNA design increasingly steers genomic language model (gLM) features to insert desired motifs, and success is judged by whether the motif appears and predicted activity rises. We surface a behavioral phenomenon we call the syntax blind spot: steering an interpretable feature to add a motif reliably increases motif content but places motifs in syntactically incoherent configurations that predicted-activity models score as non-functional — and content-based checks never notice. We then ask a mechanistic question: do gLMs represent motif syntax as a compact, causally-steerable feature the way they represent motif presence, or is syntax an emergent, non-steerable property? Using an open genomic LM (Evo 2 / a regLM-style autoregressive gLM) with sparse autoencoders and cheap in-silico oracles (FIMO/HMMER motif calling for content; a supervised sequence-to-activity model such as Enformer/DREAM for function; spacing/orientation statistics for syntax), we (1) LOCATE candidate motif-presence and motif-syntax features via probing; (2) CAUSALLY test whether steering each controls the intended axis — for syntax, whether we can shift inter-motif spacing/orientation while holding motif content fixed; (3) quantify the syntax tax that content-steering incurs on predicted activity; and (4) AUDIT design reliability, testing whether an internal syntax-consistency signal flags broken designs better than motif-content and activity-confidence checks. We predict content is steerable, syntax is decodable-but-weakly-steerable, and a cheap internal audit catches silently non-functional CREs before wet-lab validation.', 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use an open genomic LM with accessible activations — Evo 2 (using its published biologically-relevant layer, e.g. layer 26) with BatchTopK SAEs, and/or a regLM-style autoregressive gLM trained on yeast promoters / human enhancers (tractable for an academic lab). Train TopK/BatchTopK SAEs on the residual stream; verify reconstruction. CONTENT oracle: TF-motif calling via FIMO/HMMER against JASPAR PWMs (which motifs present, at what positions). SYNTAX descriptors (computed from motif calls): inter-motif spacing distributions, relative orientation (strand), motif ordering, and helical-phasing periodicity. FUNCTION oracle: a supervised sequence-to-activity predictor (Enformer/DREAM/regLM's scorer) giving predicted regulatory activity / cell-type specificity. Validity gates: sequence composition sanity (GC content, no degenerate repeats), gLM pseudo-perplexity.\n2) FEATURE BANK & LOCATION: Identify interpretable features via concept-correlation on held-out sequences: (a) MOTIF-PRESENCE features (activate when a given TF motif occurs), and (b) candidate MOTIF-SYNTAX features (correlate with inter-motif spacing/orientation/phasing, controlling for which motifs are present). Report probe AUROC/R^2 for presence vs syntax and identify top candidate directions; compare against a raw-activation probe. Test whether syntax is even decodable independent of content.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON — THE SYNTAX TAX (content-easy/syntax-hard): For each presence feature, sweep steering strength and generate N sequences per level. Measure (i) motif-content gain (FIMO), (ii) predicted activity (function oracle), and (iii) syntax coherence (deviation of realized spacing/orientation from the natural functional distribution for that motif combination). Quantify the syntax tax = activity drop per unit content gain and its dose-response, plus the SILENT-BREAKAGE RATE = fraction of content-satisfying, composition-valid sequences whose predicted activity is non-functional due to bad syntax. Show the blind spot: correlate content-satisfaction and pseudo-perplexity with true activity (hypothesis: content passes while activity fails). Random-direction and matched-non-motif-feature controls.\n4) CAUSAL SYNTAX TEST: Steer candidate syntax features and activation-patch from sequences with correct syntax; measure whether we can shift inter-motif SPACING/ORIENTATION toward a target while HOLDING motif content fixed — the operational definition of a syntax handle. Compare SAE-feature steering, difference-of-means (functional-vs-scrambled-syntax) directions, and patching; report sign, dose-response, specificity. Head-to-head calibrate against presence-feature steerability in the same pipeline. Hypothesis: syntax is decodable but far less steerable than content.\n5) INTERPRETATION: Test whether located syntax units encode true grammar (invariant to motif identity, tracking spacing/phasing) vs shallow local composition, using controlled sequence pairs that dissociate spacing from content (same motifs, functional vs scrambled spacing).\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal SYNTAX-CONSISTENCY score (syntax-probe confidence / feature coherence read mid-generation). Measure whether it flags silently-broken designs (content-OK, activity-broken) better than motif-content checks and the function oracle's own confidence: AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors, and compute/wet-lab-screening saved by flagging non-functional CREs before full activity prediction. Emit human-readable diagnoses ('motif present but spaced 4 bp off functional phasing').\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across Evo 2 vs regLM-style model, promoter vs enhancer tasks, yeast vs human, two SAE variants; report where the tax and (non-)steerability findings are stable vs artifact and whether the tax tracks how far off-distribution the requested motif combination is.\nEVALUATION METRICS: syntax tax magnitude & dose-response and SILENT-BREAKAGE RATE (primary phenomenon); content-check-vs-activity correlation (blind spot); presence vs syntax probe AUROC/R^2 (Location); syntax-steering success/specificity vs presence-steering (Causal Intervention); syntax-feature invariance to motif identity (Interpretation); internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE and screening saved (auditing); robustness across models/tasks/species.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) Both function and syntax are assessed by in-silico oracles (Enformer/DREAM/regLM scorers, FIMO/JASPAR); these are proxies for wet-lab regulatory activity, so claims concern model-internal, in-silico-verified syntactic controllability, not experimental function — stated explicitly. Sequence-to-activity models are known to capture promoters better than distal enhancers (Karollus 2022), so we focus core claims on promoter/proximal-enhancer regimes where the oracle is most reliable and report enhancer results separately. 2) The syntax-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching), sweeping strength, and head-to-head calibrating that PRESENCE features ARE steerable in the same pipeline — so a null syntax result is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable syntax handle) is equally publishable and valuable for grammar-aware CRE design. 3) Content and syntax may be irreducibly entangled for some motif combinations (identity implies canonical spacing); the controlled dissociation pairs (step 5) are designed to separate them and we report where separation is impossible. 4) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with multiple SAE variants, the Evo 2 published biologically-relevant layer, raw-activation probes, and random-direction controls. 5) An internal syntax probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (content-decodable, syntax-weakly-steerable) and the audit payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 6) If no syntax tax is found (content steering preserves grammar and activity), that is a valuable positive result establishing that gLM feature-steering is grammar-safe for regulatory DNA design.'}

