Response Content: {"total": 10, "offset": 0, "next": 5, "data": [{"paperId": "7ae60e35cbdcb8a82242f0a02f9f46580903a30d", "title": "Drift-diffusion (DrDiff) framework determines kinetics and thermodynamics of two-state folding trajectory and tunes diffusion models.", "venue": "Journal of Chemical Physics", "year": 2019, "citationCount": 13, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": "CLOSED", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1063/1.5113499?email=<IN
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "kinetic_thermodynamic_steering_gap", "Title": "Steer the Endpoint, Not the Path: Do Interpretable Features Control Thermodynamic but Not Kinetic Properties of Generated Molecules?", "Short Hypothesis": "Interpretable sparse-autoencoder features of molecular/protein sequence and graph generative models afford causal, steerable control over THERMODYNAMIC/equilibrium properties (stability, binding affinity, solubility, fold free energy) but NOT over KINETIC properties (reaction/transition-state barriers, folding rates, metastability, dissociation kinetics), because kinetics depends on pathways and barriers that are absent from the static endpoint-only training signal. Consequently, steering a feature toward a desired kinetic target silently changes the thermodynamic endpoint instead, a 'kinetics blind spot' that equilibrium-property checks miss but an internal kinetic-consistency signal flags.", "Related Work": "Two threads meet for the first time. (1) 'Physics Steering' (2025) shows a PHYSICS foundation model (trained directly on simulation trajectories) has causally-steerable directions for dynamical concepts like diffusion speed and temporal progression; Latent Thermodynamic Flows (2025), Thermodynamic Interpolation (2025), and tLaSDI (2024) build generative models that capture temperature/kinetics but do so by INJECTING thermodynamic structure or training on dynamics data. (2) Molecular/protein interpretability-of-generation (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; chemistry-LM SAEs 2025; Circuits/Features/Heuristics in Molecular Transformers 2025) discovers and steers features for endpoint/equilibrium properties and never touches kinetics. Crucially, the physics-FM result raises the sharp question: does a generator trained ONLY on static molecules/sequences (no trajectories, no barriers) also learn steerable dynamical/kinetic handles, or is dynamical controllability contingent on dynamics-bearing training data? No prior work (a) contrasts kinetic vs thermodynamic steerability in molecular/protein generators, (b) surfaces the kinetics-blind-spot phenomenon where kinetic steering collapses to endpoint change, or (c) audits when a generator's kinetic-property claim is trustworthy. This introduces the endpoint-vs-pathway axis into interpretability-of-generation, distinct from all my prior proposals (folding fold, symmetry, conservation, synthesizability, syntax) which concern static structural facts, not dynamical/pathway properties.", "Abstract": "A molecule's usefulness often depends on kinetics, not just thermodynamics: a metastable drug polymorph, a slow-off-rate binder, a fast-folding protein, or a catalyst with a low transition-state barrier are defined by PATHWAYS and BARRIERS, not by equilibrium endpoints. Generative models for molecules and proteins are trained almost entirely on static endpoints, yet interpretability-guided design increasingly steers their features to hit target properties. We ask whether these interpretable features can causally control kinetic properties at all, or only thermodynamic ones. Recent work shows a physics foundation model trained on simulation trajectories has steerable dynamical directions; we test the complementary hypothesis that generators trained WITHOUT dynamics lack a causal kinetic handle. We surface a behavioral phenomenon we call the kinetics blind spot: steering a feature toward a desired kinetic target (a higher folding rate, a lower reaction barrier, a longer-lived metastable state) reliably shifts the associated thermodynamic ENDPOINT while leaving the kinetic property uncontrolled, a failure invisible to equilibrium-property and validity checks. Using protein language models (ESM-2) and a SMILES chemical LM with sparse autoencoders, plus cheap kinetic oracles (folding-rate/contact-order proxies, transition-state barrier estimates from ML interatomic potentials, mean-first-passage-time from lightweight Langevin dynamics), we (1) LOCATE candidate thermodynamic and kinetic features via probing; (2) CAUSALLY test the steerability of each, contrasting endpoint control against pathway control; (3) quantify the blind spot; and (4) AUDIT reliability, testing whether an internal kinetic-consistency signal flags silently-uncontrolled designs better than equilibrium checks. We predict thermodynamic control succeeds, kinetic control is decodable-but-weakly-steerable, and a cheap internal audit catches kinetics blind spots before expensive simulation.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, for steerability, following the antibody-SAE decodable-but-inert finding) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. THERMODYNAMIC oracles (endpoint/equilibrium): proteins - fold stability (FoldX/Rosetta-style dG or a fine-tuned stability head), GRAVY, secondary-structure content (DSSP on ESMFold); molecules - logP, QED, formation free energy proxy, solubility proxy. KINETIC oracles (pathway/barrier): proteins - folding-rate proxy from absolute contact order (established rate predictor) and a native-state ruggedness/metastability proxy; molecules - reaction/transition-state barrier estimates via a pretrained ML interatomic potential (e.g., a NEB-lite/climbing-image estimate on a fixed reaction template) and mean-first-passage-time (MFPT) from short Langevin dynamics on a coarse energy surface; conformational metastability (number/depth of local minima). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + synthetic accessibility for molecules. First establish that the models natively produce objects spanning a meaningful kinetic RANGE so absence-of-control claims are meaningful.\n2) FEATURE BANK & LOCATION: Identify interpretable features via concept-correlation/probe AUROC/R^2 on held-out objects: (a) THERMODYNAMIC features (correlate with stability/logP/free-energy endpoints), and (b) candidate KINETIC features (correlate with folding rate / barrier / MFPT / metastability, controlling for the thermodynamic endpoint via partial correlation). Report probe performance for thermodynamic vs kinetic; identify top candidate directions; compare against a raw-activation probe; critically test whether kinetic properties are decodable AT ALL independent of endpoints.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON - THE KINETICS BLIND SPOT (endpoint-easy/pathway-hard): For each candidate KINETIC feature, sweep steering strength and generate N objects per level. At each level measure (i) the kinetic-property gain (folding rate / barrier / MFPT), (ii) the associated thermodynamic endpoint change, and (iii) validity. Quantify the blind spot = fraction of kinetic steering displacement that lands in the thermodynamic-endpoint axis rather than the kinetic axis, the SILENT-KINETIC-FAILURE RATE = fraction of 'kinetically-steered', valid objects whose kinetic property did NOT move (or moved wrong-sign) while an endpoint changed, and dose-response. Show the blind spot: correlate endpoint-property change and pseudo-perplexity with true kinetic change (hypothesis: kinetic steering is confounded by endpoint, standard checks miss it). Random-direction and matched-thermodynamic-feature controls.\n4) HEAD-TO-HEAD CAUSAL TEST: In the SAME pipeline, calibrate that THERMODYNAMIC features ARE steerable (endpoint gain per unit steer, specificity), then test whether ANY compact intervention (SAE feature, difference-of-means between fast/slow-kinetics objects, activation patching from high-kinetic-target objects, and orthogonalized-against-endpoint directions) can move the kinetic property while HOLDING the thermodynamic endpoint fixed - the operational definition of a genuine kinetic handle. Report sign, dose-response, specificity; contrast kinetic vs thermodynamic steerability effect sizes. Test whether Ordered/Matryoshka SAEs recover partial kinetic steerability.\n5) INTERPRETATION: Test whether located 'kinetic' units encode a true pathway/barrier concept (invariant to the thermodynamic endpoint, tracking barrier/rate) vs shallow endpoint proxies, using controlled object pairs that dissociate kinetics from thermodynamics (matched stability/logP endpoints but different folding rates / barriers, from the oracle-labeled corpus). Auto-label top features via max-activating contexts.\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal KINETIC-CONSISTENCY score (kinetic-probe confidence / feature coherence read mid-generation). Measure whether it flags silently-uncontrolled kinetic designs better than output-side signals (endpoint-property, validity, model likelihood): report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors, and compute saved by flagging non-controllable requests before running the expensive kinetic simulation (Langevin/NEB). Emit human-readable diagnoses ('barrier request ignored; steering only lowered fold dG').\n7) ROBUSTNESS & THE PHYSICS-FM CONTRAST: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two layers. As a conceptual control, discuss/compare against the Physics-Steering finding that a trajectory-trained physics FM DOES steer dynamical concepts - framing the result as: dynamical controllability is contingent on dynamics-bearing training data, absent in static endpoint-trained molecular/protein generators. Test whether kinetic steerability improves with model scale or with any training-set kinetic/dynamical annotation density (linking capability to training signal).\nEVALUATION METRICS: kinetics-blind-spot magnitude & dose-response and SILENT-KINETIC-FAILURE RATE (primary phenomenon); endpoint-vs-kinetic-change correlation (blind spot); thermodynamic vs kinetic probe AUROC/R^2 (Location); kinetic-steering success/specificity vs thermodynamic-steering, head-to-head effect sizes (Causal Intervention); kinetic-feature invariance to endpoint (Interpretation); internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE and simulation compute saved (auditing); scale/training-signal trends.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Kinetic properties are assessed by in-silico proxies (contact-order folding-rate predictors, ML-interatomic-potential barrier estimates, short-Langevin MFPT), which are approximations of true kinetics and far cheaper than full MD; we validate proxies against each other and against literature rate data on a subset, and restrict strong claims to cases where proxies agree. Claims concern model-internal, proxy-verified kinetic controllability, not experimental rates - stated explicitly. 2) The kinetic-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching, endpoint-orthogonalized directions), sweeping strength, requiring the model to natively span a kinetic range, and head-to-head calibrating that THERMODYNAMIC features ARE steerable in the same pipeline - so a null kinetic result is meaningful rather than a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable kinetic handle in a static-trained generator) is equally publishable and surprising. 3) Kinetics and thermodynamics are partly coupled (Bell-Evans-style barrier-stability correlations); the partial-correlation feature selection and endpoint-matched dissociation pairs (step 5) are designed to isolate genuinely endpoint-independent kinetic signal, and we report where separation is impossible. 4) Kinetic oracles (NEB/Langevin) are compute-heavier than endpoint oracles; we cap simulation on a subset and use the internal audit precisely to reduce this cost. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, raw-activation probes, and random-direction controls. 6) An internal kinetic probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (thermodynamics-decodable-and-steerable, kinetics-decodable-but-weakly-steerable) and the audit/compute-saving payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 7) If kinetic steering unexpectedly succeeds, it overturns the hypothesis but yields a strong positive finding that static-trained generators encode causal pathway information."}}
