Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "epistasis_steering_blindspot", "Title": "Levers for Positions, Not for Couplings: Can Interpretable Features Causally Control Epistasis in Protein Language Models?", "Short Hypothesis": "Interpretable sparse-autoencoder features of protein language models give causal, steerable control over SINGLE-POSITION (additive) sequence properties, but NOT over EPISTASIS \u2014 the context-dependent coupling that makes one position's optimal residue depend on another. We hypothesize that steering a feature to install a desired coupling (e.g., a compensatory residue pair, an allosteric linkage) silently produces sequences whose additive properties change while the intended coupling structure fails to form, an 'epistasis blind spot' that single-mutant checks miss but a double-mutant-cycle oracle exposes.", "Related Work": "pLM interpretability/steering work (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; Steering PLMs/ASPO 2025; antibody-SAE 2025; ProtoMech 2026; Automated Neuron Labelling 2025; SAEs for low-N design 2025) discovers and steers features that all encode INTRINSIC, single-position or local-motif properties (secondary structure, GRAVY, molecular weight, zinc-finger motifs, thermostability) and evaluate whether that standalone property appears. Separately, a distinct biology literature establishes that pLMs INTERNALLY capture epistasis: ESM-2 stores coevolving-residue statistics like a Markov Random Field (Zhang et al. 2024), pLMs capture both structural-contact and long-range FUNCTIONAL couplings zero-shot (2025), and higher-order epistasis is present in sequence-function models (2024). Crucially, no work connects these: none asks whether the epistatic/coupling structure pLMs demonstrably represent is a CAUSALLY STEERABLE design lever, contrasts coupling-steerability against single-position steerability, or audits when a designer's coupling request silently fails. This distinguishes the proposal from all single-property steering work (which assumes an additive target) and from the epistasis-probing literature (which is purely descriptive/predictive, never touching feature-level steering of generation). Epistasis is the ideal testbed because it has an exact, well-established quantitative oracle \u2014 the double-mutant cycle (epistasis = ddG_AB - ddG_A - ddG_B) computable from deep mutational scanning (DMS) data or in-silico stability predictors.", "Abstract": "A protein's function is shaped not only by which residue sits at each position but by epistasis: the coupling that makes the best residue at one position depend on another, underlying compensatory mutations, allosteric linkages, and folding cooperativity. Interpretability-guided protein design increasingly steers language-model features to install desired properties, and pLMs are known to internally capture epistatic couplings \u2014 yet every demonstrated steering target is an additive, single-position property. We ask whether interpretable features can causally control epistasis at all, or only additive traits. We surface a behavioral phenomenon we call the epistasis blind spot: steering a feature to install a target coupling (a compensatory residue pair, a contact-based or long-range functional linkage) reliably shifts additive properties while the intended coupling structure fails to form \u2014 invisible to single-mutant and validity checks but exposed by a double-mutant-cycle oracle. Epistasis is uniquely suited to this question because it is exactly quantifiable (epistasis = ddG_AB - ddG_A - ddG_B) from deep mutational scanning data and in-silico stability predictors. Using ESM-2 with TopK and Ordered sparse autoencoders, we (1) LOCATE candidate additive-property features and candidate coupling/epistasis features via probing (single-position vs pairwise-coupling signals); (2) CAUSALLY test the steerability of each, contrasting additive control against coupling control; (3) quantify the blind spot; and (4) AUDIT reliability, testing whether an internal coupling-consistency signal flags silently-uncoupled designs better than single-mutant or likelihood checks. We predict additive control succeeds, coupling control is decodable-but-weakly-steerable, and a cheap internal audit catches epistasis blind spots before expensive validation.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) with TopK and Ordered SAEs (the antibody-SAE paper shows Ordered SAEs recover steerability that TopK lacks) on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. ADDITIVE oracles: per-position property contributions \u2014 single-mutant effect on GRAVY, charge, and a stability proxy (FoldX/Rosetta-style ddG or a fine-tuned stability head), and pLM single-mutant log-likelihood scores. EPISTASIS oracle (exact): the double-mutant cycle epistasis term = ddG_AB - ddG_A - ddG_B, computed (a) from curated deep mutational scanning (DMS) datasets with measured double mutants (e.g. combinatorial DMS libraries in ProteinGym-style resources) and (b) from an in-silico stability predictor for pairs not covered experimentally; plus pLM pairwise-coupling scores (categorical Jacobian, as in Zhang et al. 2024) as an internal coupling read-out. Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity. First establish that the model natively produces sequences spanning a meaningful RANGE of epistatic couplings so absence-of-control claims are meaningful.\n2) FEATURE BANK & LOCATION: Identify interpretable features via probing on held-out sequences: (a) ADDITIVE features (correlate with single-position properties, controlling for context), and (b) candidate COUPLING/EPISTASIS features (correlate with pairwise epistasis magnitude / categorical-Jacobian coupling between specified position pairs, controlling for the two positions' additive effects via partial correlation). Report probe AUROC/R^2 for additive vs coupling; identify top candidate directions; compare against a raw-activation probe; critically test whether epistatic coupling is decodable at all, independent of the constituent single-position signals.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON \u2014 THE EPISTASIS BLIND SPOT (additive-easy/coupling-hard): For each candidate COUPLING feature, sweep steering strength and generate/infill N sequences per level (masked infilling for ESM-2). At each level measure (i) the target-coupling gain \u2014 change in the double-mutant-cycle epistasis at the intended position pair, (ii) the additive-property change at those positions, and (iii) validity. Quantify the blind spot = fraction of coupling steering displacement that lands in the additive axis rather than the epistasis axis, the SILENT-DECOUPLING RATE = fraction of 'coupling-steered', valid sequences whose intended epistasis did NOT form (or formed wrong-sign) while additive properties shifted, and dose-response. Show the blind spot: correlate additive-property change and pseudo-perplexity with true epistasis change (hypothesis: coupling steering is confounded by additive change; standard single-mutant checks miss it). Random-direction and matched-additive-feature controls.\n4) HEAD-TO-HEAD CAUSAL TEST: In the SAME pipeline, calibrate that ADDITIVE features ARE steerable (single-position property gain per unit steer, specificity), then test whether ANY compact intervention (TopK/Ordered SAE feature, difference-of-means between high-vs-low-coupling sequences, activation patching from strongly-coupled sequences, and additive-orthogonalized directions) can install the target epistasis while HOLDING the two positions' additive contributions fixed \u2014 the operational definition of a genuine coupling handle. Report sign, dose-response, and specificity; contrast coupling vs additive steerability effect sizes. Test whether Ordered SAEs recover partial coupling steerability that TopK lacks.\n5) INTERPRETATION: Test whether located 'coupling' units encode a true relational epistasis concept (invariant to which specific residues occupy the two positions, tracking the coupling magnitude) vs shallow single-position proxies, using controlled sequence pairs that dissociate epistasis from additive effects (matched single-position properties but different pairwise coupling, from the DMS-labeled corpus). Auto-label top features via max-activating contexts.\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal COUPLING-CONSISTENCY score (coupling-probe confidence / categorical-Jacobian read mid-generation). Measure whether it flags silently-decoupled designs (additive-OK, coupling-broken) better than output-side signals (single-mutant scores, validity, model likelihood): report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors, and compute/wet-lab-screening saved by flagging non-coupled designs before expensive double-mutant validation. Emit human-readable diagnoses ('positions 42/87 each shifted as requested, but their coupling energy stayed 0 \u2014 no compensatory linkage formed').\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two SAE layers, and both contact-based (short-range) vs long-range functional couplings; report where the blind spot and (non-)steerability findings are stable vs artifact, and whether coupling steerability is worse for long-range than contact-based couplings (linking to the finding that pLMs store contacts more readily than distal functional couplings).\nEVALUATION METRICS: epistasis-blind-spot magnitude & dose-response and SILENT-DECOUPLING RATE (primary phenomenon, via exact double-mutant-cycle oracle); additive-vs-coupling-change correlation (blind spot); additive vs coupling probe AUROC/R^2 (Location); coupling-steering success/specificity vs additive-steering, head-to-head effect sizes (Causal Intervention); coupling-feature invariance to residue identity (Interpretation); internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE and screening saved (auditing); short-range-vs-long-range coupling robustness.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Epistasis has an exact operational definition (double-mutant cycle), but its measurement relies on either DMS data (limited combinatorial double-mutant coverage) or in-silico stability predictors (FoldX/Rosetta), which are proxies; we validate in-silico epistasis against measured double mutants on the DMS-covered subset and restrict strong claims to where they agree, framing claims as model-internal, oracle-verified coupling controllability rather than wet-lab function. 2) The coupling-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (TopK and Ordered SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching, additive-orthogonalized directions), sweeping strength, requiring the model to natively span an epistasis range, and head-to-head calibrating that ADDITIVE features ARE steerable in the same pipeline \u2014 so a null coupling result is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable epistasis handle) is equally publishable and directly enables compensatory/allosteric design. 3) Additive and epistatic effects are partly entangled; the partial-correlation feature selection and additive-matched dissociation pairs (step 5) isolate genuinely additive-independent coupling signal, and we report where separation is impossible. 4) Double-mutant-cycle evaluation is combinatorially heavier than single-mutant checks; we cap position pairs and use the internal audit precisely to reduce this cost. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, raw-activation probes (categorical Jacobian), and random-direction controls. 6) An internal coupling probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (additive-steerable, epistasis-decodable-but-weakly-steerable) and the audit payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 7) If coupling steering unexpectedly succeeds, it overturns the hypothesis but yields a strong positive finding that pLM epistasis is a controllable design lever."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'epistasis_steering_blindspot', 'Title': 'Levers for Positions, Not for Couplings: Can Interpretable Features Causally Control Epistasis in Protein Language Models?', 'Short Hypothesis': "Interpretable sparse-autoencoder features of protein language models give causal, steerable control over SINGLE-POSITION (additive) sequence properties, but NOT over EPISTASIS — the context-dependent coupling that makes one position's optimal residue depend on another. We hypothesize that steering a feature to install a desired coupling (e.g., a compensatory residue pair, an allosteric linkage) silently produces sequences whose additive properties change while the intended coupling structure fails to form, an 'epistasis blind spot' that single-mutant checks miss but a double-mutant-cycle oracle exposes.", 'Related Work': "pLM interpretability/steering work (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; Steering PLMs/ASPO 2025; antibody-SAE 2025; ProtoMech 2026; Automated Neuron Labelling 2025; SAEs for low-N design 2025) discovers and steers features that all encode INTRINSIC, single-position or local-motif properties (secondary structure, GRAVY, molecular weight, zinc-finger motifs, thermostability) and evaluate whether that standalone property appears. Separately, a distinct biology literature establishes that pLMs INTERNALLY capture epistasis: ESM-2 stores coevolving-residue statistics like a Markov Random Field (Zhang et al. 2024), pLMs capture both structural-contact and long-range FUNCTIONAL couplings zero-shot (2025), and higher-order epistasis is present in sequence-function models (2024). Crucially, no work connects these: none asks whether the epistatic/coupling structure pLMs demonstrably represent is a CAUSALLY STEERABLE design lever, contrasts coupling-steerability against single-position steerability, or audits when a designer's coupling request silently fails. This distinguishes the proposal from all single-property steering work (which assumes an additive target) and from the epistasis-probing literature (which is purely descriptive/predictive, never touching feature-level steering of generation). Epistasis is the ideal testbed because it has an exact, well-established quantitative oracle — the double-mutant cycle (epistasis = ddG_AB - ddG_A - ddG_B) computable from deep mutational scanning (DMS) data or in-silico stability predictors.", 'Abstract': "A protein's function is shaped not only by which residue sits at each position but by epistasis: the coupling that makes the best residue at one position depend on another, underlying compensatory mutations, allosteric linkages, and folding cooperativity. Interpretability-guided protein design increasingly steers language-model features to install desired properties, and pLMs are known to internally capture epistatic couplings — yet every demonstrated steering target is an additive, single-position property. We ask whether interpretable features can causally control epistasis at all, or only additive traits. We surface a behavioral phenomenon we call the epistasis blind spot: steering a feature to install a target coupling (a compensatory residue pair, a contact-based or long-range functional linkage) reliably shifts additive properties while the intended coupling structure fails to form — invisible to single-mutant and validity checks but exposed by a double-mutant-cycle oracle. Epistasis is uniquely suited to this question because it is exactly quantifiable (epistasis = ddG_AB - ddG_A - ddG_B) from deep mutational scanning data and in-silico stability predictors. Using ESM-2 with TopK and Ordered sparse autoencoders, we (1) LOCATE candidate additive-property features and candidate coupling/epistasis features via probing (single-position vs pairwise-coupling signals); (2) CAUSALLY test the steerability of each, contrasting additive control against coupling control; (3) quantify the blind spot; and (4) AUDIT reliability, testing whether an internal coupling-consistency signal flags silently-uncoupled designs better than single-mutant or likelihood checks. We predict additive control succeeds, coupling control is decodable-but-weakly-steerable, and a cheap internal audit catches epistasis blind spots before expensive validation.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) with TopK and Ordered SAEs (the antibody-SAE paper shows Ordered SAEs recover steerability that TopK lacks) on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. ADDITIVE oracles: per-position property contributions — single-mutant effect on GRAVY, charge, and a stability proxy (FoldX/Rosetta-style ddG or a fine-tuned stability head), and pLM single-mutant log-likelihood scores. EPISTASIS oracle (exact): the double-mutant cycle epistasis term = ddG_AB - ddG_A - ddG_B, computed (a) from curated deep mutational scanning (DMS) datasets with measured double mutants (e.g. combinatorial DMS libraries in ProteinGym-style resources) and (b) from an in-silico stability predictor for pairs not covered experimentally; plus pLM pairwise-coupling scores (categorical Jacobian, as in Zhang et al. 2024) as an internal coupling read-out. Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity. First establish that the model natively produces sequences spanning a meaningful RANGE of epistatic couplings so absence-of-control claims are meaningful.\n2) FEATURE BANK & LOCATION: Identify interpretable features via probing on held-out sequences: (a) ADDITIVE features (correlate with single-position properties, controlling for context), and (b) candidate COUPLING/EPISTASIS features (correlate with pairwise epistasis magnitude / categorical-Jacobian coupling between specified position pairs, controlling for the two positions' additive effects via partial correlation). Report probe AUROC/R^2 for additive vs coupling; identify top candidate directions; compare against a raw-activation probe; critically test whether epistatic coupling is decodable at all, independent of the constituent single-position signals.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON — THE EPISTASIS BLIND SPOT (additive-easy/coupling-hard): For each candidate COUPLING feature, sweep steering strength and generate/infill N sequences per level (masked infilling for ESM-2). At each level measure (i) the target-coupling gain — change in the double-mutant-cycle epistasis at the intended position pair, (ii) the additive-property change at those positions, and (iii) validity. Quantify the blind spot = fraction of coupling steering displacement that lands in the additive axis rather than the epistasis axis, the SILENT-DECOUPLING RATE = fraction of 'coupling-steered', valid sequences whose intended epistasis did NOT form (or formed wrong-sign) while additive properties shifted, and dose-response. Show the blind spot: correlate additive-property change and pseudo-perplexity with true epistasis change (hypothesis: coupling steering is confounded by additive change; standard single-mutant checks miss it). Random-direction and matched-additive-feature controls.\n4) HEAD-TO-HEAD CAUSAL TEST: In the SAME pipeline, calibrate that ADDITIVE features ARE steerable (single-position property gain per unit steer, specificity), then test whether ANY compact intervention (TopK/Ordered SAE feature, difference-of-means between high-vs-low-coupling sequences, activation patching from strongly-coupled sequences, and additive-orthogonalized directions) can install the target epistasis while HOLDING the two positions' additive contributions fixed — the operational definition of a genuine coupling handle. Report sign, dose-response, and specificity; contrast coupling vs additive steerability effect sizes. Test whether Ordered SAEs recover partial coupling steerability that TopK lacks.\n5) INTERPRETATION: Test whether located 'coupling' units encode a true relational epistasis concept (invariant to which specific residues occupy the two positions, tracking the coupling magnitude) vs shallow single-position proxies, using controlled sequence pairs that dissociate epistasis from additive effects (matched single-position properties but different pairwise coupling, from the DMS-labeled corpus). Auto-label top features via max-activating contexts.\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal COUPLING-CONSISTENCY score (coupling-probe confidence / categorical-Jacobian read mid-generation). Measure whether it flags silently-decoupled designs (additive-OK, coupling-broken) better than output-side signals (single-mutant scores, validity, model likelihood): report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors, and compute/wet-lab-screening saved by flagging non-coupled designs before expensive double-mutant validation. Emit human-readable diagnoses ('positions 42/87 each shifted as requested, but their coupling energy stayed 0 — no compensatory linkage formed').\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two SAE layers, and both contact-based (short-range) vs long-range functional couplings; report where the blind spot and (non-)steerability findings are stable vs artifact, and whether coupling steerability is worse for long-range than contact-based couplings (linking to the finding that pLMs store contacts more readily than distal functional couplings).\nEVALUATION METRICS: epistasis-blind-spot magnitude & dose-response and SILENT-DECOUPLING RATE (primary phenomenon, via exact double-mutant-cycle oracle); additive-vs-coupling-change correlation (blind spot); additive vs coupling probe AUROC/R^2 (Location); coupling-steering success/specificity vs additive-steering, head-to-head effect sizes (Causal Intervention); coupling-feature invariance to residue identity (Interpretation); internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE and screening saved (auditing); short-range-vs-long-range coupling robustness.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) Epistasis has an exact operational definition (double-mutant cycle), but its measurement relies on either DMS data (limited combinatorial double-mutant coverage) or in-silico stability predictors (FoldX/Rosetta), which are proxies; we validate in-silico epistasis against measured double mutants on the DMS-covered subset and restrict strong claims to where they agree, framing claims as model-internal, oracle-verified coupling controllability rather than wet-lab function. 2) The coupling-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (TopK and Ordered SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching, additive-orthogonalized directions), sweeping strength, requiring the model to natively span an epistasis range, and head-to-head calibrating that ADDITIVE features ARE steerable in the same pipeline — so a null coupling result is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable epistasis handle) is equally publishable and directly enables compensatory/allosteric design. 3) Additive and epistatic effects are partly entangled; the partial-correlation feature selection and additive-matched dissociation pairs (step 5) isolate genuinely additive-independent coupling signal, and we report where separation is impossible. 4) Double-mutant-cycle evaluation is combinatorially heavier than single-mutant checks; we cap position pairs and use the internal audit precisely to reduce this cost. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, raw-activation probes (categorical Jacobian), and random-direction controls. 6) An internal coupling probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (additive-steerable, epistasis-decodable-but-weakly-steerable) and the audit payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 7) If coupling steering unexpectedly succeeds, it overturns the hypothesis but yields a strong positive finding that pLM epistasis is a controllable design lever.'}

