hich contrast intrinsic vs relational/global/pathway properties; here the contrast is categorical presence vs quantitative magnitude of the SAME property, and it borrows a specific human-numeracy finding.', 'Abstract': "Real scientific design specifies numbers, not directions: a chemist needs logP = 2.5, an enzyme engineer needs 40% helix content, not merely 'more'. Interpretability-guided design increasingly steers language-model features to hit properties, but every demonstrated result reports only that the property increased or appeared. We ask whether interpretable features actually provide a CALIBRATED MAGNITUDE DIAL or merely a PRESENCE SWITCH. Borrowing a well-established cognitive-science result—that language models encode numeric magnitude in a logarithmically-compressed, causally-manipulable subspace—we test whether scientific generative models inherit this and whether it undermines quantitative controllability. We surface a behavioral phenomenon we call the magnitude blind spot: steering an interpretable feature moves a property monotonically but cannot reliably reach a specified numeric set-point, and control error grows systematically at high magnitudes, mirroring log compression. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders and exact quantitative oracles (RDKit logP/MW/TPSA; DSSP helix/sheet fraction; GRAVY), we (1) LOCATE candidate presence features and candidate magnitude directions via probing (classification vs regression, and testing for log-scaled geometry); (2) CAUSALLY test presence-steerability vs calibrated magnitude-steerability by asking each feature to hit graded numeric targets and measuring set-point error; (3) INTERPRET whether the magnitude code is linear or log-compressed and whether re-parameterizing steering by log-magnitude restores calibration; and (4) AUDIT reliability, testing whether an internal magnitude-consistency signal flags off-target designs better than presence/validity checks. We predict presence control succeeds, calibrated magnitude control fails—worse at high values—and a simple log-reparameterized dial recovers much of the lost precision.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, since the antibody-SAE literature shows TopK features can be decodable-but-inert) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Choose properties with EXACT continuous oracles so 'magnitude' is unambiguous: molecules—logP, molecular weight, TPSA, aromatic-ring count (RDKit); proteins—alpha-helix fraction and beta-sheet fraction (DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY hydrophobicity. Validity gates: RDKit-validity+SA for molecules; ESMFold pLDDT+pseudo-perplexity for proteins. Confirm the model natively spans a wide magnitude RANGE for each property so set-point claims are meaningful.\n2) LOCATION (presence vs magnitude): For each property, on held-out objects (a) train a CLASSIFIER probe for presence (above/below median) and (b) a REGRESSION probe for the continuous value, from SAE features and from raw activations; identify the top presence feature(s) and the top magnitude direction (difference-of-means between high/low bins, and a regression-weight direction). Test the log-geometry: fit both linear and log(value) regressions and compare R^2; check whether equal STEPS in the magnitude direction correspond to equal RATIOS (log) rather than equal increments (linear) in the property, quantifying a per-property compression exponent.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON—THE MAGNITUDE BLIND SPOT (presence-easy/set-point-hard): For each property, define a ladder of numeric set-point targets spanning the native range (e.g., logP = -1, 1, 3, 5). Steer the located feature/direction and, for each target, search steering strength to best hit the target; measure SET-POINT ERROR = |achieved - requested| on the exact oracle, and the fraction of targets hit within a tolerance band. Compare against the trivial PRESENCE task (just raise the property above threshold), which we expect to succeed. Quantify (i) the presence-vs-set-point success gap (primary), (ii) MONOTONE-BUT-UNCALIBRATED behavior (property moves the right direction but overshoots/undershoots the number), and (iii) the LEVEL-DEPENDENT failure profile: regress set-point error on requested magnitude, testing the hypothesis that error grows at high magnitudes consistent with log compression. Random-direction and matched-non-property-feature controls; report validity throughout.\n4) BLIND-SPOT INVISIBILITY: Correlate output-side checks (validity, pseudo-perplexity/RDKit-validity, presence-classifier confidence, model likelihood) with true set-point error (hypothesis: near-zero—standard evals pass while the number is wrong).\n5) INTERPRETATION + FIX (log-reparameterized dial): If the magnitude code is log-compressed, re-parameterize the steering-strength->magnitude map on a log scale (or fit a per-feature calibration curve from a small strength sweep) and re-run the set-point ladder. Test whether calibrated/log-reparameterized steering substantially reduces set-point error, especially at high magnitudes, at matched validity—turning a monotone switch into a usable dial. Compare TopK vs Ordered SAE features and difference-of-means vs regression directions.\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal MAGNITUDE-CONSISTENCY score (regression-probe read-out mid-generation) as a predictor of whether a requested set-point was actually reached; report AUROC/ECE vs output-side baselines for flagging off-target designs, and compute saved by rejecting/re-steering before expensive downstream validation. Emit human-readable diagnoses ('requested logP 4.5, feature saturates near 3.2').\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two layers, and multiple properties; report where the compression exponent, blind spot, and the log-fix are stable vs artifact, and whether high-magnitude failure tracks how far off-distribution the target sits.\nEVALUATION METRICS: presence-vs-set-point success gap and SET-POINT ERROR distribution (primary phenomenon, via exact oracles); level-dependent error slope and per-property compression exponent (log-geometry); output-side-check-vs-error correlation (blind-spot invisibility); linear-vs-log probe R^2 (Interpretation); set-point-error reduction from log/calibrated reparameterization at matched validity (fix); internal magnitude-consistency AUROC/ECE and compute saved (auditing); robustness across models/SAEs/properties.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) Property oracles differ in exactness—RDKit logP/MW/TPSA are effectively exact for the definition, while DSSP-on-ESMFold helix fraction is a structure-prediction proxy; we lead with the exact molecular oracles for core claims and treat protein results as corroborating, reporting oracle fidelity. 2) The magnitude-blind-spot is partly an ABSENCE-of-calibration claim; we mitigate by trying multiple directions (SAE feature, difference-of-means, regression-weight), TopK and Ordered SAEs, strength search per target, and by head-to-head confirming the PRESENCE task succeeds in the same pipeline—so failure to hit numbers is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuinely calibrated dial exists) is equally publishable. 3) Set-point error could be confounded by validity collapse near range extremes; we restrict to strengths within the validity budget and separately report the in-validity error curve. 4) The log-reparameterization fix could merely fit noise; we fit the calibration on a held-out strength sweep and evaluate on unseen targets, and report the exponent's stability across seeds. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with multiple SAE variants, layers, raw-activation probes, and random-direction controls. 6) An internal magnitude probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (presence-steerable, magnitude-log-compressed-and-uncalibrated) and the calibration fix and audit payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 7) If calibrated set-point control already works out of the box, that is a valuable positive result establishing that interpretable features provide a usable quantitative dial for scientific design."}

