Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "convergence_order_rigidity", "Title": "Coarse Before Fine, or Broken: Is the Property Convergence Order of Scientific Diffusion Generators a Rigid Physical Schedule or a Controllable Design Knob?", "Short Hypothesis": "During iterative generation of proteins and molecules, structural/property attributes get 'locked in' in a stable coarse-to-fine ORDER (global topology/scaffold early, fine local detail late), and this order is a RIGID physical schedule the model enforces: interpretable-feature interventions can shift the timing of an individual property within its natural slot, but attempting to REORDER the schedule\u2014forcing a fine property to converge before its coarse prerequisite\u2014silently produces invalid objects because coarse-then-fine is a hard dependency, not a free hyperparameter. We further hypothesize an intervention-free 'order-violation' signal (early firing of a late-slot feature before its coarse prerequisite has stabilized) predicts these silent failures.", "Related Work": "A consistent coarse-to-fine convergence ORDER during iterative generation is documented across modalities but only DESCRIPTIVELY and never in scientific design: TIDE (2025) shows DiTs lock in 3D structure, then object class, then fine concepts; the audio-SAE paper (2025) finds DiffRhythm converges pitch first, then timbre, then loudness; 'Emergence and Evolution of Interpretable Concepts in Diffusion Models' (2025) finds composition locks early and style mid-process; DLM-Scope (2026) analyzes decoding order in diffusion language models. In scientific generation, my prior causal_timing_window_design proposal maps WHEN to intervene for a FIXED property (its causal window) but assumes the property's slot is given; all other scientific-steering work (InterPLM, ProtSAE, FoldSAE, ProtoMech) is single-pass or uniform-strength and never studies the convergence schedule at all. NO prior work (a) establishes that scientific diffusion generators have a stable coarse-to-fine property convergence order, (b) tests whether that ORDER is causally reorderable via interpretable features vs a rigid physical dependency, or (c) shows that violating the natural order silently breaks validity and provides an internal order-violation alarm. This reframes convergence order from a descriptive curiosity into a hard design constraint and a controllability/validity question\u2014distinct from both the descriptive cross-modal order literature and from timing-window steering.", "Abstract": "Iterative generative models\u2014across images, audio, and language\u2014lock in attributes in a strikingly consistent coarse-to-fine order: global structure before fine detail. We ask whether scientific diffusion generators of proteins and molecules exhibit the same convergence schedule, and more importantly, whether that ORDER is a controllable design knob or a rigid physical dependency. This matters because real design often wants to prioritize a fine property (a specific motif, a functional group) while a coarse prerequisite (fold topology, scaffold) is still forming, and it is unknown whether a model can honor such a request or will silently fail. We hypothesize an order-rigidity phenomenon: interpretable-feature interventions can shift the timing of a property WITHIN its natural coarse-to-fine slot, but REORDERING the schedule\u2014forcing a fine property to converge before its coarse prerequisite\u2014produces objects that pass fluency/validity spot-checks yet are structurally broken, because coarse-then-fine is a hard dependency. Using protein and molecular diffusion generators with sparse autoencoders on per-timestep denoiser activations, we (1) MEASURE the property convergence order via per-timestep feature/probe stabilization against hard oracles (DSSP/ESMFold, RDKit/SMARTS); (2) CAUSALLY test order malleability by forcing early convergence of late-slot features and measuring validity collapse versus in-slot timing shifts; (3) INTERPRET whether coarse features gate fine ones (do coarse-prerequisite features causally control when fine features can stabilize?); and (4) AUDIT reliability, testing whether an intervention-free order-violation signal flags silently-broken designs. We predict a rigid coarse-to-fine schedule, silent breakage under reordering, and a cheap internal alarm for it.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use iterative generators with accessible per-step denoiser activations: a discrete/masked protein diffusion or order-agnostic protein LM (e.g., DPLM/masked-diffusion ESM-style) and a molecular diffusion model over SMILES/graphs. Train TopK (and temporally-conditioned, TIDE-style) SAEs on the denoiser residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Define COARSE properties (proteins: overall secondary-structure content, fold topology/contact-order proxy via DSSP on ESMFold; molecules: scaffold/ring-system, aromatic-ring count via RDKit) and FINE properties (proteins: a single local motif via HMMER/regex, a specific loop; molecules: a specific functional group via SMARTS, a substituent). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + synthetic accessibility for molecules.\n2) MEASURE CONVERGENCE ORDER (Location, phenomenon establishment): For a large set of unsteered generations, at each denoising timestep compute how well each property's SAE feature / probe on partial activations predicts the FINISHED object's property value; define each property's CONVERGENCE TIME as the timestep at which this prediction stabilizes (crosses and stays above threshold). Test the coarse-to-fine hypothesis: coarse-property convergence times are systematically earlier than fine ones (paired test across property pairs and both domains; report effect size, CIs), replicating the cross-modal order finding in scientific generators. Cross-check with an SAE-independent raw-activation probe.\n3) CAUSAL ORDER-MALLEABILITY TEST (core): For matched coarse/fine property pairs, run two interventions via the property-linked features: (a) IN-SLOT timing shift\u2014steer a property earlier/later but WITHIN its natural slot ordering (coarse still before fine); (b) REORDER\u2014clamp the FINE-property feature strongly at early timesteps to force it to converge BEFORE its coarse prerequisite has stabilized (and the symmetric case of delaying the coarse property past the fine one). Measure, on FINISHED objects: property success for both properties, and VALIDITY. Primary metric: the REORDER-VALIDITY GAP = validity/property-success under reordering minus under in-slot shifting at matched steering budget. Hypothesis: in-slot shifts largely preserve validity while reordering causes a SILENT-BREAKAGE RATE = fraction of reordered generations that satisfy the fine property and pass fluency checks yet fail structural validity (low pLDDT / broken scaffold). Random-direction and matched-non-property-feature controls; sweep steering strength within the validity budget.\n4) DEPENDENCY TEST (Interpretation / Causal Intervention): Test whether coarse features GATE fine ones\u2014does the fine property's convergence time causally depend on the coarse prerequisite? Ablate/delay the coarse-prerequisite feature and measure whether the fine feature's stabilization is pushed later or prevented (a gating relationship), versus the reverse ablation (fine should not gate coarse). Report directional asymmetry as evidence for a hard coarse\u2192fine dependency rather than independent scheduling.\n5) BLIND-SPOT + INTERNAL ALARM (Decision Auditing, payoff): Show the breakage is silent: correlate output-side checks (pseudo-perplexity, RDKit-validity, the fine-property predictor's own confidence) with true structural validity of reordered generations (hypothesis: near-chance\u2014fine property present, object broken). Then deploy an intervention-free ORDER-VIOLATION signal read mid-generation\u2014early firing of a late-slot feature while its coarse-prerequisite feature has NOT yet stabilized\u2014and test whether it flags eventually-broken designs earlier and better than output-side signals (AUROC/ECE vs generation progress; earliest-reliable-detection timestep; compute saved by aborting doomed reordered generations before full denoising + ESMFold/validation).\n6) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model checkpoints/sizes, shared vs temporally-conditioned SAEs, and multiple coarse/fine property pairs; report where the coarse-to-fine order, its rigidity, and the alarm are stable vs model-specific, and whether rigidity is stronger for pairs with a clearer physical dependency (motif-in-fold) than weakly-coupled pairs.\nEVALUATION METRICS: coarse-vs-fine convergence-order effect size (phenomenon); REORDER-VALIDITY GAP and SILENT-BREAKAGE RATE (primary causal metric); coarse\u2192fine gating asymmetry (dependency); output-side-check-vs-validity correlation (blind spot); order-violation-alarm AUROC/ECE, earliest-detection timestep, and compute saved (auditing); robustness across models/SAEs/property pairs.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Requires generators with accessible per-step denoiser activations; scope limited to instrumentable open iterative models (masked-diffusion protein/molecule generators). 2) In-silico oracles (ESMFold, DSSP, RDKit, HMMER) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal convergence scheduling, controllability, and structural validity, not experimental function\u2014stated explicitly. 3) The rigidity claim is partly an ABSENCE claim (reordering cannot be done without breakage); we mitigate by trying multiple reordering interventions (feature clamping, difference-of-means, activation forcing) and strengths, and by confirming that IN-SLOT timing shifts DO succeed in the same pipeline\u2014so a reordering failure is meaningful, not a weak-intervention artifact. A positive result (order is freely reorderable) is equally publishable and would overturn the cross-modal coarse-to-fine dogma for scientific generators. 4) Convergence-order and validity may be confounded with generic off-distribution drift; random-direction and matched-non-property controls, plus the in-slot-vs-reorder contrast, isolate the order-specific effect. 5) Coarse and fine properties may be irreducibly entangled for some pairs; we select pairs with a clear physical coarse\u2192fine dependency (motif-within-fold, substituent-on-scaffold) and report weakly-coupled pairs separately. 6) SAE quality / layer / temporal-conditioning could confound convergence-time measurement; controlled with shared and temporally-conditioned SAEs, multiple layers, and raw-activation probes. 7) The internal order-violation alarm might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; even so, its interpretability (naming the violated dependency) and the early-abort compute savings are the contributions, reported honestly. 8) Checkpoint/timestep spacing may miss sharp convergence transitions; we densify timestep sampling around detected transitions."}}
