 (1) LOCATE candidate identity features and candidate perturbation-response features via probing; (2) CAUSALLY test the steerability of each, contrasting identity control against response control (does the steered cell's DEG signature match the true perturbation's direction and specificity?); (3) INTERPRET whether located response units encode a genuine directional-response concept or shallow identity proxies; and (4) AUDIT reliability, testing whether an internal response-consistency signal flags silently-misresponding designs. We predict identity control succeeds, response control is decodable-but-weakly-steerable, and a cheap internal audit catches response blind spots before wet-lab validation.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use two open scFMs with accessible activations—scGPT and Geneformer (optionally scFoundation)—and train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, since the antibody-SAE literature shows TopK features can be decodable-but-inert) SAEs on residual-stream activations at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic (and a late layer, where prior work finds richest biological programs); use domain-appropriate strong L1 per Kendiukhov (2026) and verify reconstruction fidelity (R^2, L0). IDENTITY oracles: cell-type / tissue / cell-cycle-phase classifiers (standard scFM downstream heads / linear probes on held-out atlas cells, e.g. Tabula Sapiens). RESPONSE oracle (directional, causal ground truth): held-out Perturb-seq atlases (Replogle K562/RPE1, Norman combinatorial) providing, for each genetic perturbation, the true DEG signature—the set and SIGNED direction of differentially expressed genes relative to control. Define response-match metrics: DEG-direction cosine / signed-rank correlation between a steered cell's induced expression shift and the true perturbation's DEG vector, plus specificity (match to the INTENDED perturbation minus best-matching DECOY perturbation). Confirm the models natively span a meaningful RANGE of perturbation responses so absence-of-control claims are meaningful; note baseline benchmarks that trivial predictors are competitive, framing why causal steering is the sharper test.\n2) FEATURE BANK & LOCATION: On held-out cells/perturbations, probe SAE features/raw activations for (a) IDENTITY features (predict cell type/tissue/phase), and (b) candidate RESPONSE features (correlate with a specific perturbation's DEG-direction signature, controlling for baseline cell identity via partial correlation / identity-matched cells). Report probe AUROC/R^2 for identity vs response; identify top candidate directions (SAE feature, difference-of-means between perturbed vs control cells, regression-weight direction); compare against a raw-activation probe. Critically test whether directional perturbation response is decodable AT ALL independent of identity.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON—THE RESPONSE BLIND SPOT (identity-easy/response-hard): For each candidate RESPONSE feature, sweep steering strength on control cells and, at each level, decode the induced transcriptome shift. Measure (i) RESPONSE-match gain—DEG-direction cosine to the true intended perturbation, and specificity vs decoys; (ii) the IDENTITY change (did the cell drift to another cell type?); and (iii) validity (in-distribution reconstruction / plausible cell embedding). Quantify the blind spot = fraction of response-steering displacement that lands in the identity axis rather than the response axis; the SILENT-MISRESPONSE RATE = fraction of 'response-steered', valid cells whose DEG direction does NOT match the intended perturbation (or matches a decoy) while identity/validity checks pass; and dose-response. Show invisibility: correlate output-side checks (identity-classifier confidence, reconstruction error, embedding plausibility) with true response-match (hypothesis: near-zero—standard evals miss it). Random-direction and matched-identity-feature controls.\n4) HEAD-TO-HEAD CAUSAL TEST: In the SAME pipeline, calibrate that IDENTITY features ARE steerable (cell-type-switch success per unit steer, specificity, replicating prior scFM-SAE steering), then test whether ANY compact intervention (TopK/Ordered SAE feature, difference-of-means perturbed-minus-control direction, activation patching from cells that truly received the perturbation, and identity-orthogonalized directions) can reproduce the correct directional DEG response while HOLDING cell identity fixed—the operational definition of a genuine response handle. Report sign, dose-response, specificity; contrast response vs identity steerability effect sizes. Test whether Ordered/Matryoshka SAEs recover partial response steerability that TopK lacks. Compare feature-steering against the model's own native in-silico perturbation mechanism (gene-token deletion/rank manipulation) to test whether interpretable steering is any better/worse at directional response.\n5) INTERPRETATION: Test whether located 'response' units encode a genuine directional-response concept (invariant to baseline cell type, tracking the perturbation's signed DEG direction) vs shallow identity/co-expression proxies, using controlled cell pairs that dissociate response from identity (same perturbation across different cell types; different perturbations in the same cell type). Auto-label top features via scFeatureLens/gene-set enrichment. A feature that fires by cell type regardless of the perturbation is identity, not response.\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal RESPONSE-CONSISTENCY score (response-probe confidence / feature coherence read from the steered activation). Measure whether it flags silently-misresponding cells (identity-OK, response-wrong) better than output-side signals (identity confidence, reconstruction, embedding density): report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors and compute/wet-lab screening saved by flagging non-responsive in-silico perturbations before expensive Perturb-seq validation. Emit human-readable diagnoses ('steering matched the CD8-T identity program but induced the wrong DEG direction for the IFNG knockdown').\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across scGPT vs Geneformer (vs scFoundation), TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two layers, single- vs double-gene perturbations, and two cell lines (K562, RPE1); report where the blind spot and (non-)steerability findings are stable vs artifact, and whether response steerability is worse for perturbations with weak native DEG signal (linking to the low perturbation-specific variance flagged by benchmarks) and for combinatorial/interaction perturbations.\nEVALUATION METRICS: response-blind-spot magnitude & dose-response and SILENT-MISRESPONSE RATE (primary phenomenon, via directional DEG oracle from Perturb-seq); identity-vs-response-change correlation (blind spot); identity vs response probe AUROC/R^2 (Location); response-steering DEG-direction match/specificity vs identity-steering, head-to-head effect sizes (Causal Intervention); response-feature invariance to cell type (Interpretation); internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE and screening saved (auditing); robustness across models/SAEs/perturbation types.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) The directional-response oracle relies on Perturb-seq data known to have low perturbation-specific variance and noisy DEG signatures; we mitigate by focusing core claims on high-signal perturbations (strong, reproducible DEG sets), using signed-rank/cosine metrics robust to noise, and reporting results stratified by native DEG-signal strength. Claims concern model-internal, in-silico-oracle-verified response controllability, not wet-lab causal biology—stated explicitly. 2) The response-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (TopK/Ordered SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching from truly-perturbed cells, identity-orthogonalized directions), sweeping strength, requiring the model to natively span a response range, and head-to-head calibrating that IDENTITY features ARE steerable in the same pipeline—so a null response result is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable directional-response handle) is equally publishable and would be a strong, surprising counter to the benchmarking literature. 3) Identity and response are partly entangled (a perturbation can shift a cell toward another state); the partial-correlation feature selection and identity-matched dissociation pairs (step 5) isolate genuinely identity-independent response signal, and we report where separation is impossible. 4) scFM benchmarks show these models barely beat trivial baselines at perturbation prediction; if response steering fails, one interpretation is 'the model has no causal response representation to steer'—which is precisely the informative, publishable finding, and the identity-steering calibration rules out that the whole pipeline is broken. 5) SAE quality/layer/regularization strongly affects biological interpretability in scFMs (needs strong L1); we control with multiple SAE variants, layers, tuned sparsity, raw-activation probes, and random-direction controls. 6) An internal response-consistency probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (identity-steerable, response-decodable-but-weakly-steerable) and the audit/screening-saving payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 7) Decoding a steered activation back to a full transcriptome shift depends on the scFM's generative/reconstruction pathway, which differs across architectures; we standardize the DEG-direction evaluation on the decoded expression shift and use activation patching as an architecture-agnostic cross-check."}

