Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "evolutionary_time_steering", "Title": "Steering Backward Through Deep Time: Is the Phylogenetic Manifold of Protein Language Models a Causal Design Lever for Ancestral Sequences?", "Short Hypothesis": "Protein language models internally encode a decodable phylogenetic 'evolutionary-time' manifold, but we hypothesize this manifold is a DECODABLE-BUT-WEAKLY-STEERABLE representation: interpretable features let you READ a sequence's evolutionary position, yet steering generation along the phylogenetic direction to design a plausible ANCESTRAL (or forward-time) sequence at a specified evolutionary distance systematically fails\u2014silently producing sequences that preserve family identity while landing at the wrong evolutionary depth and violating the substitution/indel statistics of true ancestors, a blind spot that family-membership and validity checks miss but a phylogenetic-placement oracle exposes.", "Related Work": "Multiple works establish that pLMs DECODE phylogeny: 'Do protein language models learn phylogeny?' (2024) shows <10% of ESM2 neurons recapitulate classical phylogenetic distances; MSA-Transformer column attentions correlate with Hamming distances (2022); Evo 2 encodes phylogeny as a curved manifold ('Finding the Tree of Life in Evo 2', 2025); mBERT encodes language phylogeny (2020). Separately, deep-learning ancestral sequence reconstruction (ASR) is advancing (autoregressive-generative ASR 2024; BetaReconstruct 2026; LASE 2023) but treats generative models as black-box reconstructors, NOT as interpretable steerable systems. Critically, 'Ancestral Sequences Cannot be Accurately Reconstructed via Interpolation in a VAE Latent Space' (2025) shows latent interpolation fails for ASR despite the latent space containing phylogenetic signal\u2014exactly the decodable-but-not-usable gap\u2014but never tests SAE-feature steering of a pLM, never contrasts family-identity steerability against evolutionary-depth steerability, and never audits silent failure. SAE-steering work (InterPLM, ProtSAE, Steering PLMs, low-N SAE design) steers toward static structural/functional properties and never touches the temporal/evolutionary axis. No prior work (a) asks whether the phylogenetic manifold is CAUSALLY steerable for generation, (b) contrasts steering family-identity (easy) vs evolutionary-depth (hypothesized hard), or (c) provides an internal evolutionary-placement audit. This introduces the temporal/evolutionary-time axis into interpretability-of-generation, distinct from all my prior static/endpoint/relational/pathway proposals.", "Abstract": "A protein's sequence carries not just what it does but WHEN in evolutionary history it existed: ancestral reconstruction\u2014inferring the sequences of extinct proteins\u2014is a foundational tool in evolutionary biology and a promising route to novel, robust enzymes. Protein language models are known to internally encode phylogeny as a decodable geometric manifold, and generative models are increasingly proposed for ancestral sequence reconstruction (ASR). Yet whether this encoded evolutionary structure is a CAUSALLY STEERABLE design lever\u2014whether you can steer a model to generate a plausible ancestor at a specified evolutionary depth\u2014is unknown, and prior latent-interpolation ASR fails despite the latent space containing phylogenetic signal. We surface and test a behavioral phenomenon we call the evolutionary-depth blind spot: steering an interpretable feature to move a sequence 'back in time' reliably preserves protein-family identity while failing to place the sequence at the intended evolutionary depth or match the substitution/indel statistics of true ancestors\u2014invisible to family-membership and validity checks but exposed by a phylogenetic-placement oracle. Using ESM-2 (and Evo 2 for DNA) with sparse autoencoders, we (1) LOCATE candidate family-identity features and candidate evolutionary-depth/phylogenetic-position features via probing; (2) CAUSALLY test the steerability of each, contrasting family control against evolutionary-depth control, scored against gold-standard maximum-likelihood ASR ancestors and tree-placement; (3) INTERPRET whether located depth units encode a genuine evolutionary-distance concept or shallow proxies; and (4) AUDIT reliability, testing whether an internal phylogenetic-consistency signal flags silently-misplaced designs. We predict family control succeeds, evolutionary-depth control is decodable-but-weakly-steerable, and a cheap internal audit catches misplaced ancestors before costly resurrection experiments.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) with TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, since the antibody-SAE literature shows TopK features can be decodable-but-inert) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Optionally extend to Evo 2 (DNA, using its published biologically-relevant layer) as a cross-substrate check. GOLD-STANDARD EVOLUTIONARY ORACLE: for curated protein families (Pfam alignments with well-resolved trees), run classical maximum-likelihood ASR (RAxML/IQ-TREE + FastML/PAML) to obtain reference ancestral sequences at each internal tree node and each node's evolutionary DEPTH (root-distance / total branch length). PHYLOGENETIC-PLACEMENT oracle: place any candidate sequence on the reference tree (pplacer/EPA-ng) to get its inferred depth and node position, and compute substitution/indel statistics vs the expected model. FAMILY-IDENTITY oracle: Pfam/HMMER family assignment and family-classifier probe. Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity. First confirm the model natively spans a meaningful evolutionary-depth RANGE (generations from shallow to deep) so absence-of-control claims are meaningful.\n2) FEATURE BANK & LOCATION: On held-out extant + reconstructed-ancestral sequences, probe SAE features/raw activations for (a) FAMILY-IDENTITY features (predict Pfam family), and (b) candidate EVOLUTIONARY-DEPTH features (regress on tree-depth / root-distance and predict phylogenetic position, controlling for family via partial correlation / family-matched sets). Report probe AUROC/R^2 for family vs depth; identify top candidate directions (SAE feature, difference-of-means between deep-node vs leaf sequences, regression-weight direction, and the flat phylogenetic subspace found in Evo 2); compare against raw-activation probes. Critically test whether evolutionary depth is decodable independent of family identity.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON\u2014THE EVOLUTIONARY-DEPTH BLIND SPOT (family-easy/depth-hard): For each candidate DEPTH feature, take extant leaf sequences and steer 'back in time' along the direction at swept strength; complete generation (masked infilling). At each level measure (i) DEPTH gain\u2014does the generated sequence's pplacer-inferred depth move toward the intended ancestral depth, and does it match the reference ML ancestor (sequence identity to the true node ancestor, and match of expected substitution/indel statistics)?, (ii) the FAMILY-identity change (did it drift to another family or stay in-family?), and (iii) validity. Quantify the blind spot = fraction of depth-steering displacement that lands in the family-identity axis rather than the evolutionary-depth axis; the SILENT-MISPLACEMENT RATE = fraction of 'depth-steered', valid, in-family sequences whose inferred phylogenetic depth does NOT match the request (or whose substitution profile is inconsistent with a real ancestor) while family/validity checks pass; and dose-response. Show invisibility: correlate output-side checks (family-membership confidence, pseudo-perplexity, pLDDT, model likelihood) with true depth-match (hypothesis: near-zero\u2014standard evals miss it). Random-direction and matched-family-feature controls.\n4) HEAD-TO-HEAD CAUSAL TEST: In the SAME pipeline, calibrate that FAMILY-identity features ARE steerable (family-switch/preservation success per unit steer, specificity), then test whether ANY compact intervention (TopK/Ordered SAE feature, difference-of-means deep-vs-shallow direction, activation patching from true ancestral-node sequences, and family-orthogonalized directions) can move a sequence to the correct evolutionary depth\u2014matching the ML ancestor\u2014while HOLDING family identity fixed. This is the operational definition of a genuine evolutionary-time handle. Report sign, dose-response, specificity; contrast depth vs family steerability effect sizes. Benchmark the best steering handle head-to-head against (a) VAE latent interpolation (the known-failing baseline) and (b) classical ML ASR (the gold standard) on ancestor-recovery accuracy. Test whether Ordered/Matryoshka SAEs recover partial depth steerability that TopK lacks.\n5) INTERPRETATION: Test whether located 'depth' units encode a genuine evolutionary-distance concept (invariant to which family, tracking tree-depth / branch length) vs shallow proxies (e.g., overall sequence conservation, gap content), using controlled pairs that dissociate depth from family and from raw conservation (same depth across families; different depths within a family). Auto-label top features (max-activating contexts; enrichment for slowly- vs rapidly-evolving positions).\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal PHYLOGENETIC-CONSISTENCY score (depth-probe confidence / feature coherence read from the steered activation). Measure whether it flags silently-misplaced ancestors (family-OK, depth-wrong) better than output-side signals: report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors, and compute saved by flagging non-placeable designs before running full pplacer + ML re-analysis (or, downstream, before wet-lab ancestral-protein resurrection). Emit human-readable diagnoses ('sequence stayed in the kinase family but placed at depth 0.1 vs requested 0.6\u2014not a plausible deep ancestor').\n7) ROBUSTNESS & CROSS-SUBSTRATE: Repeat across two ESM-2 sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two layers, and several protein families spanning slow/fast evolutionary rates; optionally replicate the core phenomenon on Evo 2 (DNA/species phylogeny) using its curved-manifold representation. Report where the blind spot and (non-)steerability findings are stable vs artifact, and whether depth steerability is worse for deep (ancient) than shallow (recent) targets.\nEVALUATION METRICS: evolutionary-depth blind-spot magnitude & dose-response and SILENT-MISPLACEMENT RATE (primary phenomenon, via ML-ASR + pplacer oracle); family-vs-depth-change correlation (blind spot); family vs depth probe AUROC/R^2 (Location); depth-steering ancestor-recovery accuracy / placement match vs family-steering and vs VAE-interpolation and ML-ASR baselines, head-to-head effect sizes (Causal Intervention); depth-feature invariance to family and to raw conservation (Interpretation); internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE and compute saved (auditing); robustness across sizes/SAEs/families/substrates.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The evolutionary-depth oracle relies on classical ML ancestral reconstruction and tree placement, which are themselves inferential (uncertain trees, indel handling); we mitigate by curating well-resolved Pfam families with high-support trees, using simulated-evolution benchmarks (known ground-truth ancestors) alongside empirical ones, and reporting results stratified by tree-support/oracle confidence. Claims concern model-internal, oracle-verified evolutionary-time controllability, not biological ground truth of extinct proteins\u2014stated explicitly. 2) The depth-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (TopK/Ordered SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching from true ancestral-node sequences, family-orthogonalized directions), sweeping strength, requiring the model to natively span a depth range, and head-to-head calibrating that FAMILY features ARE steerable in the same pipeline\u2014so a null depth result is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuinely steerable evolutionary-time handle) is equally publishable and would be a striking, useful advance for interpretable ASR and ancestral-protein engineering. 3) Family identity and evolutionary depth are partly entangled (deep ancestors of a family are less family-typical); the partial-correlation feature selection and dissociation pairs (step 5) isolate genuinely family-independent depth signal, and we report where separation is impossible. 4) The known VAE-interpolation failure suggests depth steering may fail for decoding-fidelity reasons rather than representation reasons; we distinguish these by comparing steering (which uses the pLM's own decoder) against probe decodability and by the activation-patching test, and treat 'decodable but not generatively usable' as itself the informative finding. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, raw-activation probes (including the flat phylogenetic subspace), and random-direction controls. 6) An internal phylogenetic-consistency probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (family-steerable, depth-decodable-but-weakly-steerable) and the audit/compute-saving payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 7) If evolutionary-depth steering unexpectedly succeeds, it overturns the hypothesis but yields a strong positive finding that pLMs offer a controllable evolutionary-time design lever for ancestral reconstruction."}}
