act intervention (TopK/Ordered SAE feature, difference-of-means deep-vs-shallow direction, activation patching from true ancestral-node sequences, and family-orthogonalized directions) can move a sequence to the correct evolutionary depth—matching the ML ancestor—while HOLDING family identity fixed. This is the operational definition of a genuine evolutionary-time handle. Report sign, dose-response, specificity; contrast depth vs family steerability effect sizes. Benchmark the best steering handle head-to-head against (a) VAE latent interpolation (the known-failing baseline) and (b) classical ML ASR (the gold standard) on ancestor-recovery accuracy. Test whether Ordered/Matryoshka SAEs recover partial depth steerability that TopK lacks.\n5) INTERPRETATION: Test whether located 'depth' units encode a genuine evolutionary-distance concept (invariant to which family, tracking tree-depth / branch length) vs shallow proxies (e.g., overall sequence conservation, gap content), using controlled pairs that dissociate depth from family and from raw conservation (same depth across families; different depths within a family). Auto-label top features (max-activating contexts; enrichment for slowly- vs rapidly-evolving positions).\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal PHYLOGENETIC-CONSISTENCY score (depth-probe confidence / feature coherence read from the steered activation). Measure whether it flags silently-misplaced ancestors (family-OK, depth-wrong) better than output-side signals: report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors, and compute saved by flagging non-placeable designs before running full pplacer + ML re-analysis (or, downstream, before wet-lab ancestral-protein resurrection). Emit human-readable diagnoses ('sequence stayed in the kinase family but placed at depth 0.1 vs requested 0.6—not a plausible deep ancestor').\n7) ROBUSTNESS & CROSS-SUBSTRATE: Repeat across two ESM-2 sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two layers, and several protein families spanning slow/fast evolutionary rates; optionally replicate the core phenomenon on Evo 2 (DNA/species phylogeny) using its curved-manifold representation. Report where the blind spot and (non-)steerability findings are stable vs artifact, and whether depth steerability is worse for deep (ancient) than shallow (recent) targets.\nEVALUATION METRICS: evolutionary-depth blind-spot magnitude & dose-response and SILENT-MISPLACEMENT RATE (primary phenomenon, via ML-ASR + pplacer oracle); family-vs-depth-change correlation (blind spot); family vs depth probe AUROC/R^2 (Location); depth-steering ancestor-recovery accuracy / placement match vs family-steering and vs VAE-interpolation and ML-ASR baselines, head-to-head effect sizes (Causal Intervention); depth-feature invariance to family and to raw conservation (Interpretation); internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE and compute saved (auditing); robustness across sizes/SAEs/families/substrates.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) The evolutionary-depth oracle relies on classical ML ancestral reconstruction and tree placement, which are themselves inferential (uncertain trees, indel handling); we mitigate by curating well-resolved Pfam families with high-support trees, using simulated-evolution benchmarks (known ground-truth ancestors) alongside empirical ones, and reporting results stratified by tree-support/oracle confidence. Claims concern model-internal, oracle-verified evolutionary-time controllability, not biological ground truth of extinct proteins—stated explicitly. 2) The depth-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (TopK/Ordered SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching from true ancestral-node sequences, family-orthogonalized directions), sweeping strength, requiring the model to natively span a depth range, and head-to-head calibrating that FAMILY features ARE steerable in the same pipeline—so a null depth result is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuinely steerable evolutionary-time handle) is equally publishable and would be a striking, useful advance for interpretable ASR and ancestral-protein engineering. 3) Family identity and evolutionary depth are partly entangled (deep ancestors of a family are less family-typical); the partial-correlation feature selection and dissociation pairs (step 5) isolate genuinely family-independent depth signal, and we report where separation is impossible. 4) The known VAE-interpolation failure suggests depth steering may fail for decoding-fidelity reasons rather than representation reasons; we distinguish these by comparing steering (which uses the pLM's own decoder) against probe decodability and by the activation-patching test, and treat 'decodable but not generatively usable' as itself the informative finding. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, raw-activation probes (including the flat phylogenetic subspace), and random-direction controls. 6) An internal phylogenetic-consistency probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (family-steerable, depth-decodable-but-weakly-steerable) and the audit/compute-saving payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 7) If evolutionary-depth steering unexpectedly succeeds, it overturns the hypothesis but yields a strong positive finding that pLMs offer a controllable evolutionary-time design lever for ancestral reconstruction."}

