Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "steering_hysteresis", "Title": "No Undo Button: Does Interpretable-Feature Steering Leave Irreversible Scars on Scientific Generative Designs?", "Short Hypothesis": "Interpretable-feature steering in scientific generative models is not a reversible knob: applying a feature-steer and then removing it (or applying the exact opposite steer) does NOT return generation to its original design, but leaves a persistent 'scar'\u2014residual, often unintended, changes in the finished object. We hypothesize this steering hysteresis is systematic (not noise), larger for iterative/autoregressive generation where an early perturbation contaminates all downstream decisions, and predicted by how early the steer is applied and by feature-decoder overlap\u2014making the naive 'undo/adjust' workflow that designers assume silently unreliable.", "Related Work": "Nearly all interpretability-guided design work (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; 'Interpreting and Steering PLMs through SAEs' 2025; the chemistry/diffusion SAE steering papers; DLM-Scope 2026; 'Enabling Precise Topic Alignment via SAEs' 2025) treats a steering vector as a reversible, dial-like control: add it to move a property, implicitly assuming you can dial it back or fine-tune around it. Steering is always evaluated in the FORWARD direction (does the target property appear?) and never for REVERSIBILITY. Separately, a physics/biology literature on representation irreversibility (Nonequilibrium Latent Cycles 2025; Measuring irreversibility from learned representations 2023; LENS 2025) shows learned dynamics can break detailed balance and be time-asymmetric, and the position paper 'Beyond Equilibrium' (2025) argues generative processes are often irreversible\u2014but none connects this to feature-steering controllability of DESIGNS. No prior work (a) tests whether steer-then-unsteer returns a scientific generator to its original design, (b) quantifies steering hysteresis as a controllability-reliability failure, or (c) ties hysteresis magnitude to steer timing and feature geometry. This reframes the ubiquitous assumption of reversible control\u2014distinct from all my prior proposals (compositionality, off-mode reach, side-effect drift), which assume a single forward steer and never study the undo operation.", "Abstract": "Designers using interpretable-feature steering treat it like a knob: add a feature vector to increase a property, and\u2014implicitly\u2014dial it back or adjust around it if you overshoot. We test whether this reversibility assumption holds for scientific generative models, and find reason to expect it does not. Because generation is a nonlinear, path-dependent process (especially autoregressive decoding and iterative diffusion), a steering perturbation applied partway through generation contaminates every subsequent decision, so removing the steer\u2014or applying the exact opposite steer\u2014need not return the model to where it started. We call this steering hysteresis: the finished object after a steer-then-unsteer cycle differs persistently from the unsteered baseline, leaving unintended 'scars' in properties the designer never touched. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders, we (1) MEASURE hysteresis via a steer-then-reverse protocol, quantifying residual property drift and sequence/graph distance from baseline after the steer is nominally undone; (2) test whether hysteresis is SYSTEMATIC (stable direction across seeds) rather than sampling noise, using paired controls; (3) INTERPRET the mechanism by testing whether hysteresis magnitude is predicted by how early in generation the steer is applied and by feature-decoder overlap; and (4) AUDIT reliability, showing that the naive undo workflow silently fails and that a simple early-vs-late scheduling fix reduces scars. We predict substantial, systematic, timing-dependent hysteresis, invisible to forward-only steering evaluations\u2014establishing reversibility as a necessary, currently-ignored axis for controllable design.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Property oracles with exact/cheap evaluators: proteins\u2014alpha-helix/beta-sheet content (DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY, molecular weight, motifs (HMMER/regex); molecules\u2014logP, QED, ring/aromatic count, functional groups (RDKit SMARTS). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + SA for molecules. Distance metrics: sequence identity / edit distance and embedding distance for proteins; Tanimoto/canonical-SMILES match and graph edit distance for molecules.\n2) STEER-THEN-REVERSE PROTOCOL (core): For a bank of property-linked SAE features, run three matched generations from the SAME initial condition (seed/prompt/masked template): (A) unsteered baseline; (B) steered forward with feature f at strength s for part of generation; (C) steer-then-reverse\u2014apply +s then remove the steer (or apply -s of equal magnitude) after a switch point. Define HYSTERESIS = residual difference between (C) and (A) in (i) the target property, (ii) all off-target battery properties, and (iii) object distance (identity/Tanimoto). Compare two reversal modes: 'remove steer' (drop the vector) and 'symmetric reverse' (apply exact opposite). Primary metric: fraction of the forward property change that PERSISTS after reversal, and off-target scar magnitude.\n3) SYSTEMATIC-VS-NOISE TEST: Establish a noise floor by comparing two independent unsteered runs from perturbed seeds (baseline stochastic drift). Test whether steer-then-reverse hysteresis significantly exceeds this floor and whether the residual DIRECTION is consistent across seeds (a systematic scar) vs random (mixed-effects model; report effect size, CIs, and a random-direction control matched in norm).\n4) MECHANISM\u2014TIMING & GEOMETRY (Location + Causal Intervention): Vary the SWITCH POINT (early vs late in autoregressive/iterative generation) and test the hypothesis that early steers leave larger scars (early perturbation contaminates all downstream tokens/denoising steps). Regress hysteresis on switch-point and on feature-decoder overlap (cosine of the feature's decoder direction with other property directions), testing whether these predict scar magnitude better than concept-correlation. Include a near-instantaneous (single-step) steer as a low-hysteresis control.\n5) BLIND-SPOT + FIX (Decision Auditing): Show forward-only evaluations miss hysteresis: correlate forward property gain and validity with residual scar (hypothesis: near-zero, i.e., a 'successful' steer can still leave large scars). Test a simple mitigation\u2014applying/removing the steer only within a late causal window (or ramping strength gradually)\u2014and measure scar reduction at matched forward property gain, tracing a control-vs-reversibility frontier.\n6) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, autoregressive vs masked-infilling protein generation, and multiple properties; report where hysteresis is stable vs artifact, and whether iterative/autoregressive generation shows more hysteresis than a single forward pass.\nEVALUATION METRICS: persistent-fraction of forward property change and off-target scar magnitude (primary); hysteresis-above-noise-floor effect size and residual-direction consistency (systematic-vs-noise); timing/geometry regression R^2 and predictor comparison vs concept-correlation (mechanism); forward-gain-vs-scar correlation (blind spot); scar reduction from scheduling fix at matched gain (mitigation); robustness across models/domains/generation modes.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) In-silico oracles (ESMFold, DSSP, RDKit, HMMER) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal controllability and design reproducibility, not experimental function\u2014stated explicitly. 2) Generation is inherently stochastic, so distinguishing hysteresis from sampling noise is essential; we mitigate with a carefully matched noise-floor baseline (independent unsteered runs), paired same-seed comparisons, direction-consistency tests, and random-direction controls\u2014if hysteresis does not exceed the floor, that is itself a clean, publishable positive result establishing that steering IS reversible. 3) 'Remove steer' vs 'symmetric reverse' may behave differently; we report both rather than assume equivalence. 4) Hysteresis could be trivially explained by validity collapse; we restrict core analysis to the regime where reversed objects remain valid and report scars separately. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound both scar and geometry; controlled with multiple SAE variants, layers, and difference-of-means baselines. 6) The timing/geometry predictors may be property- or model-specific; we test multiple properties, sizes, and generation modes and report where they fail. 7) The scheduling fix may reduce forward property gain; we quantify the control-vs-reversibility tradeoff rather than assume a free lunch."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'steering_hysteresis', 'Title': 'No Undo Button: Does Interpretable-Feature Steering Leave Irreversible Scars on Scientific Generative Designs?', 'Short Hypothesis': "Interpretable-feature steering in scientific generative models is not a reversible knob: applying a feature-steer and then removing it (or applying the exact opposite steer) does NOT return generation to its original design, but leaves a persistent 'scar'—residual, often unintended, changes in the finished object. We hypothesize this steering hysteresis is systematic (not noise), larger for iterative/autoregressive generation where an early perturbation contaminates all downstream decisions, and predicted by how early the steer is applied and by feature-decoder overlap—making the naive 'undo/adjust' workflow that designers assume silently unreliable.", 'Related Work': "Nearly all interpretability-guided design work (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; 'Interpreting and Steering PLMs through SAEs' 2025; the chemistry/diffusion SAE steering papers; DLM-Scope 2026; 'Enabling Precise Topic Alignment via SAEs' 2025) treats a steering vector as a reversible, dial-like control: add it to move a property, implicitly assuming you can dial it back or fine-tune around it. Steering is always evaluated in the FORWARD direction (does the target property appear?) and never for REVERSIBILITY. Separately, a physics/biology literature on representation irreversibility (Nonequilibrium Latent Cycles 2025; Measuring irreversibility from learned representations 2023; LENS 2025) shows learned dynamics can break detailed balance and be time-asymmetric, and the position paper 'Beyond Equilibrium' (2025) argues generative processes are often irreversible—but none connects this to feature-steering controllability of DESIGNS. No prior work (a) tests whether steer-then-unsteer returns a scientific generator to its original design, (b) quantifies steering hysteresis as a controllability-reliability failure, or (c) ties hysteresis magnitude to steer timing and feature geometry. This reframes the ubiquitous assumption of reversible control—distinct from all my prior proposals (compositionality, off-mode reach, side-effect drift), which assume a single forward steer and never study the undo operation.", 'Abstract': "Designers using interpretable-feature steering treat it like a knob: add a feature vector to increase a property, and—implicitly—dial it back or adjust around it if you overshoot. We test whether this reversibility assumption holds for scientific generative models, and find reason to expect it does not. Because generation is a nonlinear, path-dependent process (especially autoregressive decoding and iterative diffusion), a steering perturbation applied partway through generation contaminates every subsequent decision, so removing the steer—or applying the exact opposite steer—need not return the model to where it started. We call this steering hysteresis: the finished object after a steer-then-unsteer cycle differs persistently from the unsteered baseline, leaving unintended 'scars' in properties the designer never touched. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders, we (1) MEASURE hysteresis via a steer-then-reverse protocol, quantifying residual property drift and sequence/graph distance from baseline after the steer is nominally undone; (2) test whether hysteresis is SYSTEMATIC (stable direction across seeds) rather than sampling noise, using paired controls; (3) INTERPRET the mechanism by testing whether hysteresis magnitude is predicted by how early in generation the steer is applied and by feature-decoder overlap; and (4) AUDIT reliability, showing that the naive undo workflow silently fails and that a simple early-vs-late scheduling fix reduces scars. We predict substantial, systematic, timing-dependent hysteresis, invisible to forward-only steering evaluations—establishing reversibility as a necessary, currently-ignored axis for controllable design.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Property oracles with exact/cheap evaluators: proteins—alpha-helix/beta-sheet content (DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY, molecular weight, motifs (HMMER/regex); molecules—logP, QED, ring/aromatic count, functional groups (RDKit SMARTS). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + SA for molecules. Distance metrics: sequence identity / edit distance and embedding distance for proteins; Tanimoto/canonical-SMILES match and graph edit distance for molecules.\n2) STEER-THEN-REVERSE PROTOCOL (core): For a bank of property-linked SAE features, run three matched generations from the SAME initial condition (seed/prompt/masked template): (A) unsteered baseline; (B) steered forward with feature f at strength s for part of generation; (C) steer-then-reverse—apply +s then remove the steer (or apply -s of equal magnitude) after a switch point. Define HYSTERESIS = residual difference between (C) and (A) in (i) the target property, (ii) all off-target battery properties, and (iii) object distance (identity/Tanimoto). Compare two reversal modes: 'remove steer' (drop the vector) and 'symmetric reverse' (apply exact opposite). Primary metric: fraction of the forward property change that PERSISTS after reversal, and off-target scar magnitude.\n3) SYSTEMATIC-VS-NOISE TEST: Establish a noise floor by comparing two independent unsteered runs from perturbed seeds (baseline stochastic drift). Test whether steer-then-reverse hysteresis significantly exceeds this floor and whether the residual DIRECTION is consistent across seeds (a systematic scar) vs random (mixed-effects model; report effect size, CIs, and a random-direction control matched in norm).\n4) MECHANISM—TIMING & GEOMETRY (Location + Causal Intervention): Vary the SWITCH POINT (early vs late in autoregressive/iterative generation) and test the hypothesis that early steers leave larger scars (early perturbation contaminates all downstream tokens/denoising steps). Regress hysteresis on switch-point and on feature-decoder overlap (cosine of the feature's decoder direction with other property directions), testing whether these predict scar magnitude better than concept-correlation. Include a near-instantaneous (single-step) steer as a low-hysteresis control.\n5) BLIND-SPOT + FIX (Decision Auditing): Show forward-only evaluations miss hysteresis: correlate forward property gain and validity with residual scar (hypothesis: near-zero, i.e., a 'successful' steer can still leave large scars). Test a simple mitigation—applying/removing the steer only within a late causal window (or ramping strength gradually)—and measure scar reduction at matched forward property gain, tracing a control-vs-reversibility frontier.\n6) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, autoregressive vs masked-infilling protein generation, and multiple properties; report where hysteresis is stable vs artifact, and whether iterative/autoregressive generation shows more hysteresis than a single forward pass.\nEVALUATION METRICS: persistent-fraction of forward property change and off-target scar magnitude (primary); hysteresis-above-noise-floor effect size and residual-direction consistency (systematic-vs-noise); timing/geometry regression R^2 and predictor comparison vs concept-correlation (mechanism); forward-gain-vs-scar correlation (blind spot); scar reduction from scheduling fix at matched gain (mitigation); robustness across models/domains/generation modes.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) In-silico oracles (ESMFold, DSSP, RDKit, HMMER) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal controllability and design reproducibility, not experimental function—stated explicitly. 2) Generation is inherently stochastic, so distinguishing hysteresis from sampling noise is essential; we mitigate with a carefully matched noise-floor baseline (independent unsteered runs), paired same-seed comparisons, direction-consistency tests, and random-direction controls—if hysteresis does not exceed the floor, that is itself a clean, publishable positive result establishing that steering IS reversible. 3) 'Remove steer' vs 'symmetric reverse' may behave differently; we report both rather than assume equivalence. 4) Hysteresis could be trivially explained by validity collapse; we restrict core analysis to the regime where reversed objects remain valid and report scars separately. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound both scar and geometry; controlled with multiple SAE variants, layers, and difference-of-means baselines. 6) The timing/geometry predictors may be property- or model-specific; we test multiple properties, sizes, and generation modes and report where they fail. 7) The scheduling fix may reduce forward property gain; we quantify the control-vs-reversibility tradeoff rather than assume a free lunch."}

