Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "oracle_transfer_gap_steering", "Title": "Steering to the Judge, Not the Property: Does Interpretable-Feature Control of Scientific Designs Survive a Change of Oracle?", "Short Hypothesis": "Interpretable-feature steering produces property gains that are partly an artifact of the specific in-silico oracle used to guide/evaluate it: a large fraction of the measured gain evaporates when the SAME steered objects are scored by an independent, more trustworthy oracle. We hypothesize this 'oracle-transfer gap' is systematic (worse at higher steering strength, worse for oracles that share features with the steered model), and that a cheap, intervention-free internal signature\u2014the alignment of the steering direction with oracle-agnostic physicochemical features versus oracle-specific idiosyncrasy\u2014predicts which steers will transfer across oracles.", "Related Work": "Text-LLM steering studies establish that steering is fragile and trades off with capability (Mind the Performance Gap 2026; The Rogue Scalpel 2025; 'When the Coffee Feature Activates on Coffins' 2026), that interpretability weakly predicts steering utility (Does Higher Interpretability Imply Better Utility 2025), and that steering reliability has geometric predictors (Understanding Unreliability of Steering Vectors 2026). Scientific interpretability-of-generation work (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; chemistry-LM SAEs 2025) reports property GAINS measured by a single in-silico oracle and never asks whether those gains are oracle-dependent artifacts. Sim-to-real work (Intervention Design for Sim2Real 2020; SourceNet 2026) studies train/deploy domain gaps for predictors, not steering-of-generators. My own prior proposals each audit one property class against one better oracle; NONE isolates the general, cross-domain phenomenon that a steering-induced gain is contingent on the CHOICE of measuring oracle, quantifies how much survives an oracle swap, or identifies an internal signature predicting oracle-robustness. This reframes steering reliability as a MEASUREMENT-TRANSFER problem: the danger is not overshoot but that the model steers toward the quirks of whatever judge scored it.", "Abstract": "Interpretable-feature steering is judged almost universally by one number: an in-silico oracle says the target property increased. But scientific properties are measured by many imperfect instruments, and a control method that genuinely changed the property should look good to any competent judge. We test whether steering-induced gains are robust to a change of oracle, or whether steering silently optimizes the idiosyncrasies of the specific judge that guided it\u2014an 'oracle-transfer gap.' Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders, we steer property-linked features and, crucially, score the SAME steered objects with multiple independent oracles per property (e.g., for hydrophobicity/logP: distinct empirical estimators; for stability: a learned head vs a physics-based estimator; for structure content: DSSP-on-ESMFold vs an alternative predictor). We (1) quantify the oracle-transfer gap = fraction of the guiding-oracle gain that vanishes under an independent oracle, and its dose-response with steering strength; (2) test whether the gap is larger when the guiding oracle shares representational features with the steered model (feature leakage) than for a physics-based judge; (3) LOCATE and validate an intervention-free robustness signature\u2014the projection of the steering direction onto oracle-agnostic physicochemical features vs oracle-specific directions\u2014that predicts per-steer transfer; and (4) deploy a robustness-gated / multi-oracle-orthogonalized steering controller that keeps only oracle-invariant gains. We predict substantial, systematic oracle-transfer gaps invisible to single-oracle evaluation, a cheap predictor of robust steers, and a simple recipe for control that generalizes across judges.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLE PANELS: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, since TopK features can be decodable-but-inert) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. For EACH target property assemble a PANEL of >=3 independent oracles that measure the same physical quantity by different means: molecules\u2014logP via Crippen/RDKit vs an ML logP model vs a group-contribution estimator; molecular size via exact MW vs a learned descriptor; polarity via TPSA vs an empirical solubility model; proteins\u2014hydrophobicity via GRAVY vs a learned hydrophobicity head; secondary-structure content via DSSP-on-ESMFold vs an alternative structure predictor; stability via a fine-tuned ML head vs a physics-based (FoldX/Rosetta-style) estimator. Classify each oracle as 'model-adjacent' (learned, potentially sharing features with the steered LM's training signal) or 'physics-based' (mechanistic, independent). Validity gates: RDKit-validity+SA for molecules; ESMFold pLDDT+pseudo-perplexity for proteins.\n2) FEATURE BANK: Identify interpretable property-linked SAE features via concept-correlation/probe AUROC on held-out natural objects (correlation computed against the panel-AVERAGE to avoid biasing selection toward one oracle). Include difference-of-means directions as a non-SAE baseline. Assemble ~30-50 features per domain.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON\u2014THE ORACLE-TRANSFER GAP: For each feature, designate one panel member as the GUIDING oracle (the one steering is optimized/calibrated against), sweep steering strength, and generate N objects per level. Measure the property gain under the guiding oracle AND under each held-out panel oracle on the SAME objects. Quantify the ORACLE-TRANSFER GAP = 1 - (mean held-out-oracle gain)/(guiding-oracle gain), its dose-response with strength, and the SILENT-OVERCLAIM RATE = fraction of steers judged successful by the guiding oracle but not by the panel majority. Rotate which oracle guides to avoid single-oracle idiosyncrasy. Random-direction and matched-non-property-feature controls.\n4) FEATURE-LEAKAGE TEST: Test the hypothesis that the gap is larger when guiding with a MODEL-ADJACENT oracle (shares features with the steered LM) than a PHYSICS-BASED oracle. Compare gap magnitude across guiding-oracle types (mixed-effects model; report effect size, CIs). Show the blind spot: correlate validity/pseudo-perplexity and the guiding oracle's own confidence with the true transfer gap (hypothesis: near-zero\u2014standard evals miss oracle-dependence).\n5) LOCATION + PREDICTOR OF ROBUSTNESS (intervention-free): For each steering direction compute (a) its projection onto ORACLE-AGNOSTIC physicochemical feature subspace (features that predict the property consistently across ALL panel oracles) versus ORACLE-SPECIFIC directions (features predicting only the guiding oracle's residual after regressing out the panel consensus); (b) decoder-direction overlap; (c) concept-correlation. Regress per-steer oracle-transfer gap on these (Spearman/R^2, bootstrap CIs); test whether oracle-agnostic-alignment predicts robustness far better than concept-correlation. Validate as a pre-flight filter: precision@k for selecting oracle-robust steers, and compute saved by skipping non-transferring steers before running the full oracle panel.\n6) MITIGATION\u2014MULTI-ORACLE-ORTHOGONALIZED / GATED STEERING: Construct a steering direction aligned with the oracle-CONSENSUS subspace (or gate out steers whose oracle-agnostic-alignment is low). Compare against naive single-oracle steering on a gain-vs-transfer frontier: consensus property gain (panel majority), transfer gap, validity, novelty/diversity. Hypothesis: consensus-aligned steering Pareto-dominates naive steering on robust gain at matched validity, sacrificing only the spurious guiding-oracle-specific portion.\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two layers, and multiple properties; report which properties have the largest oracle-transfer gaps and where the predictor/mitigation are stable vs artifact.\nEVALUATION METRICS: oracle-transfer gap & dose-response and SILENT-OVERCLAIM RATE (primary phenomenon); model-adjacent-vs-physics guiding-oracle gap difference (feature-leakage); output-side-check-vs-gap correlation (blind spot); robustness-predictor Spearman/R^2 and precision@k of the pre-flight filter, compute saved (Location/predictor); consensus-steering gain-vs-transfer frontier area and robust-gain retention (mitigation); robustness across models/SAEs/properties.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) All oracles are in-silico; the claim is deliberately about ROBUSTNESS ACROSS INDEPENDENT JUDGES, not wet-lab truth\u2014but a gain that fails to transfer across competent oracles is prima facie untrustworthy, and a physics-based panel member reduces circularity. We report the panel's inter-oracle agreement on NATURAL objects first to bound expected gap from oracle noise alone. 2) Some panel oracles may themselves be biased/correlated, shrinking apparent gaps; we choose maximally mechanistically-distinct oracles and report their pairwise correlation so the gap is interpreted relative to oracle independence. 3) The transfer gap could partly reflect validity collapse rather than oracle-specific optimization; we restrict core analysis to valid objects and report the gap separately. 4) The robustness predictor may be property- or model-specific; we test multiple properties, sizes, and SAE variants and report where it fails. 5) Defining the 'oracle-agnostic subspace' depends on the panel; a richer panel gives a cleaner consensus\u2014we quantify sensitivity to panel size. 6) SAE quality/layer choice could confound; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, difference-of-means baselines, and random-direction controls. 7) If gains transfer cleanly across oracles (small gap), that is a valuable positive result establishing that interpretable-feature steering targets genuine physics rather than judge idiosyncrasy."}}
