Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "locality_count_generalization_design", "Title": "More Motifs Than Ever Seen: Does Conditional Score Locality Govern Count Generalization in Scientific Generative Design?", "Short Hypothesis": "Scientific generative models can design objects with MORE independent functional elements (motifs, catalytic sites, functional groups) than appeared in any training example ('count generalization') only when their internal conditional computation is LOCAL \u2014 i.e., each element's placement depends on a spatially/sequentially sparse neighborhood rather than the global configuration. We hypothesize (i) count generalization succeeds or fails per model/property, (ii) an intervention-free locality signature of the element-placement feature predicts which case holds, and (iii) causally enforcing locality (masking non-local dependencies of the placement feature) unlocks count generalization that a non-local model otherwise fails, at preserved validity.", "Related Work": "A recent line proves that compositional/length generalization in conditional diffusion models is equivalent to 'local conditional scores' (sparse pixel and conditioner dependencies) and shows a causal locality intervention restores length generalization in CLEVR/image models (Local Mechanisms of Compositional Generalization, 2025; Kamb & Ganguli 2024; Niedoba 2024). Compositional-generalization theory (kernel theory 2024; identifiability/object-centric 2023; complexity-based compositionality 2024) and disentanglement-for-C-OoD work (CLIP 2024, data-diversity 2025) all study this in vision/text. Separately, scientific-model interpretability (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; ProtoMech 2026; Circuits/Features/Heuristics in Molecular Transformers 2025) discovers and steers features for single-element properties but NEVER studies count generalization, never measures conditional-score locality, and never tests locality as its causal mechanism. My prior proposals audit compositional INTERFERENCE and off-mode reach but assume a fixed target count and never touch the locality-count-generalization link. This proposal is the first to (a) transfer the locality<->count-generalization theory into scientific sequence/graph design, (b) measure conditional locality of interpretable element-placement features, and (c) causally enforce locality to unlock designs exceeding training element counts \u2014 a Location + Causal Intervention mechanism claim in a high-stakes domain.", "Abstract": "Real scientific design frequently demands packing MORE independent functional elements into one object than nature or the training set ever showed: an enzyme with several catalytic sites, a protein with multiple binding motifs, a molecule with many decorated functional groups. Whether generative models can do this \u2014 'count generalization' \u2014 and why, is unknown. Recent theory for conditional diffusion proves that compositional/length generalization is equivalent to conditional score LOCALITY: each element's placement depending on a sparse local neighborhood rather than the global configuration. We transfer this theory into scientific design and test it mechanistically. Using protein language models (ESM-2), a SMILES chemical language model, and a masked-diffusion protein/molecule generator with sparse autoencoders, we (1) LOCATE interpretable element-placement features (features that fire when a motif/functional group is instantiated) and measure their CONDITIONAL LOCALITY \u2014 how sparsely each placement depends on distant positions/other elements, via ablation of distal context and Jacobian sparsity; (2) establish the count-generalization PHENOMENON by requesting objects with k functional elements for k beyond the training-count support and measuring valid success against hard oracles (HMMER/regex motifs, RDKit SMARTS); (3) TEST the hypothesis that locality predicts count-generalization success across models/properties; and (4) CAUSALLY ENFORCE locality \u2014 masking the placement feature's non-local dependencies during generation \u2014 and test whether it unlocks higher-count valid designs that the unmodified (non-local) model fails, at preserved validity. Results would give a mechanistic, actionable principle for pushing scientific generators past their training element counts.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M), a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM, and (for the causal locality intervention, which needs conditional scores) a masked-diffusion protein/molecule generator with accessible per-step activations. Train TopK SAEs on the residual stream / denoiser activations at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Define ELEMENTS with hard, countable oracles: proteins \u2014 discrete motif instances (zinc fingers, catalytic-triad-like motifs, specified short motifs) via HMMER/regex, counted per sequence; molecules \u2014 functional-group instances (specified SMARTS: hydroxyls, halogens, aromatic rings) counted per molecule. Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + synthetic accessibility for molecules. Record, from the training corpus, the DISTRIBUTION of element counts per object (the training-count support) so 'beyond training count' is well-defined.\n2) LOCATE ELEMENT-PLACEMENT FEATURES & MEASURE LOCALITY (Location): Identify SAE features that fire at the instantiation of a target element type (concept-correlation / probe AUROC on held-out objects, localized to the element's positions). For each placement feature, measure its CONDITIONAL LOCALITY: (a) distal-context ablation \u2014 how much the feature's activation / the placement decision changes when distant positions or other already-placed elements are ablated/shuffled (local features barely change); (b) Jacobian sparsity \u2014 the spatial/sequential decay profile of the gradient of the placement logit w.r.t. positions (a locality radius). Output a per-feature locality score.\n3) COUNT-GENERALIZATION PHENOMENON: For each element type, request objects with k elements for k spanning within-support up to BEYOND the max training count (via count-conditioned prompting / iterative element insertion / feature-steered placement). Measure VALID-COUNT-SUCCESS = fraction of generations that contain exactly k valid, correctly-formed elements AND pass validity gates, as a function of k. Establish per-model, per-property whether count generalization succeeds or breaks down beyond the training-count support, and the SILENT-COUNT-FAILURE profile (wrong count / degenerate / invalid). Random-count and unconditioned controls.\n4) LOCALITY-PREDICTS-GENERALIZATION TEST (core): Across element types, models, and layers, regress the count-generalization boundary (largest k with valid-success above threshold, normalized by training-max-count) on the placement feature's locality score (Spearman/R^2, bootstrap CIs). Hypothesis: higher locality => stronger count generalization, and locality predicts it better than concept-correlation or single-element steerability. Report per-property and pooled.\n5) CAUSAL LOCALITY ENFORCEMENT (Causal Intervention, the mechanism claim): For a model/property that FAILS count generalization and whose placement feature is measured NON-local, intervene to enforce locality during generation \u2014 mask the placement feature's dependence on distal positions/other-element features (restrict its effective receptive field to the measured local radius) at each generation/denoising step. Test whether this restores valid count generalization (raises valid-count-success beyond training-max-count) relative to the unmodified model, at matched validity. Controls: (a) enforcing locality on a random non-placement feature (should not help), (b) a model already local (should be unaffected), (c) matched-norm random masking. Report the count-generalization gain and validity retention, and whether the required locality radius matches the Location-measured one.\n6) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, autoregressive vs masked-diffusion generation, and multiple element types; report where locality<->count-generalization holds vs breaks, and whether it is stronger for elements that are physically independent (well-separated motifs) than for coupled ones.\nEVALUATION METRICS: valid-count-success vs k and count-generalization boundary (phenomenon); per-feature locality score (Location); locality-vs-boundary Spearman/R^2 and comparison vs concept-correlation/steerability baselines (core test); count-generalization gain and validity retention from causal locality enforcement, and radius-match to measured locality (Causal Intervention); robustness across models/domains/element types.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) In-silico oracles (HMMER/regex, RDKit SMARTS, ESMFold) are proxies for wet-lab function; claims concern model-internal count controllability and its locality mechanism, not experimental viability \u2014 stated explicitly. Element COUNTS, however, are exact/objective structural facts, which strengthens the core measurement. 2) The causal-locality-enforcement intervention (masking distal dependencies of a feature) is nontrivial to implement cleanly, especially for autoregressive models; we focus the causal test on the masked-diffusion generator where conditional scores are directly accessible (matching the source theory) and treat autoregressive results as corroborating, reporting implementation fidelity. 3) The locality<->count-generalization link may hold for physically-independent elements but fail for coupled ones (where non-locality is genuinely required); we stratify by element independence and report this as an informative boundary rather than a failure. 4) Count-conditioned generation may be hard to elicit; we use three elicitation methods (prompting, iterative insertion, feature-steered placement) and report the strongest fair comparison. 5) SAE quality/layer choice could confound both locality measurement and placement-feature identification; controlled with multiple layers, difference-of-means baselines, raw-activation probes, and random-feature controls. 6) If count generalization does NOT track locality (e.g., it is driven by data-count diversity instead), that is itself a valuable, publishable result adapting/refuting the diffusion-locality theory for scientific design and pointing to data diversity as the operative cause. 7) A positive causal result would provide a concrete, cheap recipe (locality masking) for exceeding training element counts \u2014 high practical value if it holds."}}
