Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "epistemic_selfsignal_design", "Title": "Does the Generator Know It's Guessing? An Internal Epistemic Self-Signal That Governs Design Quality in Scientific Generative Models", "Short Hypothesis": "Scientific sequence/graph generative models carry a compact, interpretable INTERNAL EPISTEMIC self-signal during generation that encodes how uncertain the model is about the object it is producing, and\u2014mirroring the shared uncertainty/correctness circuits found in text LLMs (SCIURus)\u2014this self-signal is causally SHARED with actual design quality: it fires low exactly when the finished object is novel/out-of-distribution and low-quality (unfoldable, invalid, off-property), it is better calibrated than output likelihood/perplexity, and steering it changes both the model's internal confidence and the true quality of generated designs together.", "Related Work": "Two literatures meet for the first time. (1) Text-LLM internal-uncertainty work shows models 'know what they know': SCIURus (2025) finds factuality and uncertainty share the same circuits; 'Internal Causal Mechanisms Robustly Predict OOD Behaviors' (2025) shows causal internal features predict correctness under shift; 'Do I Know This Entity?' (2024) and P(IK)-probe work map self-knowledge features. ALL are in text and concern factual QA, not generation of scientific objects. (2) Scientific interpretability-of-generation (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; chemistry-LM SAEs 2025; single-cell SAEs 2024) discovers/steers features for TARGET PROPERTIES and never studies whether the generator internally represents its own epistemic uncertainty about the object it is building, nor whether that self-signal is causally tied to design quality. Molecular-OOD work (MOOD 2024) shows external PREDICTOR calibration collapses under covariate shift but says nothing about a GENERATOR's internal self-signal. No prior work (a) locates an internal epistemic self-signal inside a scientific generator, (b) tests whether it is a shared circuit with true design quality (the SCIURus phenomenon transferred to a new, high-stakes object class where quality is objectively verifiable), or (c) uses it as a calibrated, causally-validated confidence readout for design. This is distinct from all my prior proposals, which audit controllability of a specific property; here the object of study is the model's self-knowledge and its causal link to quality.", "Abstract": "Generative models for proteins and molecules routinely emit confident-looking designs that are actually invalid, unfoldable, or property-wrong\u2014especially in the novel, out-of-distribution regions where design is most valuable. In text language models, recent work shows that uncertainty and correctness are computed by shared internal circuits: the model 'knows what it knows.' We ask whether scientific generators possess an analogous internal epistemic self-signal during generation, whether it is causally shared with the true quality of the object being produced, and whether it can serve as a calibrated confidence readout that output likelihood cannot. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders, we (1) LOCATE candidate epistemic self-signal features by probing intermediate activations for how novel/OOD and how ultimately-valid the object being generated is, verified by hard oracles (ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity, RDKit-validity) and novelty measures (identity/Tanimoto to the training set); (2) test the SHARED-CIRCUIT hypothesis via causal ablation and patching\u2014does perturbing the located self-signal move BOTH the model's internal confidence AND the true design quality together, as SCIURus finds for text?; (3) evaluate the self-signal as a CALIBRATED confidence readout, comparing its ability to flag low-quality/OOD designs against likelihood, perplexity, and a black-box activation probe; and (4) deploy it as an epistemic-gated generation controller that abstains or resamples on low-confidence designs, measuring valid-yield-per-compute. We predict a genuine, shared, better-calibrated self-signal\u2014recasting interpretability as a source of trustworthy design confidence.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, since TopK features can be decodable-but-inert) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Define DESIGN QUALITY with hard oracles: proteins\u2014ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity + non-degeneracy (foldable & valid); molecules\u2014RDKit-validity + synthetic accessibility + non-degeneracy. Define NOVELTY/OOD: identity/edit distance to nearest training protein (mmseqs2/BLAST) or Tanimoto/nearest-neighbor distance to nearest training molecule. Generate a large corpus (~20k/model) spanning in-distribution (typical sampling) and OOD (high-temperature, steered-off-mode, rare-scaffold-conditioned) regimes so both quality and novelty vary widely.\n2) LOCATE THE EPISTEMIC SELF-SIGNAL (Location): At multiple generation-progress fractions, probe SAE features and raw activations to predict (a) eventual design quality (valid vs invalid/low-quality) and (b) novelty/OOD-ness of the object being generated. Report probe AUROC/R^2 vs progress and layer; identify a compact self-signal feature set (information-based selection). Test whether an epistemic signal is decodable BEFORE the object is finished, and whether the SAME features predict both quality and novelty (a first sign of sharing). Compare against a raw-activation probe baseline.\n3) SHARED-CIRCUIT TEST (Causal Intervention, core, transferring SCIURus): Ablate/patch the located self-signal features and measure whether BOTH (i) the model's internal confidence read-out (the self-signal's own value / a P(valid) probe) AND (ii) the TRUE finished-object quality (oracle) move together and correlated, as the shared-circuit hypothesis predicts. Conversely, patch self-signal activations from high-confidence contexts into low-confidence generations and test whether internal confidence rises AND real quality rises. Controls: random-direction and matched-property-feature ablations (should not couple confidence and quality), and a specificity check that ablating a pure target-property feature does NOT move the epistemic signal. Report sign, dose-response, and the confidence\u2013quality coupling correlation under intervention.\n4) CALIBRATION AS A CONFIDENCE READOUT (Decision Auditing): Evaluate the intervention-free self-signal (read mid-generation) as a predictor of low-quality/OOD designs. Metrics: AUROC and calibration/ECE for flagging invalid/off-property/novel-but-broken designs, vs output-side baselines\u2014generation likelihood, pseudo-perplexity/token-entropy, RDKit-validity heuristics\u2014and vs a black-box raw-activation probe. Critically, stratify by NOVELTY: test the hypothesis that the self-signal stays calibrated in the OOD regime where likelihood/perplexity fail (mirroring MOOD's predictor-calibration collapse), i.e., the internal signal 'knows' it is guessing on novel designs.\n5) EPISTEMIC-GATED CONTROLLER (payoff): Deploy the self-signal as a generation-time gate\u2014abstain/resample when internal confidence is low. Sweep the threshold to trace a valid-yield-per-compute frontier; compare against run-to-completion, likelihood/perplexity gating, and a black-box probe gate. Report valid+in-quality objects per unit compute, false-abort rate, and diversity/novelty retention (ensure gating does not merely collapse to memorized in-distribution outputs).\n6) INTERPRETATION & ROBUSTNESS (Unit Interpretation): Auto-label the self-signal features (max-activating partial generations; do they fire on frustrated partial folds / chemically inconsistent prefixes?). Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, and two layers; report where the self-signal, the shared-circuit coupling, and the OOD-calibration advantage are stable vs artifact, and whether the coupling weakens for subtle vs gross failures.\nEVALUATION METRICS: self-signal probe AUROC/R^2 vs generation progress and shared-quality-and-novelty prediction (Location); confidence\u2013quality coupling under ablation/patching and specificity (shared-circuit Causal Intervention); calibration/ECE and AUROC vs baselines, stratified by novelty (Decision Auditing); valid-yield-per-compute frontier, false-abort rate, diversity retention (controller); feature interpretation labels; robustness across models/SAEs/layers.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) In-silico quality oracles (ESMFold, RDKit) are proxies for wet-lab function; claims concern model-internal epistemic self-knowledge and design reliability, not experimental viability\u2014stated explicitly. 2) The shared-circuit claim requires distinguishing genuine coupling from a trivial 'ablation breaks everything' artifact; we mitigate with random-direction and matched-property-feature controls, and a specificity test that property-feature ablation leaves the epistemic signal intact while epistemic-signal ablation couples confidence and quality\u2014if coupling is non-specific, we report that as a negative result. 3) The self-signal may merely track OOD-ness rather than a genuine correctness representation; the novelty-stratified calibration test and the causal coupling test separate 'detects novelty' from 'predicts quality', and both are informative. 4) An internal SAE-based self-signal might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; even so, interpretability (naming what the signal fires on), the shared-circuit causal finding, and the OOD-calibration/compute-saving payoff are the contributions, reported honestly. 5) Gating could bias outputs toward memorized in-distribution designs; we explicitly measure diversity/novelty retention. 6) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, raw-activation probes, and random-direction controls. 7) If no shared, better-calibrated self-signal exists, that is a clean, publishable negative result establishing that scientific generators\u2014unlike text LLMs\u2014do NOT 'know what they know' about their own designs, a safety-relevant finding."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'epistemic_selfsignal_design', 'Title': "Does the Generator Know It's Guessing? An Internal Epistemic Self-Signal That Governs Design Quality in Scientific Generative Models", 'Short Hypothesis': "Scientific sequence/graph generative models carry a compact, interpretable INTERNAL EPISTEMIC self-signal during generation that encodes how uncertain the model is about the object it is producing, and—mirroring the shared uncertainty/correctness circuits found in text LLMs (SCIURus)—this self-signal is causally SHARED with actual design quality: it fires low exactly when the finished object is novel/out-of-distribution and low-quality (unfoldable, invalid, off-property), it is better calibrated than output likelihood/perplexity, and steering it changes both the model's internal confidence and the true quality of generated designs together.", 'Related Work': "Two literatures meet for the first time. (1) Text-LLM internal-uncertainty work shows models 'know what they know': SCIURus (2025) finds factuality and uncertainty share the same circuits; 'Internal Causal Mechanisms Robustly Predict OOD Behaviors' (2025) shows causal internal features predict correctness under shift; 'Do I Know This Entity?' (2024) and P(IK)-probe work map self-knowledge features. ALL are in text and concern factual QA, not generation of scientific objects. (2) Scientific interpretability-of-generation (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; chemistry-LM SAEs 2025; single-cell SAEs 2024) discovers/steers features for TARGET PROPERTIES and never studies whether the generator internally represents its own epistemic uncertainty about the object it is building, nor whether that self-signal is causally tied to design quality. Molecular-OOD work (MOOD 2024) shows external PREDICTOR calibration collapses under covariate shift but says nothing about a GENERATOR's internal self-signal. No prior work (a) locates an internal epistemic self-signal inside a scientific generator, (b) tests whether it is a shared circuit with true design quality (the SCIURus phenomenon transferred to a new, high-stakes object class where quality is objectively verifiable), or (c) uses it as a calibrated, causally-validated confidence readout for design. This is distinct from all my prior proposals, which audit controllability of a specific property; here the object of study is the model's self-knowledge and its causal link to quality.", 'Abstract': "Generative models for proteins and molecules routinely emit confident-looking designs that are actually invalid, unfoldable, or property-wrong—especially in the novel, out-of-distribution regions where design is most valuable. In text language models, recent work shows that uncertainty and correctness are computed by shared internal circuits: the model 'knows what it knows.' We ask whether scientific generators possess an analogous internal epistemic self-signal during generation, whether it is causally shared with the true quality of the object being produced, and whether it can serve as a calibrated confidence readout that output likelihood cannot. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders, we (1) LOCATE candidate epistemic self-signal features by probing intermediate activations for how novel/OOD and how ultimately-valid the object being generated is, verified by hard oracles (ESMFold pLDDT, pseudo-perplexity, RDKit-validity) and novelty measures (identity/Tanimoto to the training set); (2) test the SHARED-CIRCUIT hypothesis via causal ablation and patching—does perturbing the located self-signal move BOTH the model's internal confidence AND the true design quality together, as SCIURus finds for text?; (3) evaluate the self-signal as a CALIBRATED confidence readout, comparing its ability to flag low-quality/OOD designs against likelihood, perplexity, and a black-box activation probe; and (4) deploy it as an epistemic-gated generation controller that abstains or resamples on low-confidence designs, measuring valid-yield-per-compute. We predict a genuine, shared, better-calibrated self-signal—recasting interpretability as a source of trustworthy design confidence.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, since TopK features can be decodable-but-inert) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Define DESIGN QUALITY with hard oracles: proteins—ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity + non-degeneracy (foldable & valid); molecules—RDKit-validity + synthetic accessibility + non-degeneracy. Define NOVELTY/OOD: identity/edit distance to nearest training protein (mmseqs2/BLAST) or Tanimoto/nearest-neighbor distance to nearest training molecule. Generate a large corpus (~20k/model) spanning in-distribution (typical sampling) and OOD (high-temperature, steered-off-mode, rare-scaffold-conditioned) regimes so both quality and novelty vary widely.\n2) LOCATE THE EPISTEMIC SELF-SIGNAL (Location): At multiple generation-progress fractions, probe SAE features and raw activations to predict (a) eventual design quality (valid vs invalid/low-quality) and (b) novelty/OOD-ness of the object being generated. Report probe AUROC/R^2 vs progress and layer; identify a compact self-signal feature set (information-based selection). Test whether an epistemic signal is decodable BEFORE the object is finished, and whether the SAME features predict both quality and novelty (a first sign of sharing). Compare against a raw-activation probe baseline.\n3) SHARED-CIRCUIT TEST (Causal Intervention, core, transferring SCIURus): Ablate/patch the located self-signal features and measure whether BOTH (i) the model's internal confidence read-out (the self-signal's own value / a P(valid) probe) AND (ii) the TRUE finished-object quality (oracle) move together and correlated, as the shared-circuit hypothesis predicts. Conversely, patch self-signal activations from high-confidence contexts into low-confidence generations and test whether internal confidence rises AND real quality rises. Controls: random-direction and matched-property-feature ablations (should not couple confidence and quality), and a specificity check that ablating a pure target-property feature does NOT move the epistemic signal. Report sign, dose-response, and the confidence–quality coupling correlation under intervention.\n4) CALIBRATION AS A CONFIDENCE READOUT (Decision Auditing): Evaluate the intervention-free self-signal (read mid-generation) as a predictor of low-quality/OOD designs. Metrics: AUROC and calibration/ECE for flagging invalid/off-property/novel-but-broken designs, vs output-side baselines—generation likelihood, pseudo-perplexity/token-entropy, RDKit-validity heuristics—and vs a black-box raw-activation probe. Critically, stratify by NOVELTY: test the hypothesis that the self-signal stays calibrated in the OOD regime where likelihood/perplexity fail (mirroring MOOD's predictor-calibration collapse), i.e., the internal signal 'knows' it is guessing on novel designs.\n5) EPISTEMIC-GATED CONTROLLER (payoff): Deploy the self-signal as a generation-time gate—abstain/resample when internal confidence is low. Sweep the threshold to trace a valid-yield-per-compute frontier; compare against run-to-completion, likelihood/perplexity gating, and a black-box probe gate. Report valid+in-quality objects per unit compute, false-abort rate, and diversity/novelty retention (ensure gating does not merely collapse to memorized in-distribution outputs).\n6) INTERPRETATION & ROBUSTNESS (Unit Interpretation): Auto-label the self-signal features (max-activating partial generations; do they fire on frustrated partial folds / chemically inconsistent prefixes?). Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, and two layers; report where the self-signal, the shared-circuit coupling, and the OOD-calibration advantage are stable vs artifact, and whether the coupling weakens for subtle vs gross failures.\nEVALUATION METRICS: self-signal probe AUROC/R^2 vs generation progress and shared-quality-and-novelty prediction (Location); confidence–quality coupling under ablation/patching and specificity (shared-circuit Causal Intervention); calibration/ECE and AUROC vs baselines, stratified by novelty (Decision Auditing); valid-yield-per-compute frontier, false-abort rate, diversity retention (controller); feature interpretation labels; robustness across models/SAEs/layers.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) In-silico quality oracles (ESMFold, RDKit) are proxies for wet-lab function; claims concern model-internal epistemic self-knowledge and design reliability, not experimental viability—stated explicitly. 2) The shared-circuit claim requires distinguishing genuine coupling from a trivial 'ablation breaks everything' artifact; we mitigate with random-direction and matched-property-feature controls, and a specificity test that property-feature ablation leaves the epistemic signal intact while epistemic-signal ablation couples confidence and quality—if coupling is non-specific, we report that as a negative result. 3) The self-signal may merely track OOD-ness rather than a genuine correctness representation; the novelty-stratified calibration test and the causal coupling test separate 'detects novelty' from 'predicts quality', and both are informative. 4) An internal SAE-based self-signal might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; even so, interpretability (naming what the signal fires on), the shared-circuit causal finding, and the OOD-calibration/compute-saving payoff are the contributions, reported honestly. 5) Gating could bias outputs toward memorized in-distribution designs; we explicitly measure diversity/novelty retention. 6) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, raw-activation probes, and random-direction controls. 7) If no shared, better-calibrated self-signal exists, that is a clean, publishable negative result establishing that scientific generators—unlike text LLMs—do NOT 'know what they know' about their own designs, a safety-relevant finding."}

