Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "control_authority_vs_sampling_noise",
"Title": "Signal or Seed Lottery? Whether Interpretable-Feature Steering Actually Controls Scientific Designs or Just Nudges a Distribution Swamped by Sampling Noise",
"Short Hypothesis": "Interpretable-feature steering of scientific generative models is routinely reported as a mean property shift, but we hypothesize that for a large fraction of features the property VARIANCE induced by ordinary sampling stochasticity (seed/temperature) dwarfs the steering-induced mean shift, so a single steered generation is a seed lottery rather than deterministic control. We further hypothesize an intervention-free 'control-authority' score (steer-shift-to-sampling-spread ratio, estimable from a tiny sample) predicts which steers give reproducible per-object control versus which are effectively noise, and that this reframes when steering can be trusted for real one-shot design.",
"Related Work": "All steering-of-generation work in science reports AGGREGATE mean property shifts: InterPLM (2024), ProtSAE (2025), Steering Protein Language Models/ASPO (2025), CB-pLM (2024), Automated Neuron Labelling (2025), Circuits/Features/Heuristics in Molecular Transformers (2025), Steering Vector Fields for Molecular Generation (2025), and Physics Steering (2025). Crucially, every one of these quantifies success as the average change in a target property across many generations and never separates the steering signal from the sampling-noise floor at the level of an individual design. Text-LLM work on steering-vector unreliability (Understanding the Unreliability of Steering Vectors 2026) studies cross-prompt/method fragility but not the signal-vs-sampling-noise decomposition, and not for scientific objects with objective oracles. The Steering-Vector-Fields paper even notes larger shifts come 'at the expense of validity' but never measures per-generation reproducibility. No prior work (a) decomposes steering-induced mean shift against the sampling-induced property variance to compute a per-feature control-authority ratio, (b) shows that many celebrated steers have authority near or below 1 (seed-lottery regime), or (c) provides a cheap predictor of which steers deliver reproducible one-shot control. This is distinct from my prior blind-spot proposals (which contrast property classes) by asking a stochasticity/reproducibility question about steering ITSELF.",
"Abstract": "Interpretability-guided design steers a feature and reports that the target property increased on average. But real design is often one-shot: you generate a design and use it. We ask whether a single steered generation actually delivers the intended property, or whether the property you get is dominated by which random seed you drew. Scientific generative models sample stochastically, so any target property already has a baseline VARIANCE across seeds and temperatures; steering adds a mean SHIFT. We hypothesize that for many interpretable features the shift is small relative to this sampling spread, placing steering in a seed-lottery regime where per-object control is illusory despite a real average effect. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders and exact/cheap property oracles (DSSP/ESMFold, GRAVY, molecular weight; RDKit logP, QED, ring counts), we (1) decompose, per feature, the steering-induced mean shift against the sampling-induced property variance to compute a Control-Authority ratio (Cohen's-d-style shift/spread); (2) establish the seed-lottery phenomenon by measuring the fraction of features whose authority is below a per-object-reliability threshold, and the resulting one-shot success rate; (3) test whether an intervention-free control-authority score estimated from a tiny sample predicts reproducible one-shot control better than the reported mean shift; and (4) show a practical fix\u2014a small best-of-n selection budget guided by an internal property readout\u2014that converts low-authority steers into reliable one-shot designs, tracing an authority-vs-selection-cost frontier. Results recast steering evaluation from mean shifts to per-design reliability.",
"Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Properties with cheap/exact oracles: proteins\u2014alpha-helix/beta-sheet content (DSSP on ESMFold), GRAVY, molecular weight; molecules\u2014logP, QED, ring/aromatic count (RDKit). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity for proteins; RDKit-validity + SA for molecules. Assemble ~30-50 interpretable, property-linked features per domain (concept-correlation/probe AUROC) plus difference-of-means directions as a baseline.\n2) NOISE FLOOR + SHIFT (core decomposition): For each feature/property, at fixed steering strength generate N objects across many seeds (and a small temperature sweep) UNSTEERED and STEERED. Measure the sampling-induced property VARIANCE (unsteered spread across seeds) and the steering-induced MEAN SHIFT. Compute the CONTROL-AUTHORITY ratio = mean shift / sampling std (a standardized effect size), and the per-object OVERLAP = fraction of steered generations whose property does NOT clear the unsteered distribution's threshold. Random-direction control matched in norm to bound spurious shift.\n3) SEED-LOTTERY PHENOMENON: Report the distribution of control-authority across features and the fraction in the seed-lottery regime (authority below the threshold needed for reliable one-shot control). Compute ONE-SHOT SUCCESS RATE = probability a single steered draw hits the target-property band, and contrast it with the AGGREGATE mean-shift the steering literature would report\u2014quantifying the gap between 'works on average' and 'works once'. Sweep steering strength to test whether higher strength buys authority or just trades it for validity collapse (report the authority-vs-validity frontier).\n4) CHEAP PREDICTOR OF AUTHORITY (intervention-free / small-sample): Test whether control-authority estimated from a TINY sample (e.g., 8-16 steered + 8-16 unsteered draws) predicts the full-sample authority and one-shot success (Spearman/R^2, bootstrap CIs), giving a pre-flight reliability check. Also test whether authority is predicted by intervention-free geometry (decoder-direction norm relative to residual-stream norm; feature-decoder overlap) better than by the reported mean shift or concept-correlation.\n5) PRACTICAL FIX\u2014SELECTION BUDGET: For low-authority steers, apply best-of-n generation ranked by a cheap internal property readout (a probe on the steered activation) and/or the oracle, and measure how one-shot success rises with n. Trace the AUTHORITY-vs-SELECTION-COST frontier: how many extra samples convert a seed-lottery steer into reliable control, versus high-authority steers that need none. Compare selection guided by an internal probe vs by the external oracle (compute vs reliability).\n6) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, and multiple properties; report where the seed-lottery regime and the authority predictor are stable vs artifact, and whether autoregressive generation shows lower authority (more accumulated noise) than masked-infilling.\nEVALUATION METRICS: control-authority ratio distribution and seed-lottery fraction (primary phenomenon); one-shot success rate vs reported aggregate mean shift (the gap); authority-vs-validity frontier; small-sample and geometric predictor Spearman/R^2 and precision@k for flagging unreliable steers; selection-budget one-shot-success-vs-n frontier and compute cost; robustness across models/SAEs/properties/generation modes.",
"Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) In-silico oracles (ESMFold, DSSP, RDKit) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal per-design controllability and reproducibility, not experimental function\u2014stated explicitly, though property values are objective given the oracle. 2) Control-authority depends on the chosen target-property band width; we report authority as a distribution over reasonable bands and use a standardized effect-size that is band-agnostic for the core claim. 3) Higher steering strength could raise authority; we sweep strength fully and report the authority-vs-validity frontier so the seed-lottery finding is not an artifact of a single weak strength. 4) The small-sample authority predictor may be noisy; we quantify its calibration and the minimal sample size needed, and compare to intervention-free geometric predictors. 5) The best-of-n fix trades compute for reliability and depends on internal-probe quality; we report the frontier rather than claim a free lunch, and compare internal-probe vs oracle-guided selection. 6) SAE quality/layer choice could confound; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, difference-of-means baselines, and random-direction controls. 7) If most steers turn out to have high authority (deterministic one-shot control), that is a valuable positive result establishing that interpretable-feature steering is reliable at the level of individual designs, not just on average."
}}
