Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "interpolation_path_validity", "Title": "Cliffs on the Morphing Path: Do Interpretable-Feature Interpolations Between Two Valid Designs Traverse Valid, Monotone Ground?", "Short Hypothesis": "When a designer morphs one valid scientific object into another by interpolating in the interpretable SAE feature space of a frozen foundation-model generator, the intermediate designs are NOT reliably valid nor monotone in the property being morphed: the path silently crosses validity 'cliffs' (invalid/unfoldable/unsynthesizable intermediates) and exhibits non-monotone property overshoot, even though both endpoints are valid and the endpoints' property values bracket the intermediates'. We further hypothesize that interpolating in the SAE feature space stays valid and monotone MORE often than interpolating in the raw activation space, and that a cheap intervention-free path-curvature/off-manifold signature predicts where a given interpolation will break.", "Related Work": "Latent-space interpolation and manifold traversal for design is a mature line: ACAI (2018) regularizes autoencoders for realistic interpolation; STONED (2021), ChemFlow (2024), ChemSpacE (2022), ChemoVerse (2020), and Navigating Chemical Space with Latent Flows all traverse or interpolate WHOLE-OBJECT VAE/GAN latent spaces for molecule discovery; 'Revisiting Latent-Space Interpolation' (2021) benchmarks interpolation ALGORITHMS (linear vs slerp) on VAEs. Crucially, ALL of these (a) operate on bespoke VAE/GAN latent spaces, not the INTERPRETABLE SAE feature space of a frozen foundation-model generator, (b) evaluate interpolation by endpoint recovery, smoothness, or aggregate validity, and (c) never test whether property change along the path is MONOTONE or whether intermediates cross validity CLIFFS, nor tie failures to named interpretable features. SAE-steering work (InterPLM 2024, ProtSAE 2025, chemistry/RNA SAEs) only does single-point steering and never studies interpolation paths at all. My own prior proposals study endpoint steering, off-mode reach, and side-effect drift, but never the reliability of a design-MORPHING trajectory. This is the first to reframe interpolation-for-lead-optimization as a path-validity/monotonicity reliability question in interpretable feature space, and to contrast SAE-feature vs raw-activation interpolation on that axis.", "Abstract": "Lead optimization and design exploration routinely proceed by morphing one good design into another\u2014interpolating between two valid objects and picking intermediates. With interpretable sparse autoencoders (SAEs) now decomposing frozen protein and molecule generators into named feature directions, a natural and appealing workflow is to interpolate in this interpretable feature space, reading off which concepts turn on and off along the way. We ask whether that morphing path is trustworthy. Even when both endpoints are valid and their property values bracket the intermediates', the path between them need not stay on the valid manifold or change the property monotonically. We surface a behavioral phenomenon we call path cliffs: interpolations between two valid designs silently cross validity cliffs (invalid, unfoldable, or unsynthesizable intermediates) and exhibit non-monotone property overshoot, a failure invisible to endpoint checks. Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with SAEs and cheap oracles (ESMFold pLDDT, RDKit validity/logP, DSSP, GRAVY), we (1) quantify path-validity and monotonicity along interpolations between valid endpoint pairs; (2) test whether SAE-feature-space interpolation stays valid and monotone more often than raw-activation interpolation and simple sequence/graph edits; (3) LOCATE where cliffs occur and which interpretable features flip at the cliff; and (4) validate a cheap intervention-free path-curvature/off-manifold signature that predicts where an interpolation will break, plus a cliff-avoiding reparameterization. Results recast interpretable interpolation from an assumed-smooth design tool into an auditable, geometry-predictable one.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction and encode/decode round-trip fidelity (object -> activations -> SAE features -> reconstructed activations -> regenerated object). Property/validity oracles: proteins - ESMFold pLDDT (validity/foldability), DSSP secondary-structure content, GRAVY, molecular weight, pseudo-perplexity; molecules - RDKit-validity, logP, QED, ring count, synthetic accessibility. 2) ENDPOINT PAIRS: Sample many pairs of VALID objects whose property values bracket a target range (e.g., a low-logP and a high-logP valid molecule; a high-helix and a high-sheet valid protein). Encode both to SAE feature space; interpolate along the straight line (and slerp variant) between the two feature vectors; decode intermediates to objects at K steps. 3) CORE PHENOMENON - PATH CLIFFS: For each path measure (i) PATH-VALIDITY = fraction of intermediate steps that pass validity gates (the CLIFF RATE = fraction of paths with at least one invalid intermediate despite valid endpoints); (ii) MONOTONICITY = whether the morphed property changes monotonically from endpoint A to B, quantified by an overshoot/violation score (max deviation outside the endpoint-bracketed range, and count of sign reversals of the step-wise property delta). Report distributions across pairs and properties, and show the blind spot: endpoint validity and endpoint property values do NOT predict intermediate validity or monotonicity. 4) SAE-VS-RAW COMPARISON: Repeat interpolation in (a) SAE feature space, (b) raw residual-activation space, and (c) direct object-space interpolation (SMILES edit-path / sequence crossover) as baselines; test the hypothesis that SAE-feature interpolation has lower cliff rate and better monotonicity at matched path length (paired statistics across pairs; random-pair controls). 5) LOCATION - WHERE/WHAT BREAKS: For paths with cliffs, identify the step where validity drops and which interpretable SAE features flip most sharply at that step (auto-labeled), testing whether cliffs coincide with abrupt on/off switching of a small set of features vs smooth blends. 6) PREDICTOR + FIX (intervention-free): Compute a cheap path-geometry signature per path - local off-manifold distance of intermediates (distance to nearest natural-object embedding / SAE reconstruction error) and feature-path curvature (nonlinearity of feature activations along the interpolant) - and test whether it predicts cliff location/monotonicity violation (Spearman/R^2, precision@k), before decoding. Validate a cliff-avoiding reparameterization: reroute the path to minimize off-manifold distance (or take small on-manifold projected steps) and measure reduction in cliff rate and monotonicity violation at matched endpoint fidelity. 7) ROBUSTNESS: across both domains, two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, linear vs slerp, and multiple properties; report where cliffs and the predictor are stable vs artifact. EVALUATION METRICS: cliff rate and per-path monotonicity/overshoot score (primary phenomenon); endpoint-check-vs-intermediate-failure correlation (blind spot); SAE-vs-raw-vs-object-space cliff/monotonicity comparison (paired effect sizes); cliff-feature-flip coincidence (Location); path-geometry predictor Spearman/R^2 and precision@k, and cliff-avoiding-reparameterization improvement at matched fidelity (predictor + fix); round-trip fidelity sanity.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) In-silico oracles (ESMFold, RDKit, DSSP) are proxies for wet-lab truth; claims concern model-internal path reliability of interpretable interpolation, not experimental viability - stated explicitly, though validity/property values are objective given the oracle. 2) SAE encode/decode round-trip is lossy and could itself introduce apparent cliffs; we quantify round-trip fidelity and, as a control, compare against a no-SAE (raw-activation) interpolation that shares the same decode pathway to isolate SAE-specific effects. 3) Cliffs might trivially reflect endpoints being far apart in feature space; we control for path length and report cliff rate as a function of endpoint distance, and use random-endpoint-pair controls. 4) Monotonicity is only meaningful for a chosen morphed property; we restrict monotonicity claims to the single property being interpolated toward and report off-target drift separately. 5) The SAE-beats-raw hypothesis may be property- or model-specific; we test multiple properties, sizes, and SAE variants and report where it fails - a null (SAE no better than raw) is itself an informative, publishable result about the value of interpretable interpolation. 6) The path-geometry predictor may be underpowered; mitigated by many endpoint pairs and both domains. 7) The cliff-avoiding fix may lengthen paths or reduce endpoint fidelity; we quantify the tradeoff on a cliff-rate-vs-fidelity frontier rather than assume a free lunch. 8) If interpolation turns out reliably valid and monotone, that is a valuable positive result establishing interpretable feature-space interpolation as a safe design tool."}}
