Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "antiproperty_design_asymmetry", "Title": "Design Everything But That: Do Scientific Generators Suffer an Ironic-Rebound Asymmetry Between Positive and Negative Property Specification?", "Short Hypothesis": "Scientific generative models can causally steer TOWARD a specified structural element (positive design: 'include motif X') but cannot reliably steer to EXCLUDE it while designing everything else freely (negative/anti-property design: 'design a valid object that does NOT contain X'). We hypothesize a positive-vs-negative specification asymmetry: attempting to suppress an element via its interpretable feature triggers an ironic-rebound-like effect\u2014the forbidden element reappears at rates far above the unconditioned base rate\u2014because suppressing a concept requires internally activating it, and a compact 'exclude-X' handle does not exist the way an 'include-X' handle does; an intervention-free rebound signature predicts which anti-property requests will silently fail.", "Related Work": "Two literatures meet for the first time in scientific design. (1) Text/vision negation interpretability establishes that negation is uniquely hard: 'Strong hallucinations from negation' (2024) proves LMs' string distributions cannot respect logical negation; 'Don't Think of the White Bear' (2025) shows ironic REBOUND\u2014negation instructions paradoxically increase a forbidden concept's accessibility via middle-layer heads that amplify it; INLP (2020) removes a linguistic feature only by null-space PROJECTION (single-vector suppression is insufficient); RePS (2025) and SRS (2025) note suppression is systematically harder and more entangled than positive steering. ALL of this is in text/CLIP with fuzzy concepts. (2) Scientific interpretability-of-generation (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; chemistry-LM SAEs 2025) steers features TOWARD single positive properties and evaluates whether the property appears; none studies negative specification. My own prior intervention_leak proposal asks whether single-feature suppression of a property leaks through alternative DECODING routes; this proposal is different and complementary: it studies the POSITIVE-vs-NEGATIVE SPECIFICATION ASYMMETRY itself\u2014whether 'exclude X while designing everything else' is even a representable, steerable target class, tests the ironic-rebound analog (forbidden element appears ABOVE base rate under suppression), and contrasts it head-to-head against the trivially-easy positive case. This transfers a well-established text/human negation phenomenon into high-stakes scientific design (excluding toxicophores, off-target motifs, disallowed folds), where an anti-property that silently rebounds is a safety failure.", "Abstract": "Real scientific design constantly specifies what must be ABSENT: a drug candidate must exclude toxicophores, an enzyme must avoid an off-target binding motif, a designed protein must not fold into a disallowed conformation. Yet interpretability-guided design, and the human/text literature it borrows from, has focused almost entirely on POSITIVE specification\u2014steering a feature to ADD a desired element. Negation is known to be uniquely hard for language models: suppressing a concept requires internally activating it, producing ironic rebound where the forbidden concept becomes MORE accessible. We ask whether scientific generative models inherit this, surfacing a phenomenon we call the positive-vs-negative specification asymmetry: models causally steer toward a structural element easily, but attempting to EXCLUDE it while designing everything else freely fails\u2014often making the forbidden element appear ABOVE its unconditioned base rate (a design-space ironic rebound). Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders and hard, exactly-checkable oracles (RDKit SMARTS for toxicophores/functional groups; HMMER/regex for protein motifs; DSSP for disallowed structure), we (1) head-to-head measure positive (include-X) vs negative (exclude-X) specification success at matched steering budget; (2) test the rebound hypothesis\u2014does single-feature suppression push forbidden-element occurrence above base rate?; (3) LOCATE and causally test whether an 'exclude-X' handle exists (does subspace null-projection succeed where single-feature suppression fails?); and (4) AUDIT reliability\u2014does an intervention-free rebound signature (early amplification of the forbidden feature) predict silent anti-property failure? We predict a stark asymmetry, an ironic-rebound analog, and that projection-based exclusion plus a rebound audit partially restore reliable negative design.", "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, since TopK features can be decodable-but-inert) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Define ELEMENTS with hard, exact oracles so presence/absence is objective: molecules\u2014specific toxicophore/PAINS/reactive-group SMARTS and common functional groups (RDKit); proteins\u2014specific motifs (zinc finger, catalytic triad) via HMMER/regex and a disallowed secondary-structure target via DSSP-on-ESMFold. Validity gates: RDKit-validity+SA for molecules; ESMFold pLDDT+pseudo-perplexity for proteins. For each element, record the UNCONDITIONED base rate (fraction of ordinary generations that contain it) so rebound is measured relative to it. Select the SAE feature(s) best encoding each element via concept-correlation/probe AUROC.\n2) CORE PHENOMENON\u2014POSITIVE vs NEGATIVE ASYMMETRY (head-to-head): For each element, at matched steering budget run (a) POSITIVE design: steer the element's feature to INCLUDE it, measuring inclusion success + validity; (b) NEGATIVE design: steer to EXCLUDE it (suppress/clamp the feature negatively) while generating everything else freely, measuring EXCLUSION success = fraction of valid generations that do NOT contain the element. Primary metric: the ASYMMETRY GAP = positive success minus negative success at matched budget and validity, across elements and both domains (mixed-effects model, effect size, CIs). Random-direction and matched-non-element-feature controls.\n3) IRONIC-REBOUND TEST (transferring the white-bear effect): Compare forbidden-element occurrence under single-feature SUPPRESSION against the UNCONDITIONED base rate. The rebound hypothesis predicts suppression can push occurrence ABOVE base rate (suppressing the element requires activating it). Quantify the REBOUND RATE = fraction of elements whose suppression raises occurrence above base rate, and the rebound magnitude, swept over suppression strength (does stronger suppression paradoxically increase rebound, mirroring the cognitive-load finding?). Trace WHERE it rebounds during autoregressive/iterative generation and whether a small set of components re-amplifies the forbidden feature (a design-space analog of the middle-layer amplifier heads).\n4) LOCATION + CAUSAL TEST\u2014DOES AN 'EXCLUDE-X' HANDLE EXIST? Contrast three exclusion mechanisms: (a) single-feature negative steering (hypothesized to fail/rebound), (b) SUBSPACE NULL-PROJECTION\u2014project out the full concept subspace (top-k correlated feature directions / SVD subspace), the INLP analog, and (c) activation patching from element-free generations. Test whether projection succeeds where single-feature suppression fails, establishing that reliable negation requires removing a SUBSPACE, not adding a single 'not' vector\u2014the operational finding that no compact 'exclude-X' feature exists. Report exclusion success vs subspace dimension k, the minimal k to drive occurrence below base rate, and the validity cost.\n5) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free REBOUND SIGNATURE read mid-generation\u2014early amplification / coherence of the forbidden element's feature despite active suppression\u2014as an alarm that an anti-property request will silently fail. Measure AUROC/ECE for predicting eventual forbidden-element presence, vs output-side signals (validity, pseudo-perplexity, the suppression's apparent forward-pass success), and compute saved by flagging/aborting doomed negative-design requests before full generation + oracle checking. Emit human-readable diagnoses ('toxicophore feature rebounded at token 30 despite -3 clamp').\n6) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two layers, and multiple elements; report where the asymmetry, rebound, and projection-fix are stable vs artifact, and whether rebound is stronger for elements with higher base rate or richer feature redundancy.\nEVALUATION METRICS: positive-vs-negative ASYMMETRY GAP at matched budget/validity (primary); REBOUND RATE and magnitude vs base rate and vs suppression strength (ironic-rebound test); exclusion success vs subspace dimension k and single-feature-vs-projection-vs-patching comparison (Location/Causal); rebound-signature AUROC/ECE and compute saved (auditing); validity/novelty retention; robustness across models/SAEs/elements.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Element presence/absence is defined by hard, exact oracles (RDKit SMARTS, HMMER/regex, DSSP), a strength that makes the asymmetry and rebound objective; the main proxy is downstream 'harm/function', which we do not claim\u2014claims concern the objectively-checkable structural presence of forbidden elements, and we restrict to structural-alert-level elements with unambiguous detectors, responsibly avoiding synthesizable-threat detail. 2) The 'no exclude-X handle' claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple exclusion mechanisms (single-feature negative steering, subspace null-projection, activation patching), sweeping strength, and head-to-head confirming that POSITIVE inclusion succeeds in the same pipeline\u2014so exclusion failure is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable exclusion handle exists) is equally publishable. 3) Rebound could be confounded with generic off-distribution drift from strong suppression; random-direction and matched-non-element-feature controls, plus measuring occurrence strictly relative to base rate, isolate the rebound-specific effect. 4) Base rates for rare elements may be low, making rebound-above-base-rate easy to trigger trivially; we include elements spanning a range of base rates and report rebound as an effect size relative to base-rate uncertainty. 5) Subspace projection may reduce validity or interpretability; we quantify the exclusion-vs-validity frontier rather than assume a free lunch. 6) SAE quality/layer choice could confound; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, and difference-of-means baselines. 7) If negative specification turns out as easy as positive (small asymmetry, no rebound), that is a valuable positive result establishing that scientific generators handle anti-property design robustly\u2014unlike text LLMs on linguistic negation."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'antiproperty_design_asymmetry', 'Title': 'Design Everything But That: Do Scientific Generators Suffer an Ironic-Rebound Asymmetry Between Positive and Negative Property Specification?', 'Short Hypothesis': "Scientific generative models can causally steer TOWARD a specified structural element (positive design: 'include motif X') but cannot reliably steer to EXCLUDE it while designing everything else freely (negative/anti-property design: 'design a valid object that does NOT contain X'). We hypothesize a positive-vs-negative specification asymmetry: attempting to suppress an element via its interpretable feature triggers an ironic-rebound-like effect—the forbidden element reappears at rates far above the unconditioned base rate—because suppressing a concept requires internally activating it, and a compact 'exclude-X' handle does not exist the way an 'include-X' handle does; an intervention-free rebound signature predicts which anti-property requests will silently fail.", 'Related Work': "Two literatures meet for the first time in scientific design. (1) Text/vision negation interpretability establishes that negation is uniquely hard: 'Strong hallucinations from negation' (2024) proves LMs' string distributions cannot respect logical negation; 'Don't Think of the White Bear' (2025) shows ironic REBOUND—negation instructions paradoxically increase a forbidden concept's accessibility via middle-layer heads that amplify it; INLP (2020) removes a linguistic feature only by null-space PROJECTION (single-vector suppression is insufficient); RePS (2025) and SRS (2025) note suppression is systematically harder and more entangled than positive steering. ALL of this is in text/CLIP with fuzzy concepts. (2) Scientific interpretability-of-generation (InterPLM 2024; ProtSAE 2025; chemistry-LM SAEs 2025) steers features TOWARD single positive properties and evaluates whether the property appears; none studies negative specification. My own prior intervention_leak proposal asks whether single-feature suppression of a property leaks through alternative DECODING routes; this proposal is different and complementary: it studies the POSITIVE-vs-NEGATIVE SPECIFICATION ASYMMETRY itself—whether 'exclude X while designing everything else' is even a representable, steerable target class, tests the ironic-rebound analog (forbidden element appears ABOVE base rate under suppression), and contrasts it head-to-head against the trivially-easy positive case. This transfers a well-established text/human negation phenomenon into high-stakes scientific design (excluding toxicophores, off-target motifs, disallowed folds), where an anti-property that silently rebounds is a safety failure.", 'Abstract': "Real scientific design constantly specifies what must be ABSENT: a drug candidate must exclude toxicophores, an enzyme must avoid an off-target binding motif, a designed protein must not fold into a disallowed conformation. Yet interpretability-guided design, and the human/text literature it borrows from, has focused almost entirely on POSITIVE specification—steering a feature to ADD a desired element. Negation is known to be uniquely hard for language models: suppressing a concept requires internally activating it, producing ironic rebound where the forbidden concept becomes MORE accessible. We ask whether scientific generative models inherit this, surfacing a phenomenon we call the positive-vs-negative specification asymmetry: models causally steer toward a structural element easily, but attempting to EXCLUDE it while designing everything else freely fails—often making the forbidden element appear ABOVE its unconditioned base rate (a design-space ironic rebound). Using ESM-2 protein language models and a SMILES chemical language model with sparse autoencoders and hard, exactly-checkable oracles (RDKit SMARTS for toxicophores/functional groups; HMMER/regex for protein motifs; DSSP for disallowed structure), we (1) head-to-head measure positive (include-X) vs negative (exclude-X) specification success at matched steering budget; (2) test the rebound hypothesis—does single-feature suppression push forbidden-element occurrence above base rate?; (3) LOCATE and causally test whether an 'exclude-X' handle exists (does subspace null-projection succeed where single-feature suppression fails?); and (4) AUDIT reliability—does an intervention-free rebound signature (early amplification of the forbidden feature) predict silent anti-property failure? We predict a stark asymmetry, an ironic-rebound analog, and that projection-based exclusion plus a rebound audit partially restore reliable negative design.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) and a GPT-style SMILES chemical LM. Train TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, since TopK features can be decodable-but-inert) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. Define ELEMENTS with hard, exact oracles so presence/absence is objective: molecules—specific toxicophore/PAINS/reactive-group SMARTS and common functional groups (RDKit); proteins—specific motifs (zinc finger, catalytic triad) via HMMER/regex and a disallowed secondary-structure target via DSSP-on-ESMFold. Validity gates: RDKit-validity+SA for molecules; ESMFold pLDDT+pseudo-perplexity for proteins. For each element, record the UNCONDITIONED base rate (fraction of ordinary generations that contain it) so rebound is measured relative to it. Select the SAE feature(s) best encoding each element via concept-correlation/probe AUROC.\n2) CORE PHENOMENON—POSITIVE vs NEGATIVE ASYMMETRY (head-to-head): For each element, at matched steering budget run (a) POSITIVE design: steer the element's feature to INCLUDE it, measuring inclusion success + validity; (b) NEGATIVE design: steer to EXCLUDE it (suppress/clamp the feature negatively) while generating everything else freely, measuring EXCLUSION success = fraction of valid generations that do NOT contain the element. Primary metric: the ASYMMETRY GAP = positive success minus negative success at matched budget and validity, across elements and both domains (mixed-effects model, effect size, CIs). Random-direction and matched-non-element-feature controls.\n3) IRONIC-REBOUND TEST (transferring the white-bear effect): Compare forbidden-element occurrence under single-feature SUPPRESSION against the UNCONDITIONED base rate. The rebound hypothesis predicts suppression can push occurrence ABOVE base rate (suppressing the element requires activating it). Quantify the REBOUND RATE = fraction of elements whose suppression raises occurrence above base rate, and the rebound magnitude, swept over suppression strength (does stronger suppression paradoxically increase rebound, mirroring the cognitive-load finding?). Trace WHERE it rebounds during autoregressive/iterative generation and whether a small set of components re-amplifies the forbidden feature (a design-space analog of the middle-layer amplifier heads).\n4) LOCATION + CAUSAL TEST—DOES AN 'EXCLUDE-X' HANDLE EXIST? Contrast three exclusion mechanisms: (a) single-feature negative steering (hypothesized to fail/rebound), (b) SUBSPACE NULL-PROJECTION—project out the full concept subspace (top-k correlated feature directions / SVD subspace), the INLP analog, and (c) activation patching from element-free generations. Test whether projection succeeds where single-feature suppression fails, establishing that reliable negation requires removing a SUBSPACE, not adding a single 'not' vector—the operational finding that no compact 'exclude-X' feature exists. Report exclusion success vs subspace dimension k, the minimal k to drive occurrence below base rate, and the validity cost.\n5) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free REBOUND SIGNATURE read mid-generation—early amplification / coherence of the forbidden element's feature despite active suppression—as an alarm that an anti-property request will silently fail. Measure AUROC/ECE for predicting eventual forbidden-element presence, vs output-side signals (validity, pseudo-perplexity, the suppression's apparent forward-pass success), and compute saved by flagging/aborting doomed negative-design requests before full generation + oracle checking. Emit human-readable diagnoses ('toxicophore feature rebounded at token 30 despite -3 clamp').\n6) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across both domains, two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two layers, and multiple elements; report where the asymmetry, rebound, and projection-fix are stable vs artifact, and whether rebound is stronger for elements with higher base rate or richer feature redundancy.\nEVALUATION METRICS: positive-vs-negative ASYMMETRY GAP at matched budget/validity (primary); REBOUND RATE and magnitude vs base rate and vs suppression strength (ironic-rebound test); exclusion success vs subspace dimension k and single-feature-vs-projection-vs-patching comparison (Location/Causal); rebound-signature AUROC/ECE and compute saved (auditing); validity/novelty retention; robustness across models/SAEs/elements.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) Element presence/absence is defined by hard, exact oracles (RDKit SMARTS, HMMER/regex, DSSP), a strength that makes the asymmetry and rebound objective; the main proxy is downstream 'harm/function', which we do not claim—claims concern the objectively-checkable structural presence of forbidden elements, and we restrict to structural-alert-level elements with unambiguous detectors, responsibly avoiding synthesizable-threat detail. 2) The 'no exclude-X handle' claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple exclusion mechanisms (single-feature negative steering, subspace null-projection, activation patching), sweeping strength, and head-to-head confirming that POSITIVE inclusion succeeds in the same pipeline—so exclusion failure is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable exclusion handle exists) is equally publishable. 3) Rebound could be confounded with generic off-distribution drift from strong suppression; random-direction and matched-non-element-feature controls, plus measuring occurrence strictly relative to base rate, isolate the rebound-specific effect. 4) Base rates for rare elements may be low, making rebound-above-base-rate easy to trigger trivially; we include elements spanning a range of base rates and report rebound as an effect size relative to base-rate uncertainty. 5) Subspace projection may reduce validity or interpretability; we quantify the exclusion-vs-validity frontier rather than assume a free lunch. 6) SAE quality/layer choice could confound; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, and difference-of-means baselines. 7) If negative specification turns out as easy as positive (small asymmetry, no rebound), that is a valuable positive result establishing that scientific generators handle anti-property design robustly—unlike text LLMs on linguistic negation."}

