Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "periodicity_repeat_steering_blindspot",
  "Title": "Copy, Don't Just Contain: Do Interpretable Features Control the Periodic Repeat Structure of Generated Proteins, or Only Local Motif Content?",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Interpretable sparse-autoencoder features of protein language models afford causal, steerable control over LOCAL, single-position content (which residues/motifs appear), but NOT over PERIODIC REPEAT STRUCTURE\u2014the long-range copy-consistency that makes a sequence a genuine tandem-repeat protein (near-identical units repeated at a specified period and count). Consequently, steering a feature to 'make it repetitive' silently produces sequences with repeat-like local content but broken periodicity (wrong unit length, degraded copy-consistency, collapsed foldable repeat topology), a blind spot that local-content and validity checks miss but an exact repeat-detection oracle exposes; and an intervention-free internal periodicity-consistency signal flags it.",
  "Related Work": "All protein-LM interpretability/steering work targets LOCAL or single-position properties: InterPLM (2024) and 'Interpreting and Steering PLMs through SAEs' (2025) steer toward local motifs (zinc fingers, transmembrane regions, binding sites); 'Automated Neuron Labelling' (2025) and 'Steering Protein Language Models'/ASPO (2025) steer molecular weight, GRAVY, instability index, secondary-structure content and thermostability; CB-pLM (2024) controls per-position interpretable concepts. Every one evaluates whether a local property/motif appears\u2014none studies PERIODIC REPEAT STRUCTURE, a global, position-coupled property requiring copy-consistency across widely separated positions. Separately, a mature biology literature establishes tandem repeats as ubiquitous (present in ~14% of proteins), functionally important, and exactly detectable with well-established tools (RepeatsDB, CE-Symm, TRIPS; Tandem Repeats in Proteins survey 2015), and difficult even for humans/DNA to synthesize precisely (TReSR 2023). No prior work asks whether the periodic/repeat structure is a causally-steerable design lever, contrasts local-content steerability against periodicity steerability, surfaces the phenomenon that 'repetitive' steering silently breaks periodicity, or provides an internal periodicity audit. This introduces the local-content vs global-periodicity axis into interpretability-of-generation, is distinct from my prior fold/symmetry/epistasis/count proposals (repeats are an exact, countable, copy-consistency invariant, not a fold or an element count), and turns exact repeat detection into a mechanism-and-audit target.",
  "Abstract": "Tandem-repeat proteins\u2014sequences built from near-identical units repeated at a regular period\u2014make up roughly 14% of all proteins and underpin scaffolds, binders, and designable modular architectures. Building one requires not just repeat-like local content but long-range COPY-CONSISTENCY: the same unit reproduced faithfully at a specified period and count. Interpretability-guided protein design increasingly steers language-model features to install desired properties, but every demonstrated target is LOCAL (a motif, hydrophobicity, molecular weight). We ask whether interpretable features can causally control periodic repeat structure at all, or only local content. We surface a behavioral phenomenon we call the periodicity blind spot: steering a feature to make a sequence 'repetitive' reliably raises local repeat-like content while failing to enforce the correct period, unit count, and copy-consistency\u2014invisible to composition and validity checks but exposed exactly by repeat-detection tools (T-REKS/HHrepID/CE-Symm on ESMFold structures). Repeats are the ideal testbed because periodicity is an exact, cheap, position-coupled oracle. Using ESM-2 with sparse autoencoders, we (1) LOCATE candidate local-content features and candidate periodicity/copy-consistency features via probing; (2) CAUSALLY test the steerability of each, contrasting content control against periodicity control; (3) INTERPRET whether located periodicity units encode a genuine copy-consistency concept or shallow local-composition proxies; and (4) AUDIT reliability, testing whether an internal periodicity-consistency signal flags silently-broken repeats better than content or model likelihood. We predict content control succeeds, periodicity control is decodable-but-weakly-steerable, and a cheap internal audit catches broken repeats before costly structural validation.",
  "Experiments": "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) with TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, since the antibody-SAE literature shows TopK features can be decodable-but-inert) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. PERIODICITY oracles (exact, cheap): sequence-level repeat detection (T-REKS / HHrepID / XSTREAM) returning detected unit length (PERIOD), number of copies (COUNT), and COPY-CONSISTENCY (mean pairwise identity among detected units); plus a structural repeat check (CE-Symm on ESMFold-predicted structures) for foldable repeat topology. LOCAL-CONTENT oracles: amino-acid composition, motif/tract presence (regex/HMMER), and a shuffled-sequence control that preserves composition but destroys periodicity (isolating content from structure). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity. First confirm the model natively spans a meaningful RANGE of periodicity states (non-repetitive to well-formed repeats) so absence-of-control claims are meaningful; assemble a labeled corpus of repeat vs non-repeat proteins (RepeatsDB) and shuffled controls.\n2) FEATURE BANK & LOCATION: On held-out sequences, probe SAE features/raw activations for (a) LOCAL-CONTENT features (predict composition / motif presence), and (b) candidate PERIODICITY features (predict repeat presence, period, and copy-consistency, controlling for composition via partial correlation and via composition-matched shuffled controls). Report probe AUROC/R^2 for content vs periodicity; identify top candidate directions (SAE feature, difference-of-means between repeat vs shuffled-composition-matched sequences, regression-weight direction); compare against a raw-activation probe. Critically test whether periodicity/copy-consistency is decodable at all, independent of local composition.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON\u2014THE PERIODICITY BLIND SPOT (content-easy/periodicity-hard): For each candidate PERIODICITY feature, sweep steering strength and generate/infill N sequences per level (masked infilling for ESM-2). At each level measure (i) PERIODICITY gain\u2014does a repeat form at the intended period and count with high copy-consistency (T-REKS/CE-Symm)?, (ii) the LOCAL-CONTENT change (composition shift), and (iii) validity. Quantify the blind spot = fraction of periodicity-steering displacement that lands in the local-content axis rather than the periodicity axis; the SILENT-BROKEN-REPEAT RATE = fraction of 'repetitive-steered', valid sequences that have repeat-like composition but FAIL the exact repeat oracle (no detected period / wrong period / low copy-consistency); and dose-response. Show invisibility: correlate composition/validity/pseudo-perplexity with true periodicity (hypothesis: near-zero\u2014standard checks miss broken periodicity, and composition-matched shuffles score high on content but zero on periodicity). Random-direction and matched-content-feature controls.\n4) HEAD-TO-HEAD CAUSAL TEST: In the SAME pipeline, calibrate that LOCAL-CONTENT features ARE steerable (composition/motif gain per unit steer, specificity), then test whether ANY compact intervention (TopK/Ordered SAE feature, difference-of-means repeat-vs-shuffled direction, activation patching from true repeat proteins, and content-orthogonalized directions) can install a specified period/count with high copy-consistency while HOLDING local composition fixed\u2014the operational definition of a genuine periodicity handle. Report sign, dose-response, specificity; contrast periodicity vs content steerability effect sizes. Additionally test SET-POINT control: request specific periods (e.g., unit length 20, 30, 40) and copy counts, measuring hit-rate on the exact target vs merely 'more repetitive'. Test whether Ordered/Matryoshka SAEs recover partial periodicity steerability that TopK lacks.\n5) INTERPRETATION: Test whether located 'periodicity' units encode a genuine copy-consistency concept (invariant to which residues fill the unit, tracking period/consistency) vs shallow local-composition proxies, using controlled pairs that dissociate periodicity from composition (same composition, repeat vs shuffled; different compositions, same period). Auto-label top features via max-activating contexts (do they fire at unit boundaries / on high-identity duplications?).\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal PERIODICITY-CONSISTENCY score (periodicity-probe confidence / feature coherence read from the steered activation). Measure whether it flags silently-broken repeats (content-OK, periodicity-broken) better than output-side signals (composition, validity, model likelihood): report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors, and compute saved by flagging non-periodic designs before running CE-Symm/ESMFold structural validation. Emit human-readable diagnoses ('repeat-like composition present but no consistent period; copy-consistency 41%').\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two layers, and short vs long unit lengths / low vs high copy counts; report where the blind spot and (non-)steerability findings are stable vs artifact, and whether periodicity steerability degrades with longer periods and higher copy counts (longer-range copy-consistency being harder).\nEVALUATION METRICS: periodicity-blind-spot magnitude & dose-response and SILENT-BROKEN-REPEAT RATE (primary phenomenon, via exact repeat-detection oracles); content-vs-periodicity-change correlation and composition-matched-shuffle contrast (blind spot); content vs periodicity probe AUROC/R^2 (Location); periodicity-steering set-point hit-rate/copy-consistency/specificity vs content-steering, head-to-head effect sizes (Causal Intervention); periodicity-feature invariance to residue identity (Interpretation); internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE and structural-validation compute saved (auditing); robustness across models/SAEs/period-count regimes.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Periodicity is measured by well-established repeat-detection tools (T-REKS/HHrepID/CE-Symm), which are near-exact for sequence period/count/copy-consistency and slightly noisier for structural repeats (CE-Symm on predicted structures); we lead with sequence-level oracles for core claims and treat structural repeats as corroborating, reporting oracle sensitivity to thresholds. Claims concern model-internal, oracle-verified periodicity controllability, not wet-lab function\u2014stated explicitly. 2) The periodicity-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (TopK/Ordered SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching from real repeat proteins, content-orthogonalized directions), sweeping strength, requiring the model to natively span a periodicity range, and head-to-head calibrating that LOCAL-CONTENT features ARE steerable in the same pipeline\u2014so a null periodicity result is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable periodicity/copy-consistency handle) is equally publishable and directly enables modular repeat-protein design. 3) Composition and periodicity are partly entangled (repeats bias composition); the partial-correlation feature selection and composition-matched shuffled controls (steps 2,3,5) isolate genuinely composition-independent periodicity signal, and we report where separation is impossible. 4) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, raw-activation probes, and random-direction controls. 5) An internal periodicity probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (content-steerable, periodicity-decodable-but-weakly-steerable) and the audit/compute-saving payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 6) Masked-infilling steering of long-range copy-consistency is nontrivial; we standardize on finished-sequence oracle evaluation and use activation patching from true repeats as an architecture-agnostic cross-check. 7) If no blind spot is found (content steering yields well-formed repeats), that is a valuable positive result establishing that PLM feature-steering can control long-range periodic structure."
}}
