ility of each, contrasting content control against periodicity control; (3) INTERPRET whether located periodicity units encode a genuine copy-consistency concept or shallow local-composition proxies; and (4) AUDIT reliability, testing whether an internal periodicity-consistency signal flags silently-broken repeats better than content or model likelihood. We predict content control succeeds, periodicity control is decodable-but-weakly-steerable, and a cheap internal audit catches broken repeats before costly structural validation.", 'Experiments': "1) MODELS, SAEs, ORACLES: Use ESM-2 (150M/650M) with TopK (and Ordered/Matryoshka, since the antibody-SAE literature shows TopK features can be decodable-but-inert) SAEs on the residual stream at a layer chosen by an intrinsic-dimension heuristic; verify reconstruction. PERIODICITY oracles (exact, cheap): sequence-level repeat detection (T-REKS / HHrepID / XSTREAM) returning detected unit length (PERIOD), number of copies (COUNT), and COPY-CONSISTENCY (mean pairwise identity among detected units); plus a structural repeat check (CE-Symm on ESMFold-predicted structures) for foldable repeat topology. LOCAL-CONTENT oracles: amino-acid composition, motif/tract presence (regex/HMMER), and a shuffled-sequence control that preserves composition but destroys periodicity (isolating content from structure). Validity gates: ESMFold pLDDT + pseudo-perplexity. First confirm the model natively spans a meaningful RANGE of periodicity states (non-repetitive to well-formed repeats) so absence-of-control claims are meaningful; assemble a labeled corpus of repeat vs non-repeat proteins (RepeatsDB) and shuffled controls.\n2) FEATURE BANK & LOCATION: On held-out sequences, probe SAE features/raw activations for (a) LOCAL-CONTENT features (predict composition / motif presence), and (b) candidate PERIODICITY features (predict repeat presence, period, and copy-consistency, controlling for composition via partial correlation and via composition-matched shuffled controls). Report probe AUROC/R^2 for content vs periodicity; identify top candidate directions (SAE feature, difference-of-means between repeat vs shuffled-composition-matched sequences, regression-weight direction); compare against a raw-activation probe. Critically test whether periodicity/copy-consistency is decodable at all, independent of local composition.\n3) CORE PHENOMENON—THE PERIODICITY BLIND SPOT (content-easy/periodicity-hard): For each candidate PERIODICITY feature, sweep steering strength and generate/infill N sequences per level (masked infilling for ESM-2). At each level measure (i) PERIODICITY gain—does a repeat form at the intended period and count with high copy-consistency (T-REKS/CE-Symm)?, (ii) the LOCAL-CONTENT change (composition shift), and (iii) validity. Quantify the blind spot = fraction of periodicity-steering displacement that lands in the local-content axis rather than the periodicity axis; the SILENT-BROKEN-REPEAT RATE = fraction of 'repetitive-steered', valid sequences that have repeat-like composition but FAIL the exact repeat oracle (no detected period / wrong period / low copy-consistency); and dose-response. Show invisibility: correlate composition/validity/pseudo-perplexity with true periodicity (hypothesis: near-zero—standard checks miss broken periodicity, and composition-matched shuffles score high on content but zero on periodicity). Random-direction and matched-content-feature controls.\n4) HEAD-TO-HEAD CAUSAL TEST: In the SAME pipeline, calibrate that LOCAL-CONTENT features ARE steerable (composition/motif gain per unit steer, specificity), then test whether ANY compact intervention (TopK/Ordered SAE feature, difference-of-means repeat-vs-shuffled direction, activation patching from true repeat proteins, and content-orthogonalized directions) can install a specified period/count with high copy-consistency while HOLDING local composition fixed—the operational definition of a genuine periodicity handle. Report sign, dose-response, specificity; contrast periodicity vs content steerability effect sizes. Additionally test SET-POINT control: request specific periods (e.g., unit length 20, 30, 40) and copy counts, measuring hit-rate on the exact target vs merely 'more repetitive'. Test whether Ordered/Matryoshka SAEs recover partial periodicity steerability that TopK lacks.\n5) INTERPRETATION: Test whether located 'periodicity' units encode a genuine copy-consistency concept (invariant to which residues fill the unit, tracking period/consistency) vs shallow local-composition proxies, using controlled pairs that dissociate periodicity from composition (same composition, repeat vs shuffled; different compositions, same period). Auto-label top features via max-activating contexts (do they fire at unit boundaries / on high-identity duplications?).\n6) DECISION AUDITING (payoff): Deploy an intervention-free internal PERIODICITY-CONSISTENCY score (periodicity-probe confidence / feature coherence read from the steered activation). Measure whether it flags silently-broken repeats (content-OK, periodicity-broken) better than output-side signals (composition, validity, model likelihood): report AUROC/ECE of internal vs output-side detectors, and compute saved by flagging non-periodic designs before running CE-Symm/ESMFold structural validation. Emit human-readable diagnoses ('repeat-like composition present but no consistent period; copy-consistency 41%').\n7) ROBUSTNESS: Repeat across two model sizes, TopK vs Ordered SAEs, two layers, and short vs long unit lengths / low vs high copy counts; report where the blind spot and (non-)steerability findings are stable vs artifact, and whether periodicity steerability degrades with longer periods and higher copy counts (longer-range copy-consistency being harder).\nEVALUATION METRICS: periodicity-blind-spot magnitude & dose-response and SILENT-BROKEN-REPEAT RATE (primary phenomenon, via exact repeat-detection oracles); content-vs-periodicity-change correlation and composition-matched-shuffle contrast (blind spot); content vs periodicity probe AUROC/R^2 (Location); periodicity-steering set-point hit-rate/copy-consistency/specificity vs content-steering, head-to-head effect sizes (Causal Intervention); periodicity-feature invariance to residue identity (Interpretation); internal-vs-output-side detection AUROC/ECE and structural-validation compute saved (auditing); robustness across models/SAEs/period-count regimes.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) Periodicity is measured by well-established repeat-detection tools (T-REKS/HHrepID/CE-Symm), which are near-exact for sequence period/count/copy-consistency and slightly noisier for structural repeats (CE-Symm on predicted structures); we lead with sequence-level oracles for core claims and treat structural repeats as corroborating, reporting oracle sensitivity to thresholds. Claims concern model-internal, oracle-verified periodicity controllability, not wet-lab function—stated explicitly. 2) The periodicity-non-steerability claim is partly an ABSENCE claim; we mitigate by trying multiple intervention types (TopK/Ordered SAE features, difference-of-means, activation patching from real repeat proteins, content-orthogonalized directions), sweeping strength, requiring the model to natively span a periodicity range, and head-to-head calibrating that LOCAL-CONTENT features ARE steerable in the same pipeline—so a null periodicity result is meaningful, not a weak-model artifact. A positive result (a genuine steerable periodicity/copy-consistency handle) is equally publishable and directly enables modular repeat-protein design. 3) Composition and periodicity are partly entangled (repeats bias composition); the partial-correlation feature selection and composition-matched shuffled controls (steps 2,3,5) isolate genuinely composition-independent periodicity signal, and we report where separation is impossible. 4) SAE quality/layer choice could confound Location; controlled with TopK and Ordered SAEs, multiple layers, raw-activation probes, and random-direction controls. 5) An internal periodicity probe might not beat a black-box raw-activation probe on raw AUROC; the interpretability diagnosis (content-steerable, periodicity-decodable-but-weakly-steerable) and the audit/compute-saving payoff remain the contributions, reported honestly. 6) Masked-infilling steering of long-range copy-consistency is nontrivial; we standardize on finished-sequence oracle evaluation and use activation patching from true repeats as an architecture-agnostic cross-check. 7) If no blind spot is found (content steering yields well-formed repeats), that is a valuable positive result establishing that PLM feature-steering can control long-range periodic structure.'}
